(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-congressional"]={"plain":{"bills":{"congressional":{"slug":"congressional","title":"The Congressional Integrity Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short Title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the Congressional Integrity Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n1. A Member of Congress who holds assets whose value depends on legislation the Member votes upon, or on the actions of agencies the Member oversees and funds, holds an interest adverse to the constituents the office serves. The appearance of that adversity is itself an injury to public confidence, whether or not any vote is in fact affected.\n\n2. The remedy that reaches the problem without inquiry into any legislative act is structural: a Member holds only assets whose value does not depend on the Member's official acts, discloses what is held, and is answerable for what is concealed. No provision of this Act requires proof of how any Member legislated.\n\n3. The interest served by this Act is the prevention of self-dealing and the appearance of self-dealing. This Act does not depend on any finding that Members achieve abnormal investment returns.\n\n4. **Enforcement must not require an executive officer.** Enforcement of this Act against a Member by an officer removable by the President would place the legislative branch under the supervision of the executive. Enforcement of this Act by an officer of the legislative branch who imposes financial consequences would place the Congress in the position of executing the law. This Act therefore commits internal consequences to an officer of the legislative branch, financial and criminal consequences to the courts and to the Department of Justice, and makes its obligations self-executing where it can.\n\n5. **Enumerated powers.** Congress enacts the obligations of this Act pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 18, as applied to the legislative power vested by Article I, Section 1, Congress having authority to legislate the conditions under which the offices and employments it creates and funds are held. Article I, Section 5, Clause 2, by which each House determines the rules of its proceedings and punishes its Members, is a complementary internal authority and supplies the basis for the internal consequences under \u00a7 4-2. The compensation provisions are enacted pursuant to Article I, Section 6, Clause 1, subject to the Twenty-Seventh Amendment. The tax provisions are enacted pursuant to the Sixteenth Amendment. The application of \u00a7 3-3B and \u00a7 3-3C to an individual who has access to non-public information of the United States in the performance of an arrangement with the United States, and the duties of a venue under \u00a7 3-3B, are enacted pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 3, and as necessary and proper to the performance of the functions of the Government through such arrangements.\n\n6. **No qualification for office is imposed.** Nothing in this Act adds to or subtracts from the qualifications for service in either House fixed by Article I, and no consequence under this Act operates to exclude a Member from a seat to which the Member has been elected.\n\n7. A wager held by an officer or employee, or by an individual who has access to the Government's non-public information in performing an arrangement with it, on the acts, decisions, or outcomes the Government will produce is a stake adverse to the public's, and it injures public confidence whether or not any act is affected. A market in such wagers gives every insider an edge the public cannot hold, and the edge follows access rather than the form of employment, which is why the prohibition of \u00a7 3-3B reaches every officer and employee, every individual with such access, and every venue, wherever organized; wagering regulated by the States on athletic contests and games of chance, where no official holds an insider's edge by reason of office, is left to the States."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"For purposes of this Act:\n\n**(a) \"Covered person\"** means a Member of Congress; a Delegate or Resident Commissioner; an officer of either House; and a congressional employee compensated above the threshold set in the schedule to this Act, which threshold shall be set so as to reach committee staff directors, chiefs of staff, legislative directors, and their equivalents. An officer or employee of the executive branch is not a covered person under this Act and is governed by the Executive Integrity Act. A judge or Justice of the United States is not a covered person under this Act and is governed by the Judiciary Integrity Act.\n\n**(b) \"Eligible asset\"** has the meaning given in \u00a7 2-0.\n\n**(c) \"Middle-Class Growth Rate\"** has the meaning given in the People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 1-1, certified as that Act \u00a7 1-3 provides. Where that Act is not in effect, the compensation provisions of \u00a7 2-3 do not operate, and no other provision of this Act is affected.\n\n**(d) \"The Office\"** means the Office of Congressional Integrity established under \u00a7 4-1.\n\n**(e) \"The Attorney General\"** means the Attorney General of the United States.\n\n**(f) \"Shared household\"** means a dwelling in which the covered person and another person both reside and between whom expenses of the household are shared, without regard to marital status.\n\n**(g) \"Domestic partner\"** means a person who shares a household with the covered person and with whom the covered person maintains a relationship evidenced by any of the following: a joint financial account; a shared dependent; designation as a beneficiary, emergency contact, or attorney-in-fact; a registered domestic partnership under State or local law; or joint ownership or tenancy of the residence. A domestic partner is treated as a spouse for all purposes of this Act.\n\n**(h) \"Family member\"** means a spouse, domestic partner, parent, child, sibling, grandparent, grandchild, or dependent of the covered person, together with the relations enumerated in 5 U.S.C. \u00a7 3110(a)(3).\n\n**(i) \"Prohibited source\"** means a person who has a matter pending before a committee on which the covered person sits or before a program the covered person funds or oversees; who is a registered lobbyist or an agent of a foreign principal; or who seeks legislative or appropriations action from the covered person.\n\n**(j) \"Involuntary acquisition\"** means an asset received during covered service by inheritance, bequest, intestate succession, court judgment or settlement, the vesting of equity compensation earned before covered service, or a bona fide gift from a family member, where the covered person did not solicit or arrange the acquisition. Carried interest and profits interests are not involuntary acquisitions.\n\n**(k) \"Identified by another\"** means, with respect to a holding, the earliest of: written notice to the covered person from the Office or the Attorney General identifying the holding; the filing of an action under \u00a7 4-3 or \u00a7 4-5 identifying the holding; and publication of the holding in a generally circulated publication or in a public record. The Office shall record and publish the date of identification. No other event constitutes identification, and a holding is not identified by an internal note, an unpublished tip, or an inquiry not communicated to the covered person.\n\n**(l) \"Conceal\"** means to fail to disclose a holding required to be disclosed under Section 1, before the holding is identified by another, where the covered person knew of the holding and of the obligation to disclose it, or was aware of facts from which that obligation was apparent and did not inquire. A failure to disclose is not concealment where the covered person did not know of the holding and could not reasonably have known of it; where the covered person reasonably relied on a valuation or report of a custodian or adviser, after having disclosed to that person all facts material to the question; or where the covered person relied on an opinion under \u00a7 4-1(h) obtained on a complete and accurate description of the arrangement. Reliance is not reasonable where the covered person withheld a material fact from the custodian, adviser, or issuing authority, or where the covered person knew or had reason to know the valuation, report, or opinion was incorrect. A person who corrects a failure within the cure period is treated as having disclosed. The Government bears the burden of establishing concealment.\n\n**(m) \"Covered service\"** means the period during which an individual is a covered person under this Act, beginning on the date the individual becomes a covered person and ending on the date the individual ceases to be one. A change from one covered position to another without interruption does not end a period of covered service. **\"Covered position\"** means a position or capacity described in subsection (a)."},{"anchor":"sec-0-4","heading":"\u00a7 0-4. Relationship to Existing Law","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"**(a) Floor, not ceiling.** No provision of this Act reduces, repeals, or supersedes any prohibition or penalty of existing law, including the Ethics in Government Act (5 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 13101 et seq.) and the Stop Trading on Congressional Knowledge Act. Where existing law is stricter, it continues to apply; where this Act is stricter, it governs.\n\n**(b) Rules of each House preserved.** Nothing in this Act limits the authority of either House to adopt rules more restrictive than this Act, to discipline its Members, or to determine the rules of its proceedings.\n\n**(c) Independence from the other Acts.** No provision of this Act depends for its operation or validity on the enactment or continued effect of the People's Prosperity Act, the Executive Integrity Act, the Judiciary Integrity Act, or the Candidate Integrity Act. Where this Act refers to a provision of another Act, the reference is to that provision as a source of definition, and \u00a7 2-0(b) supplies the definition if it is unavailable."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Wealth Reconciliation Statement","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a)** Each covered person shall file a complete, sworn statement of all assets, attributed assets, entities, trusts, beneficial interests, foreign holdings, digital assets, and liabilities, upon entering covered service, annually during covered service, and upon leaving covered service.\n\n**(a-1) Assets with a readily ascertainable market price.** Each asset having a readily ascertainable market price \u2014 including a security, a fund interest, a digital asset, a deposit, and a like holding \u2014 and each liability, valued above $5,000, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment, shall be reported at its fair market value as of the reporting date, stated as a point estimate and not as a range or category. For such an asset, fair market value is that market price. Any single item valued at or below that amount may be reported in aggregate. This standard applies notwithstanding any range-based reporting permitted under the Ethics in Government Act.\n\n**(a-2) Assets without a readily ascertainable market price.** An asset having no readily ascertainable market price \u2014 including an interest in a private business or partnership, real property other than a residence, a work of art, and a collectible or other tangible object held for investment \u2014 shall be reported only if its fair market value is reasonably believed to exceed $100,000, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment. An asset reportable under this subsection shall be valued by independent appraisal upon entering covered service, upon leaving covered service, and upon any material change in the asset or its value; in an intervening year, the covered person may report a good-faith updated estimate stating the date and basis of the most recent appraisal. The Office may require an appraisal in an intervening year on reasonable grounds. No appraisal is required of an asset not reportable under this subsection.\n\n**(a-2-1) Prohibited assets reportable regardless of value.** The threshold in subsection (a-2) applies only to an asset the covered person may lawfully hold under this Act. An asset the covered person is prohibited from holding, or is required to divest or convert, shall be identified in the report regardless of value, together with a good-faith estimate of value and the date of acquisition. No independent appraisal is required of such an asset below the threshold in subsection (a-2), the estimate being sufficient for an asset that must in any event be divested.\n\n**(a-3) Purpose of the differing thresholds.** Subsection (a-2) applies a higher threshold, and requires appraisal only above it, because the cost of valuing an illiquid or tangible asset is borne by the covered person and recurs, while the conflict such an asset presents is ordinarily remote until its value is substantial. Subsection (a-1) reaches the holdings that generate the issuer-specific conflicts with which this Act is principally concerned, and those holdings are valued at no cost. Neither subsection limits the holding prohibitions of Section 4, which apply to an asset whether or not it is reportable.\n\n**(a-4) Appraisal on entry: an existing appraisal suffices.** An appraisal of an asset prepared within the two years preceding entry into covered service satisfies the entry requirement of subsection (a-2), provided it was prepared for a purpose other than compliance with this Act \u2014 including a transaction, a financing, an estate or gift valuation, or a lending requirement. Where no such appraisal exists, the covered person files a good-faith estimate upon entering covered service and the appraisal required by subsection (a-2) within 180 days. This subsection changes when the appraisal is furnished and not which assets must be appraised.\n\n**(b)** Where the net worth of a covered person's household increases in any period by an amount materially greater than the sum of (1) the index return on disclosed assets, (2) the Middle-Class Growth Rate crediting on People's Prosperity Fund holdings, (3) federal salary, (4) documented inheritance or gift, and (5) family support excluded under \u00a7 3-5(f), the covered person shall file a written explanation of the difference.\n\n**(b-1) What net worth means for this purpose.** Household net worth under subsection (b) is computed from the assets, attributed assets, and liabilities reportable under this Section and from no others. The five sources enumerated in subsection (b) are supplemented by any other lawful source the covered person identifies in the explanation. This subsection creates no obligation to report an asset that this Section does not otherwise reach.\n\n**(b-2) What an explanation must contain.** An explanation under subsection (b) that identifies the source, the amount, and the date of each component of the difference satisfies that subsection. An explanation that does not identify those three for a component does not satisfy it as to that component.\n\n**(b-3) Notice before a violation arises.** Where the Office's reconciliation identifies a difference the covered person has not explained, the Office shall notify the covered person, identifying the period and the amount, and the covered person has 60 days from the notice to file an explanation under subsection (b). Subsection (c) operates only upon the expiry of that period without a sufficient explanation. This subsection conditions when subsection (c) operates and does not narrow what it reaches.\n\n**(b-4) The Office performs the reconciliation.** Within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the filing of each statement under subsection (a), other than the first statement of a period of covered service, the Office shall compare the household net worth that statement reports with the household net worth reported in the preceding statement, computing each as subsection (b-1) provides and applying a method it publishes, and shall identify whether the increase between them exceeds the sum of the sources enumerated in subsection (b) by more than the threshold under subsection (c). Where it does, the Office shall give the notice subsection (b-3) requires within the same period. The Office shall publish the method before the first statement to which this subsection applies is filed. The method shall name, for each source enumerated in subsection (b), the published series or the filing under this Act from which that source is computed, and for the index return under paragraph (1) of subsection (b) the series the schedule to this Act sets for each class of asset, which the Office has no authority to vary, and a method that does not, or that uses any other input, does not satisfy this subsection. The sources in paragraphs (4) and (5) of subsection (b) are counted as a filing under this Act states them. An amended statement starts no new period under this subsection and suspends no period under subsection (b-3), and a statement amended before the reconciliation is completed is reconciled as amended. An explanation filed before a notice is an explanation under subsection (b-3), and the covered person need not file again as to a component it identifies as subsection (b-2) requires. Where the period subsection (b-3) sets expires and the covered person has not filed an explanation identifying the source, the amount, and the date of each component of the difference stated in the notice, the Office shall transmit the notice, any explanation filed, and the computation to the Attorney General within 7 days. A transmission under this subsection is not a finding and is not publication. The Office's identification under this subsection has no effect in an action under \u00a7 4-3, in which the court determines for itself whether the increase exceeds the sum and the threshold, and the period subsection (b-3) sets runs notwithstanding any action challenging a notice unless the court in that action finds that the notice misstates the period or the amount and so orders. The Office shall record in the covered person's entry in the database under \u00a7 1-5 the date on which each reconciliation under this subsection was completed, and shall publish nothing else that identifies the covered person to whom a reconciliation relates or states its result; this sentence does not limit the publication under \u00a7 1-5(a) of an explanation the covered person files, or the publication of aggregate information that identifies no covered person. Each duty under this subsection is ministerial and is owed to every person whom its nonperformance injures, including the United States acting by the Attorney General, and the district courts of the United States have jurisdiction of an action in the nature of mandamus by any such person against the Director or, while that office is vacant, the officer performing its functions, to compel its performance, without regard to whether the duty is owed to the plaintiff within the meaning of section 1361 of title 28, United States Code. The court shall order performance upon finding that the duty was not performed within the period this subsection sets.\n\n**(c)** An increase in net worth that is not explained under subsection (b) is a violation of this Act, without regard to the form of any arrangement. A violation under this subsection is enforced civilly only, under \u00a7 4-3, consistent with \u00a7 4-9(a); no criminal liability arises from an unexplained increase. For purposes of subsection (b), an increase is materially greater than the sum of the enumerated sources if it exceeds that sum by more than a threshold set in the schedule to this Act, expressed as the greater of a fixed amount or a percentage of the household's disclosed net worth.\n\n**(d) Certification.** Each report under this Section, whether prepared by the covered person or by the Office under \u00a7 1-7, shall carry the covered person's sworn affirmation that the person: (1) made good-faith efforts to obtain all relevant information across the categories reported; and (2) asked each spouse, domestic partner, and dependent of the person to provide information on each such category. Where a spouse, domestic partner, or dependent declined to provide requested information, the person shall so state, and the report is subject to enhanced reconciliation review. A certification is made to the best of the person's knowledge after reasonable inquiry.\n\n**(e) Constructive knowledge on filing.** The filing of a report under this Section deems the covered person to know, as of the filing date, each attributed asset that a reasonable inquiry under subsection (d) would have identified. The cure period under \u00a7 3-2(f) runs from that date as to any such asset.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Disclosure of Liabilities","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a) The duty, and the benchmark.** Each covered person shall disclose all liabilities, their terms, their counterparties, and any collateral. The Office shall publish benchmark rates and terms for common categories of consumer, mortgage, and commercial credit; an extension of credit on terms at or more favorable to the lender than the applicable published benchmark is conclusively compliant with this Section. Any extension of credit to a covered person on terms not available to a similarly situated private borrower, whether or not collateralized, shall be disgorged in an amount equal to three times the value of the differential. The disgorgement under this Section is a remedy for a prohibited below-market benefit and is computed on the economic differential; where 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 7872 imputes interest on the same loan, the imputed amount is credited against the differential so that the same below-market value is not counted twice.\n\n**(a-1) The Office designates a series; it does not compute one.** The Office discharges the publication duty in subsection (a) by designating, for each category of credit, a rate series published by the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council, or another Federal source, and by publishing the designation, the source, and the date from which it applies. The Office publishes the designation rather than the rates, and computes no rate of its own. A designation under this subsection is ministerial.\n\n**(a-2) Credit no designated series covers.** Where no designated series covers an extension of credit, the applicable benchmark is the rate and terms the lender contemporaneously offered a similarly situated borrower not a covered person, established by the lender's own records. The absence of a designated series is not a ground for treating an extension of credit as compliant."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Custodial Reporting","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a)** Each custodian or brokerage holding an account of a covered person or an attributed asset shall report that account, and each transaction in it, directly to the Office, in structured, machine-readable form.\n\n**(a-1) Identification precedes the duty.** The Office shall furnish each custodian, on a secure channel, the information necessary to determine whether an account is subject to subsection (a). A custodian's duty under subsection (a) attaches only as to an account so identified. No custodian is required to determine whether an account holder is a covered person or whether an asset is attributed to one.\n\n**(a-2) The party who knows, tells.** Each covered person shall identify to the Office each custodian or brokerage holding an account of the person or an attributed asset, upon entering covered service and within 30 days of any change. The duty under this subsection runs to the only party who knows the fact, and is discharged by a single identification per custodian.\n\n**(a-3) No public roster.** Information furnished under subsection (a-1) passes from the Office to the custodian on a secure channel and is not published. This Section requires no list of covered persons, spouses, domestic partners, or dependents to be made public, and authorizes no custodian to disclose information so furnished.\n\n**(b) Auto-population.** From the data reported under subsection (a), the Office shall generate a draft of each report required under \u00a7 1-4 and \u00a7 1-4A and present it to the covered person for confirmation or correction. A covered person satisfies the filing obligation by timely confirming or correcting the draft, and the confirmation carries the certification required under \u00a7 1-1(d).\n\n**(c) Effect on liability.** A covered person is not liable for an error in a draft generated under subsection (b) that arises from data reported by a custodian, provided the person timely confirms or corrects the draft and has not failed to disclose an asset or account. This subsection does not excuse a failure to report an asset or account that has no custodian, which remains the covered person's obligation.\n\n**(d)** This Section applies to every covered person, whether or not the person has elected the public option under \u00a7 1-7.\n\n**(e) An account may not be declined by reason of this Section.** No custodian may decline to open or to maintain an account, close an account, or vary its terms, fees, or availability, by reason of a duty imposed by this Section or by reason of an account holder being a covered person or an attributed person. **No cost of compliance with this Section may be charged to the account or to the account holder.** This subsection binds the custodian's conduct."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3a","heading":"\u00a7 1-3A. Reconciliation of filings against custodial reports","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a) Duty.** The Office shall test annually whether the reports filed under \u00a7 1-4 and \u00a7 1-4A reconcile with the custodial reports received under \u00a7 1-3(a), applying a published method, and shall publish the results.\n\n**(b) The comparison is between two sources the Office already holds.** No new filing, certification, or submission is required of any person by this Section.\n\n**(c) What is published.** The Office shall publish, in aggregate and without identifying any individual, the number of filings tested, the number in which a holding, transaction, or valuation reported by a custodian did not appear in the filing, and the number in which a holding appeared in a filing and not in any custodial report.\n\n**(d) Referral, not adjudication.** Where a discrepancy is material and is not resolved on inquiry within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, the Office shall refer the matter. **The Office determines nothing, imposes nothing, and its referral is not a finding that any provision has been violated.**\n\n**(e) Amendment is not a discrepancy.** A filing amended by the covered person before the test is conducted is tested as amended, and an amendment is not itself evidence of concealment.\n\n**(f) No approval function.** Nothing in this Section authorizes the Office to approve, disapprove, delay, or condition any filing or any holding."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Monthly Filing of Foreign and Digital Holdings","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"In addition to the periodic statements under \u00a7 1-1, each covered person shall file monthly with the Office the value, at the market rate on the filing date, of the person's foreign-currency holdings and digital-asset holdings, valued as separate pools under \u00a7 3-5B."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4a","heading":"\u00a7 1-4A. Real-Time Transaction Reporting","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a)** In addition to the periodic and monthly filings under this Section, a covered person shall report to the Office, within 48 hours, any acquisition, disposition, or exchange of an asset or attributed asset above a de minimis amount set in the schedule to this Act, whether or not the asset is an eligible asset.\n\n**(b)** The Office shall publish each report under this Section within 48 hours of filing, in structured, machine-readable form, in the database under \u00a7 1-5.\n\n**(c)** This Section supplements the monthly filing under \u00a7 1-4; the monthly filing continues to govern the valuation of foreign-currency and digital-asset pools, and this Section governs the reporting of individual transactions.\n\n**(d)** A knowing failure to report under this Section within the required period is subject to \u00a7 4-3, and a willful false report is subject to \u00a7 4-7."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Publication","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a)** The Office shall publish each disclosure filed under this Section within 48 hours of filing, in structured, machine-readable form.\n\n**(a-1) Validation before publication.** Before publishing, the Office shall apply automated checks for apparent error, including internal inconsistency and implausible magnitude, and shall give the filer a brief opportunity, not exceeding the publication period, to correct an apparent error identified by those checks. This subsection does not delay publication beyond the period in subsection (a), does not permit substantive revision, and does not apply to a filing the Office has reason to believe is deliberately false.\n\n**(b) Public database.** The Office shall maintain a public, searchable, machine-readable database with an entry for each covered person, current and former. Each entry shall contain: the person's disclosures published under subsection (a); and a timeline of the person's periods of covered service, showing the beginning and end of each period. The database shall allow the public to search and filter entries by name, position or title, agency or House, and year or period of covered service, and shall be available in bulk, downloadable, machine-readable form.\n\n**(b-1) Common schema.** The database shall follow a common schema, published and maintained jointly with the bodies administering the corresponding databases under the other Acts of this suite, with documented fields and a common bulk format, so that the financial-disclosure record of all three branches may be merged and compared by any person. Where another Act is not in effect, this database follows the schema without regard to it.\n\n**(b-2) Adoption or published divergence.** The Office shall adopt the common schema published under Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 1-5(b-2), or shall publish the respects in which its schema diverges and the reason for each divergence. Where that Act is not in effect, the Office publishes the common schema and each revision of it. No body directs another under this subsection. Divergence is permitted; an unpublished divergence is not, and a database whose divergence is unpublished does not satisfy subsection (b-1).\n\n**(c)** Publication under this Section discloses only filed disclosures and periods of service. It does not disclose any raw account data held by the Office under \u00a7 1-8."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Use of Filings","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a) Regulatory character.** This Section applies uniformly to all covered persons, is regulatory in purpose, and requires records of a kind customarily kept and bearing public aspects. The obligation to file is sustained on that basis, in the manner of a required-records regime, and not on any waiver of a constitutional privilege.\n\n**(b) Limited use immunity.** The compelled contents of a filing under this Section shall not be used as evidence against the filer in a criminal prosecution, other than a prosecution for a false or incomplete filing, for an offense under \u00a7 4-7, or for an offense under \u00a7 1-8(e-1). This immunity extends to the compelled contents and to evidence derived directly from them.\n\n**(c) Published filings.** A disclosure published under \u00a7 1-5 is a public record. Its publication, and the use of the public database by any person, do not by themselves taint an investigation, and information independently obtained from a public source is not derived from a compelled filing for purposes of subsection (b).\n\n**(d) Filter procedure and independent source.** The Office and the Department of Justice shall maintain a filter procedure by which personnel conducting an investigation or prosecution of a covered person are walled from the raw compelled contents of that person's filings, other than personnel assigned to a prosecution described in subsection (b). The investigating or prosecuting authority shall maintain a contemporaneous record of the independent source of each item of evidence, created before exposure to any compelled filing.\n\n**(e) Fallback.** If a court holds that the filing obligation compels testimony within the privilege against self-incrimination and that the regulatory grounding of subsection (a) does not sustain it, the immunity granted by subsection (b) is expanded, as to that filer, to full use and derivative-use immunity coextensive with the privilege, and the obligation to file continues.\n\n**(f) Construction.** Nothing in this Section confers immunity for conduct, for the underlying transactions disclosed, or for evidence obtained from a custodian, a counterparty, a co-participant, or any other independent source."},{"anchor":"sec-1-7","heading":"\u00a7 1-7. The Public Option for Managed Compliance","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a) Election.** A covered person may elect to grant the Office read-only access to all of the person's financial accounts and attributed accounts. Upon such an election, the Office shall prepare and file all reports required of the person under this Act, at government expense.\n\n**(a-1) How access is provided.** Access under subsection (a) may be provided through a custodian's reporting under \u00a7 1-3 or through a standard read-only interface furnished by the custodian. No covered person is required to surrender the credentials to any account, and no election is conditioned on doing so.\n\n**(b) Safe harbor.** A covered person who has elected under subsection (a) is not liable for any error in a report prepared by the Office, including an error of valuation, classification, or omission of data within an account to which access was granted.\n\n**(c) Duty to make assets visible.** The safe harbor under subsection (b) does not apply to any asset the covered person failed to make visible to the Office, whether by failing to grant access to an account or by failing to disclose an asset that has no accessible account. Failure to make an asset visible is subject to escalating civil penalties under \u00a7 4-3 after written notice, and, if willful, to prosecution under \u00a7 4-7.\n\n**(d) Revocation.** An election under subsection (a) is revocable prospectively. Revocation does not affect liability for a period during which the election was in effect."},{"anchor":"sec-1-8","heading":"\u00a7 1-8. Confidentiality and Retention of Public-Option Data","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a) Confidentiality.** Data accessed by the Office under \u00a7 1-7 is confidential. No officer, employee, or contractor of the Office shall disclose such data except as required to prepare the filer's reports, to conduct reconciliation under \u00a7 1-1, or to enforce this Act.\n\n**(b) Use limitation.** Such data shall be used only to prepare the filer's reports and to enforce this Act. It shall not be shared with any other agency, used against the filer in any matter other than enforcement of this Act, or admitted in any proceeding other than enforcement of this Act. Another agency, including the Department of Justice, may obtain such data only by order of a court in a matter arising under this Act.\n\n**(c) Penalties for disclosure.** Unauthorized disclosure of data under this Section is a felony punishable to the same extent as unauthorized disclosure of return information under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 7213, and the injured filer has a civil action to the same extent as under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 7431. An officer, employee, or contractor who discloses such data shall be removed.\n\n**(d) Encryption.** Such data shall be protected at rest and in transit to the confidentiality and encryption standard applied to census data under title 13, United States Code.\n\n**(d-1) The standard is verified by someone other than the Office.** The Office shall obtain an independent assessment, not less than annually, of its compliance with subsection (d) and with the use and retention limits of this Section, and shall publish the fact that the assessment was obtained, the identity of the assessor, and the fact of any material finding that remains unremediated. The Office shall not publish the finding itself, the vulnerability it describes, or any detail by which it could be exploited.\n\n**(e) Retention, return, and verification.** The Office shall not hold raw account data under this Section longer than necessary to prepare and file the filer's reports and to complete any reconciliation then pending. Upon completion, the Office shall return the raw data to the filer and delete its own copy, retaining only a cryptographic hash of the data as filed. The hash does not reveal the contents of the data and is retained to verify, in any later proceeding, that data produced by the filer is the genuine, unaltered data as filed.\n\n**(e-1) Preservation duty.** A filer to whom data is returned under subsection (e), and any custodian holding the underlying accounts, shall preserve that data for the period during which a proceeding under this Act could be brought, and shall produce it on lawful demand under subsection (e-2). Destruction, alteration, or failure to preserve data required to be preserved under this subsection is a violation of this Act, and willful destruction to impair an investigation is subject to \u00a7 4-7.\n\n**(e-2) Access for investigation.** In a predicated investigation under this Act \u2014 meaning an investigation opened on a written determination, made by the Office or the Department of Justice and retained in the record, of specific and articulable facts giving reason to believe a violation of this Act has occurred \u2014 the Office or the Department of Justice may obtain returned data from the filer or a custodian by subpoena or court order. Data so obtained shall be verified against the hash retained under subsection (e); a discrepancy is evidence of alteration. Data obtained under this subsection remains subject to the confidentiality, use-limitation, and encryption obligations of this Section.\n\n**(e-3) The concentration is published.** The Office shall publish annually the number of filers whose raw account data it then holds and the median period for which such data has been held, without identifying any filer.\n\n**(f) Data held by the Office at any time.** While the Office holds raw data under \u00a7 1-7, all obligations of this Section apply, and the obligations bind successors in office. Because the Office does not retain raw data long-term under subsection (e), no permanent government archive of raw account data is created by this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-1-8a","heading":"\u00a7 1-8A. Death, Incapacity, and Departure","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"**(a) Death.** Upon the death of a covered person, no further filing is required of the person, and no penalty accrues against the person or the person's estate for a filing not made after the date of death. The Office shall close the person's file and publish the fact and date of closure.\n\n**(b) Incapacity.** Where a covered person is unable by reason of physical or mental incapacity to make a filing, the obligation is suspended for the duration of the incapacity upon certification by a physician or by a person holding the covered person's power of attorney. A representative may file on the covered person's behalf, and a filing so made carries the representative's certification under \u00a7 1-1(d) to the best of the representative's knowledge.\n\n**(c) Departure from service.** A person who leaves covered service files the departure statement required by \u00a7 1-1(a) and thereafter files only as \u00a7 3-7 requires. A divestiture obligation outstanding at departure is governed by \u00a7 6-3(e).\n\n**(d) Estate.** A divestiture or conversion obligation does not survive against the estate of a deceased covered person. Liability for a violation committed during life survives to the extent provided by generally applicable law, and the personal-liability rule of \u00a7 4-3(f) does not require payment by an estate of a penalty not reduced to judgment before death."},{"anchor":"sec-1-9","heading":"\u00a7 1-9. Filer Access","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE","body":"A covered person may inspect the data the Office holds concerning the person under \u00a7 1-7 and \u00a7 1-8, and the log of access to that data."},{"anchor":"sec-2-0","heading":"\u00a7 2-0. Eligible Assets","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 ELIGIBLE ASSETS, ALLOCATION, AND COMPENSATION","body":"**(a)** \"Eligible asset\" means: an interest in the People's Prosperity Fund established under the People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 2-1; an interest in a diversified lifecycle fund offered under the People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4; cash and deposits as provided in \u00a7 3-5A; a primary or secondary residence; use property as provided in \u00a7 3-5; an interest in a defined-benefit plan; and any asset excluded under \u00a7 3-5.\n\n**(b)** Where the People's Prosperity Act is not in effect, or the Fund or the lifecycle funds are unavailable, \"eligible asset\" means cash, obligations of the United States, and a diversified index fund or exchange-traded fund tracking a published broad-market index and applying no screen, together with the assets excluded under \u00a7 3-5.\n\n**(c)** An interest in the Fund is subject to the restrictions of the People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 2-3.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 ELIGIBLE ASSETS, ALLOCATION, AND COMPENSATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. The Allocation Election","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 ELIGIBLE ASSETS, ALLOCATION, AND COMPENSATION","body":"**(a)** A covered person shall hold their eligible assets, allocated between the People's Prosperity Fund and the age-adjusted diversified lifecycle fund under the People\u2019s Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4, in any proportion from zero to one hundred percent.\n\n**(b) Candidates.** A candidate for a covered elective office shall state the person's allocation election on the statement of candidacy filed with the Federal Election Commission. The election is binding upon assuming office.\n\n**(c) Appointees and persons assuming office otherwise than by election.** For a person who assumes a covered position otherwise than by election, including by appointment or by filling of a vacancy, the allocation defaults to one hundred percent to the diversified lifecycle fund. Such a person may elect a different allocation by filing with the Office within 90 days of assuming office. An election under this subsection is voluntary.\n\n**(d) Lock.** An allocation, whether elected or defaulted, is fixed until the next congressional term boundary and may not be changed during that period. It governs all eligible assets held and all eligible assets acquired during the period. It may be changed only at a congressional term boundary or upon re-entry into a covered position.\n\n**(e) Publication.** The Office shall publish each person's allocation, including a default allocation, under \u00a7 1-5.\n\n**(f)** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of either House to adopt rules more restrictive than this Section with respect to its Members and employees."},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Retirement Accounts","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 ELIGIBLE ASSETS, ALLOCATION, AND COMPENSATION","body":"**(a)** The tax-advantaged retirement holdings of a covered person, including holdings in the Thrift Savings Plan, an individual retirement account, and any account rolled over from prior employment, are subject to the same eligible-asset and allocation rules as the person's other holdings. Such holdings shall be invested only in the People's Prosperity Fund and the diversified lifecycle fund under the People\u2019s Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4, in the proportion of the person's allocation election under \u00a7 2-1, within their existing tax-advantaged form.\n\n**(b)** Except as \u00a7 4-8(a) provides upon conviction, this Act does not eliminate, replace, or reduce any federal retirement benefit. A covered person continues to participate in the Federal Employees Retirement System, the Thrift Savings Plan, and any other applicable retirement program on the same terms as before, except that the investment of covered retirement holdings is confined to the eligible assets under subsection (a) for the duration of covered service."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Prosperity-Pegged Compensation","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 ELIGIBLE ASSETS, ALLOCATION, AND COMPENSATION","body":"**(a) Purpose.** The federal salary of a covered person in a pegged position is adjusted by the Middle-Class Growth Rate, so that the compensation of those who govern rises by the same percentage by which the earnings of the middle of the distribution rise, and falls in real terms in the years in which those earnings fall in real terms.\n\n**(b) The true salary.** For each pegged position, the Bureau of Labor Statistics shall determine a true salary. Within the period provided by subsection (b-5), the Bureau shall reconstruct, using the methodology fixed under the People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 1-2, the salary the position would carry today had its compensation been adjusted by the Middle-Class Growth Rate in each year since the most recent statutory adjustment of compensation applicable to that position. That reconstructed amount is the initial true salary. In each subsequent year, the true salary is adjusted from the prior year by the Middle-Class Growth Rate for that year. The Bureau's determination is computational; no covered person or House may alter it.\n\n**(b-1) Phase-in of the initial adjustment.** Where the initial true salary determined under subsection (b) exceeds the salary in effect for a pegged position immediately before this Section takes effect by more than the amount stated in the schedule, the difference is phased in in equal installments across the number of intervening elections stated in the schedule, beginning with the first Congress to which subsection (c) applies. This subsection applies only to the initial transition to the true salary and does not affect the operation of subsection (b) in any subsequent period, and does not alter the timing rule of subsection (e) or \u00a7 6-1(b).\n\n**(b-4) How far back the reconstruction runs.** The reconstruction under subsection (b) runs from the earliest year for which the Bureau can compute the Middle-Class Growth Rate from published series, and the Bureau shall publish that year and the series used. For a year earlier than that, the position is carried at the statutory adjustment then in effect. The Bureau is not required to compute the Middle-Class Growth Rate for a year in which the series necessary to compute it were not published.\n\n**(b-5) The deadline.** The reconstruction shall be completed before the beginning of the second Congress following enactment.\n\n**(b-6) The computation is published.** The Bureau shall publish, for each pegged position, the computation of the reconstructed amount and the series used. The methodology lock of People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 1-2 applies to the reconstruction as it applies to the Middle-Class Growth Rate itself.\n\n**(c) Pegged positions.** A Member of Congress, the President, the Vice President, the head of an executive department listed in 5 U.S.C. \u00a7 101, and a person holding a Senate-confirmed position at Executive Schedule Level I or II is compensated at the true salary.\n\n**(c-1) Floor against nominal decline only.** The salary of a pegged position shall not be reduced below its nominal amount for the preceding period. Where the Middle-Class Growth Rate is negative, the salary is unchanged in nominal terms rather than reduced.\n\n**(c-2) No inflation floor.** The salary of a pegged position is not adjusted for inflation and is not maintained at any inflation-adjusted amount. Where the Middle-Class Growth Rate is less than the rate of inflation, the salary rises by the Middle-Class Growth Rate and falls in real terms.\n\n**(d) All other covered persons.** A covered person not described in subsection (c) is not subject to this Section, and the person's compensation is set as under existing law. This Act's conflict, disclosure, and holding rules apply to such a person without regard to this Section.\n\n**(e) Timing.** An adjustment under this Section takes effect only for a Congress beginning, or a presidential or vice-presidential term beginning, after the determination, and no salary is diminished during a term or a Congress already begun.\n\n**(f) No discretionary override.** No cost-of-living or other adjustment mechanism applicable to a pegged position operates in addition to this Section. The true salary, or the nominal floor under subsection (c-1) where it applies, is the compensation of the position.\n\n**(g) No suspension in a year of decline.** No officer of the United States may suspend, waive, defer, or decline to apply this Section in any year, and no such action is effective. A statute suspending or modifying the application of this Section in a year in which the Middle-Class Growth Rate is negative or below the rate of inflation requires a two-thirds vote of each House and a recorded roll-call vote."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. The Obligation to Elect","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Compensation.** A covered person shall not receive compensation from any source other than the United States Government, whether directly or through any structure, arrangement, entity, trust, nominee, or intermediary, and regardless of form, title, or characterization. The receipt of value from any non-Federal source, in cash or any other form, is prohibited under this subsection regardless of form.\n\n**(b) Non-eligible assets: the obligation to elect.** With respect to each asset other than an eligible asset that a covered person holds, acquires, or from which the covered person derives economic benefit, whether directly or through any structure, arrangement, entity, trust, nominee, or intermediary, the covered person shall do one of the following:\n\n1. **Divest or convert** the asset within the period allowed by \u00a7 6-2;\n\n2. **Place the asset under an instrument** meeting \u00a7 4-10; or\n\n3. **Disclose and retain**: disclose the asset in full under Section 1, recuse from every matter that could affect its value as \u00a7 3-1A requires, and pay the tax imposed by \u00a7 4-3A.\n\nEach of the three is a lawful course. A covered person violates this Section only by taking none of them with respect to an asset.\n\n**(c) When the election under subsection (b)(3) is unavailable.** The election to disclose and retain is not available, and the covered person shall proceed under subsection (b)(1) or (b)(2), where the asset is:\n\n1. an interest in a prohibited source;\n\n2. an interest in an entity that is regulated by, holds a contract or grant with, or seeks a license, permit, or approval from the covered person's agency, or that is within the jurisdiction of a committee on which the covered person sits;\n\n3. an interest in an entity whose value depends in substantial part on action the covered person is authorized to take or to influence; or\n\n4. a private-company or other non-publicly-traded investment interest, except as \u00a7 3-5 excludes it, or an economic beneficial interest in a trust holding non-eligible assets where the covered person or any person in the covered person's shared household is an economic beneficiary.\n\nThese exclusions are categorical. No officer determines their application, and no waiver of them is available.\n\n**(d) Acquisition.** \"Acquire\" includes acquisition by purchase, issuance, gift, grant, award, barter, settlement, or compensation. Gifts are governed by \u00a7 3-5C and involuntary acquisitions by \u00a7 3-5D.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-1a","heading":"\u00a7 3-1A. Recusal Under an Election to Disclose and Retain","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person who elects under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) shall recuse from any particular matter, rulemaking, adjudication, contract, vote, investigation, appropriation, nomination, or other official action that could reasonably be expected to affect the value of the retained asset, or the value of a class of assets of which it is a part.\n\n**(b)** Each recusal under this Section shall be recorded in writing, filed with the Office, and published in the database under \u00a7 1-5, identifying the matter and the asset giving rise to it.\n\n**(c)** Where recusal under this Section would prevent the covered person from performing a substantial portion of the duties of the office, the covered person shall proceed under \u00a7 3-1(b)(1) or (b)(2). A person who cannot do the work of the office while retaining an asset has not made a lawful election.\n\n**(d)** The Office shall publish annually, for each covered person and in the aggregate, the number of recusals under this Section and the matters recused from, by category.\n\n**(e)** A failure to file a recusal required by subsection (b), or the filing of a recusal that is false, is a violation of \u00a7 3-1 and terminates the election, and the covered person shall divest or convert the asset within 90 days.\n\n**(f) How this Section is enforced.** Liability under this Act with respect to this Section arises from the failure to file a recusal required by subsection (b), or from the falsity of a filed recusal, each of which is established without reference to any legislative act. Nothing in this Act subjects a vote, a committee deliberation, or any other legislative act to inquiry. The obligation to recuse under subsection (a) is enforceable by the House of which the covered person is a Member, under the rules of that House."},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Attribution","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Automatic attribution.** An asset held by the spouse or a dependent child of a covered person is attributed to the covered person.\n\n**(b) Flow-based attribution.** An asset held by any other person or entity is attributed to a covered person if any of the following applies:\n\n1. The covered person or the covered person's spouse funded, gifted, transferred, guaranteed, or lent against the asset, at any time during covered service or within the ten years preceding covered service, or in contemplation of covered service.\n\n2. The covered person or the covered person's spouse settled the trust or entity holding the asset, or holds an interest, reversion, power of appointment, power of substitution, or the practical ability to appoint or remove a trustee, including through a trust protector or other intermediary.\n\n3. The covered person or the covered person's spouse derives economic benefit from the asset, including distribution, use, security for borrowing, or benefit upon death.\n\n4. The covered person or the covered person's spouse exercises control or direction over the investment of the asset.\n\n5. The asset is treated as held by the covered person under \u00a7 3-3.\n\n**(c)** A transfer described in subsection (b)(1) made within ten years preceding covered service is rebuttably presumed to have been made in contemplation of covered service.\n\n**(d) Look-through.** The assets of an entity attributed to a covered person under this Section are attributed to that person in proportion to the person's interest in the entity.\n\n**(e) Former spouses and continued household.** Where a covered person divorces during covered service or within two years before entering covered service, and continues to maintain a shared household with the former spouse, the assets of the former spouse are rebuttably presumed to remain attributed to the covered person. The covered person may rebut this presumption by a preponderance of the evidence showing a genuine, arm's-length separation of domestic and financial lives. A property settlement is not, by itself, sufficient to rebut the presumption.\n\n**(f) Knowledge and cure.** A covered person is not liable under this Act for an attributed asset acquired or arising without the person's action \u2014 including an asset acquired by another person, received passively, distributed in kind, or brought into a restricted category by a change in market value \u2014 until the person knew or reasonably should have known of it. Upon knowing or having reason to know, the person shall disclose the asset and divest or convert it to an eligible asset within 90 days. Timely cure discharges liability. This subsection does not protect a person who arranged not to know, and constructive knowledge attaches on filing under \u00a7 1-1(e).\n\n**(g) Newly attributed persons.** Where a person becomes an attributed person during covered service, including by marriage, by becoming a domestic partner, or by becoming a dependent, the pre-existing assets of that person are subject to divestiture under \u00a7 6-3, measured from the date attribution begins, rather than to immediate liability.\n\n**(h) Co-owned assets.** Where an attributed asset is co-owned with a person whose assets are not attributed, and the covered person cannot unilaterally divest it, the covered person shall make good-faith efforts to divest and shall report those efforts to the Office every six months. The covered person shall not receive economic benefit from the asset beyond its fair market value as of the date the applicable divestiture period under \u00a7 6-3 expired; any benefit above that amount shall be disgorged. Genuine inability to divest, documented under this subsection, is not a violation."},{"anchor":"sec-3-2a","heading":"\u00a7 3-2A. Classes of Connection; Prohibition and Disclosure","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Purpose.** This Section calibrates the reach of the economic prohibitions of this Act by the closeness of the connection between the covered person and another person, and provides that where prohibition does not apply, disclosure applies in its place. Each element of this Section is separately severable under subsection (e).\n\n**(b) Class 1 \u2014 the covered person and the unified household.** The economic prohibitions of \u00a7 3-1 and the holding and divestiture rules of this Act apply in full to the assets of the covered person, the covered person's spouse or domestic partner, the covered person's dependent children, and any asset attributed under \u00a7 3-2 by reason of funding, control, or economic benefit by the covered person or the covered person's spouse or domestic partner. A partner who shares the covered person's household is a domestic partner under \u00a7 0-3(g) and is within this Class.\n\n**(c) Class 2 \u2014 independent adult relatives.** For a parent, sibling, or adult, financially-independent child of the covered person who is not within Class 1, the economic prohibitions of \u00a7 3-1 do not apply by reason of the relationship alone. Instead, the covered person shall disclose the relative's assets and financial dealings to the extent known or reasonably obtainable, which are then subject to the wealth reconciliation of \u00a7 1-1 and the anti-evasion rule of \u00a7 3-3. The relationship alone shall not compel the relative to divest, restructure, or alter the conduct of the relative's economic life.\n\n**(c-1) Ask, and record the answer.** The covered person shall ask each Class 2 relative to provide information within the categories disclosable under subsection (c), and shall record and report the request and the response. Where a relative declines or does not respond, the covered person shall so state. A statement that information was not obtainable, without a record of the request and the response, does not satisfy subsection (c).\n\n**(c-2) A declination is surfaced, not buried.** A declination reported under subsection (c-1) shall be reported to the Office and included in the reconciliation under \u00a7 1-1(b-4). A relative who declines is compelled to nothing; the fact of the declination is what enters the record.\n\n**(d) Anti-evasion backstop.** Nothing in subsection (c) permits use of a person as a conduit. Where a covered person funds, controls, directs the investment of, or derives economic benefit from a Class 2 person's asset, that asset is attributed under \u00a7 3-2 and subject to the full prohibitions of Class 1, and concealment of such an arrangement is subject to \u00a7 4-7. The relaxation of prohibition for Class 2 reaches only the person's own independent economic life, never value routed through them.\n\n**(e) Severability by class and to disclosure.** Each of subsections (b) and (c) is severable from the other, and within Class 2 the prohibition and the disclosure obligation are severable from each other. If the application of the economic prohibitions to any class is held invalid, the prohibitions remain in force as to every other class, and the disclosure obligation of subsection (c) applies to the class as to which prohibition is held invalid. If a disclosure obligation is held invalid as to any class, the remaining obligations and prohibitions are not affected."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Anti-Evasion","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** Any arrangement a principal purpose of which is to obtain economic exposure to a non-eligible asset, or to a specific issuer, shall be treated as the holding of that asset by the covered person.\n\n**(b)** An arrangement a principal purpose of which is to hold value through a person in a marriage-like relationship with the covered person while avoiding the indicia of a domestic partner under \u00a7 0-3(g) shall be treated as attribution of that value to the covered person.\n\n**(c)** The form, title, characterization, independence of any trustee, and presence of any intermediary are not determinative under this Section. The characterization of a relationship, and the maintenance of separate residences or finances arranged to avoid attribution, are likewise not determinative. The covered person bears the burden of establishing compliance where an arrangement is ambiguous.\n\n**(d)** No criminal liability shall be predicated on this Section. A violation of this Section is subject only to the civil consequences provided in \u00a7 4-3. This subsection does not limit prosecution under \u00a7 4-7 for concealment or misrepresentation."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3a","heading":"\u00a7 3-3A. Trading on Non-Public Government Information","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** A covered person shall not acquire, hold, or dispose of any financial instrument the value of which depends, in whole or in part, upon the occurrence, non-occurrence, timing, or outcome of an event about which the covered person holds material non-public information obtained in the course of official duties or by reason of the person's position.\n\n**(b) Financial instrument defined.** For purposes of this Section, \"financial instrument\" includes a security, commodity interest, futures or forward contract, option, swap, other derivative, **event contract, prediction-market position, wager, and any other contract, instrument, or arrangement the value of which depends upon the occurrence or non-occurrence of an event** \u2014 whether or not it is regulated as a security or commodity interest, and whether or not the venue on which it is transacted is organized within the United States.\n\n**(c) Attribution.** This Section applies to an instrument held by, or for the benefit of, any person whose holdings are attributed to the covered person under \u00a7 3-2 or \u00a7 3-2A, and to an instrument acquired through any arrangement described in \u00a7 3-3.\n\n**(d) Eligible assets unaffected.** This Section does not apply to a holding in an eligible asset under \u00a7 2-0, or to a diversified fund that neither the covered person nor any person acting at the covered person's direction selected by reference to the event in question.\n\n**(e) Remedy.** A covered person who violates this Section is liable to the United States for the amount of any gain realized or loss avoided, and an additional amount equal to that sum, recoverable in a civil action. Liability under this subsection requires knowledge that the information was non-public and was obtained in the course of official duties or by reason of the person's position, and requires no further showing of intent.\n\n**(f) Matching and publication.** The Office shall compare positions reported under \u00a7 1-4A against records of transactions on any venue on which instruments described in subsection (b) are transacted, to the extent such records are available to it, and shall publish the fact and disposition of each match. The absence of matching in a period does not excuse a violation.\n\n**(g) Relation to other law.** This Section is in addition to, and not in lieu of, any other prohibition or remedy, including any provision of the securities or commodities laws, and does not require that the conduct constitute fraud or a breach of any duty of confidentiality."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3b","heading":"\u00a7 3-3B. Prediction-Market Positions and Event Wagers","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** A covered individual shall not enter into, acquire, hold, or maintain a covered event position, whether directly or through any structure, arrangement, entity, trust, nominee, or intermediary, and regardless of form, title, or characterization. The prohibition of this subsection does not depend on the covered individual holding any non-public information, and applies whatever the event on which the position turns.\n\n**(b) Covered individual defined.** For purposes of this Section, \"covered individual\" means:\n\n1. a Member of Congress; a Delegate or Resident Commissioner; an officer or employee of either House of Congress; and each officer and employee of an office, agency, or instrumentality of the legislative branch of the United States, including each covered person; and\n\n2. each individual who has access to non-public information of the United States in the performance of an arrangement with either House of Congress or with an office, agency, or instrumentality of the legislative branch, within the meaning of subsection (b-2), during the period of that access.\n\n**(b-2) Access to non-public information under an arrangement.** For purposes of subsection (b)(2), of \u00a7 3-3C, and of the certification under subsection (j):\n\n1. \"Arrangement with the United States\" means a contract, a subcontract at any tier, a grant, a cooperative agreement, an other-transaction agreement, an intergovernmental or interagency agreement, a detail, a fellowship, or any other arrangement, however denominated, under which an individual performs services or other work for, on behalf of, or with funds provided by the United States or an agency, office, court, or instrumentality of the United States, whether the individual performs them as a party to the arrangement or as an owner, officer, employee, member, partner, or agent of a party. The forms enumerated in this paragraph are illustrative, and an arrangement of any other form under which services are so performed is an arrangement with the United States regardless of its title or characterization.\n\n2. \"Non-public information of the United States\" means information held by the United States or by an agency, office, court, or instrumentality of the United States that has not been made generally available to the public, and includes classified information, information the disclosure of which a statute or regulation restricts, information held in a non-public information system, and information concerning a pending or contemplated action, decision, procurement, investigation, adjudication, or proceeding.\n\n3. An individual has access to non-public information of the United States during any period in which such information is made available to the individual in the performance of an arrangement with the United States. An individual has such access, without regard to any other fact, during any period in which the individual holds, by reason of the arrangement, a security clearance issued by the United States or an account on a non-public information system of the United States.\n\n4. An individual whose performance of an arrangement consists only of the supply of goods, or of services or other work performed without access to non-public information of the United States, is not a covered individual under subsection (b)(2). A party to an arrangement is not a covered individual by reason of an individual's access, and this Section imposes no duty on a party to an arrangement other than the duties stated in subsection (k-1). The exclusions of this paragraph are those and only those stated in it.\n\n5. Subsection (b)(2) applies to an individual without regard to the individual's nationality or to the place at which the arrangement is performed, and this Section applies to a covered event position entered into by such an individual wherever the individual is located. The application of this Section to an individual described in subsection (b)(2) is severable from its application to every other covered individual, and the application of \u00a7 3-3C to such an individual is severable from every other application of that Section.\n\n**(c) Covered event position defined.** For purposes of this Section, \"covered event position\" means an event contract, a prediction-market position, a binary option on the occurrence of an event, a wager, and any other contract, position, or arrangement the value or payout of which depends upon the occurrence, non-occurrence, timing, or outcome of an event, entered into on or through a market, exchange, platform, or other venue that offers such positions to the public or to any class of persons, whether access is open or conditioned upon membership, invitation, registration, or qualification \u2014 whether or not the position is regulated as a security or commodity interest, and whether or not the venue is organized or operated within the United States.\n\n**(d) What is not a covered event position.** A covered event position does not include:\n\n1. an instrument the value of which depends upon the price or level of a security, commodity, currency, rate of interest, or published index of prices or values, and not upon the occurrence, non-occurrence, timing, or outcome of a discrete specified event;\n\n2. a contract of insurance, an annuity, or a warranty or guarantee incidental to a sale of goods or services;\n\n3. a wager on an athletic contest or a game of chance, placed under a license issued under the law of a State or of an Indian tribe, or with a lottery conducted under the law of a State; or\n\n4. a position entered into in the ordinary course of a bona fide trade or business, to hedge or manage a commercial risk arising from the operations of that business, and not for speculation; this paragraph does not extend to a position on an election, on the action or inaction of a government or an officer of a government, or on litigation.\n\nThe exclusions of this subsection are those and only those stated in it, and the omission of any other exclusion is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(e) No election to retain; no instrument.** As to a covered person, a covered event position is not an asset with respect to which an election under \u00a7 3-1(b)(2) or \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) is available, and \u00a7 4-10 does not apply to it. The sole lawful course with respect to a covered event position is closure or divestiture under subsection (f).\n\n**(f) Onset and closure.** This Section applies to a covered individual who is not a covered person beginning one year after the date of enactment of this Act, and otherwise as \u00a7 6-1 provides. A covered event position held on the date this Section first applies to a covered individual, or held on the date a person first becomes a covered individual, shall be closed or divested within 90 days of that date. A position arising without the covered individual's action is governed by the knowledge-and-cure rule of \u00a7 3-2(f), applied as if the position were an attributed asset.\n\n**(g) A position held or placed by another.** A covered event position entered into by another person is the covered individual's for purposes of this Section where the covered individual directed, requested, or arranged it, furnished the funds staked upon it, or is entitled to share in its proceeds. As to a covered person, this Section also applies to a position held by, or for the benefit of, any person whose holdings are attributed to the covered person under \u00a7 3-2 or \u00a7 3-2A, and to a position acquired through any arrangement described in \u00a7 3-3.\n\n**(h) Void.** A covered event position entered into in violation of this Section, or accepted by a venue in violation of subsection (j), is void. A venue or counterparty acquires no right upon it against any person, and is liable to the United States for the amount staked upon or paid to acquire the position, less any amount actually paid out upon it, recoverable in a civil action; no part of an amount so recovered is payable to the covered individual. An amount recovered from the venue under the corresponding provision of another Act of this suite is credited against liability under this subsection.\n\n**(i) Remedy against the covered individual.** A covered individual who violates this Section is liable to the United States for the amount staked upon or paid to acquire the position and any gain realized, and an additional amount equal to that sum, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General. Liability under this subsection requires no showing of intent and no showing that the covered individual held non-public information. As to an individual described in subsection (b)(2) who, when the position was entered into, held neither a clearance nor an account described in subsection (b-2)(3), liability under this subsection requires that the individual had received the notice subsection (k-1) requires, or otherwise knew of the access, before the position was entered into; subsection (h) applies to the position without regard to this sentence.\n\n**(j) Certification.** A venue that offers covered event positions to the public, and that is organized under the laws of, or transacts business in, the United States, shall obtain from each account holder, upon the opening of an account and annually thereafter, a written certification stating whether the holder serves as an officer or employee of the United States in any branch, including as a Member of Congress, a judge or judicial officer, or a member of the uniformed services, or has access to non-public information of the United States in the performance of an arrangement with the United States within the meaning of subsection (b-2), and shall not knowingly accept a covered event position from a person who so serves or has such access. A venue that has obtained the certifications this subsection requires, and that has no knowledge that a certification is false, is not liable under subsection (h) with respect to a position accepted in reliance upon it. A covered individual shall state the fact of the individual's service or access truthfully in each certification under this subsection, and a false statement in such a certification is a violation of this Section. A certification obtained under the corresponding provision of another Act of this suite satisfies this subsection, and one certification satisfies each such provision.\n\n**(k) Civil enforcement only for individuals who are not covered persons.** As to a covered individual who is not a covered person, this Section is enforced only under subsections (h) through (j), through the disciplinary authority governing the individual's employment, and, as to an individual described in subsection (b)(2), through the arrangement as subsection (k-1) provides, and no criminal liability arises under this Act from a violation of this Section. A violation of this Section is a basis for adverse action under the authority governing the covered individual's employment and, as to an individual described in subsection (b)(2), for removal of the individual from performance of the arrangement and from the access it affords. Nothing in this subsection limits \u00a7 4-7(a)(2) as applied to a covered person who conceals or misrepresents an arrangement to evade this Section.\n\n**(k-1) The arrangement.** The prohibition of subsection (a) is a term of every arrangement with the United States under which an individual described in subsection (b)(2) has access, whether or not the arrangement states it, and neither the omission of the term nor any provision of the arrangement affects its application. Each party to such an arrangement shall give each such individual written notice of this Section before the individual's access begins or, for an individual having access on the date this Section first applies to the individual, within 30 days of that date, and shall retain a record of the notice for the period during which an action under this Section could be brought. A notice that does not identify this Section and state the prohibition of subsection (a) and the closure period of subsection (f) does not satisfy this subsection. A failure to give notice is a breach of the arrangement by the party and excuses no individual. A party to an arrangement is not liable under subsection (h) or subsection (i) by reason of an individual's violation, and an individual's violation is not a breach of the arrangement by the party.\n\n**(l) The venue duty is not a condition of the prohibition.** The failure of a venue to obtain a certification, and the unavailability or invalidity of subsection (j) as to any venue, excuse no covered individual, and the prohibition of subsection (a) and the remedies of subsections (h) and (i) operate without regard to subsection (j).\n\n**(m) Relation to other law.** This Section is in addition to, and not in lieu of, \u00a7 3-3A and every other prohibition or remedy, including the securities and commodities laws and any regulation governing gambling by officers or employees of the United States, and nothing in this Section authorizes a position that any other provision prohibits."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3c","heading":"\u00a7 3-3C. Tipping and Trading on Government Information","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Trading prohibited.** No person shall acquire, hold, or dispose of any financial instrument on the basis of material non-public information that the person:\n\n1. obtained in the course of official duties as an officer or employee of the United States, or by reason of such a position;\n\n2. knows, or is reckless in not knowing, derives directly or indirectly from an officer or employee of the United States who obtained it in the course of official duties or by reason of such a position, or from an individual who obtained it as paragraph (3) describes, where the information was communicated other than in an authorized disclosure; or\n\n3. obtained in the performance of an arrangement with the United States, as an individual having access to non-public information of the United States within the meaning of \u00a7 3-3B(b-2), or by reason of that access.\n\n**(b) Tipping prohibited.** An officer or employee of the United States, and an individual described in subsection (a)(3), shall not communicate material non-public information obtained in the course of official duties, by reason of the position held, or in the performance of an arrangement with the United States or by reason of the access it affords, to any person, where the officer or employee knows or has reason to believe that the person may acquire, hold, or dispose of a financial instrument on the basis of it, unless the communication is an authorized disclosure.\n\n**(c) Definitions.** For purposes of this Section:\n\n1. \"Financial instrument\" has the meaning given in \u00a7 3-3A(b).\n\n2. \"Officer or employee of the United States\" means each officer and employee of the executive, legislative, or judicial branch of the United States, including a Member of Congress, a Delegate or Resident Commissioner, a judge or Justice of the United States, and each person described in \u00a7 3-3B(b)(1).\n\n3. \"Authorized disclosure\" means a communication made in the proper discharge of official duties; a dissemination that makes the information generally available to the public; a disclosure required by law, or made in a judicial, legislative, or administrative proceeding or in a published report, opinion, order, or docket entry; a disclosure to an agency, to an Inspector General, to the Congress or a committee or Member of Congress acting in an official capacity, or to a law-enforcement authority; and a disclosure protected by a whistleblower statute. The enumeration of this paragraph is exclusive, and the omission of any other class of disclosure is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d) No further element.** Liability under this Section does not require that the officer, employee, or individual described in subsection (a)(3) have acted for personal gain or received any benefit; that any communication have breached a fiduciary or other duty; that the person acquiring or disposing have owed a duty to any person; or that the conduct constitute fraud. The elements are those stated in subsections (a) and (b) and no others, and the existence and content of the duties this Section imposes do not depend on the construction given to any other statute or regulation addressing similar conduct. A person who acquires or disposes of a financial instrument while aware of the information acts on the basis of it, unless the acquisition or disposition is made pursuant to a binding contract, instruction, or written plan entered into before the person became aware of it.\n\n**(e) Diversified funds.** This Section does not apply to an acquisition or disposition of a diversified fund that was not selected by reference to the event or matter to which the information relates.\n\n**(f) Remedy.** A person who violates subsection (a) is liable to the United States for the gain realized or loss avoided, and an additional amount equal to that sum, recoverable in a civil action brought by the Attorney General. An officer or employee, or an individual described in subsection (a)(3), who violates subsection (b) is jointly and severally liable for the amounts recoverable under this subsection from each person who acquired or disposed on the basis of the information communicated, and a violation of subsection (b) is a basis for adverse action under the authority governing the officer's or employee's employment and, as to an individual described in subsection (a)(3), for removal from performance of the arrangement and from the access it affords.\n\n**(g) Criminal liability.** A violation of subsection (b) committed willfully and with intent that a person acquire, hold, or dispose of a financial instrument on the basis of the information, or with knowledge that a person will do so, is a felony as \u00a7 4-7(a)(4) provides. A violation of subsection (b) without that intent or knowledge is enforced civilly only, and no criminal liability arises under this Act from a violation of subsection (a).\n\n**(h) Effect and relation to other law.** This Section takes effect on the date of enactment of this Act and applies to communications, acquisitions, and dispositions occurring after that date. It is in addition to, and not in lieu of, \u00a7 3-3A, \u00a7 3-3B, section 4c(a)(3) and (4) of the Commodity Exchange Act (7 U.S.C. \u00a7 6c(a)(3) and (4)), section 2102 of title 41, United States Code, the duties affirmed by subsections (g) and (h) of section 21A of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (15 U.S.C. \u00a7 78u-1), and every other prohibition or remedy, and nothing in this Section limits any of them.\n\n**(i) No proof of legislative acts.** \u00a7 4-6 applies to this Section. Liability rests on the possession of the information, the communication, and the acquisition or disposition, each provable without proof of any legislative act, and nothing in this Section abrogates the privilege of Article I, Section 6, Clause 1."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Treatment of Wrappers","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"The prohibition in \u00a7 3-1 applies to the assets held within any account, and not to the account itself. A tax-advantaged or other account is permitted if its holdings are eligible assets. The Office shall issue opinions under \u00a7 4-1(h) applying this Section to particular account types."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Exclusions","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"The following are excluded from the prohibition in \u00a7 3-1:\n\n**(a) Residences.** Not more than two active residences at any time. For a Member of Congress, one shall be located in the State or district the Member represents. A covered person may change a residence during covered service, but upon acquiring a new residence that would exceed two, shall vacate a prior residence and list it for public sale at a reasonable market price within 90 days, and shall not rent it, let it on a short-term basis, or use it for business in the interim. A residence used to generate rental income, let on a short-term basis, or used for business is not excluded and is subject to \u00a7 3-1. Any purchase, sale, or refinancing of a residence during covered service or the period under \u00a7 3-7 requires independent appraisal, disclosure of the counterparty, and financing on market terms; a transaction on off-market terms or financed by below-market credit shall be disgorged in an amount equal to three times the differential.\n\n**(b) Household member earned income and employment benefits.** The earned income of a covered person's spouse, domestic partner, or other attributed person \u2014 including wages, salary, and bona fide self-employment income \u2014 and the ordinary benefits of their bona fide employment \u2014 including health insurance, retirement contributions, tuition remission or assistance, employee discounts, and employer-provided housing. Equity compensation, profits interests, carried interest, partnership interests, and deferred compensation are not excluded and are subject to \u00a7 3-1. This exclusion is subject to \u00a7 3-3.\n\n**(c) Use property.** Ordinary personal-use property, except that any single item valued above $50,000, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment, and any work of art, collectible, or precious metal is subject to \u00a7 3-1 and requires independent appraisal and disclosure of the counterparty upon purchase or sale. A digital asset is not use property and is governed by \u00a7 3-5B.\n\n**(d) Prior royalties.** Royalties on works created before covered service, at rates set by contracts existing before covered service. No new contract, renegotiation, or advance is permitted during covered service.\n\n**(e) A pre-existing operating business,** owned before covered service, held under a structure providing no participation in management and no receipt of non-public operational information, from which the covered person recuses on any matter affecting the business, and divested within 48 months under \u00a7 6-3.\n\n**(f) Family support.** Child support, alimony, spousal maintenance, and any other support payment for the benefit of the covered person, a former spouse, or a dependent. Such payments are excluded regardless of amount and are not subject to \u00a7 3-1.\n\n**(g) Earned benefits and ordinary income.** Received by a covered person or an attributed person: earned pension and retirement benefits from employment other than the covered service; veterans' benefits; Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid, and other social-insurance and public-benefit programs; unemployment compensation and workers' compensation; insurance proceeds and indemnity payments; distributions from retirement accounts; government transfer and assistance benefits; and other income or benefits earned, or arising, independently of the covered person's office and not constituting compensation for services rendered during covered service. A scholarship or educational award to a family member of the covered person is excluded; a scholarship, fellowship, or educational award to the covered person is not excluded and is subject to \u00a7 3-1. This subsection does not extend to equity compensation, carried interest, profits interests, deferred compensation, or investment securities, which remain subject to \u00a7 3-1 and to the divestiture and holding rules of this Act.\n\n**(h) Actively-operated business of a household member.** A bona fide trade or business actively conducted by an attributed person as that person's trade or occupation is excluded, and the attributed person is not required to divest it, hold it passively, or refrain from conducting, financing, raising capital for, or selling it. No prior approval of the Office or any other authority is required for any such activity; the attributed person acts freely. The covered person shall disclose the business, and any outside investment or debt it takes on and the identity of each investor or lender, under Section 1, and shall recuse from any matter specifically affecting the business or, where it has taken outside investment or debt, any investor in or lender to it. The business and its financing remain subject to \u00a7 3-3, \u00a7 4-3, and \u00a7 4-7 on the same terms as any other arrangement. A passive ownership interest not actively conducted as a trade or occupation \u2014 including a limited-partner interest, a holding company, or a portfolio of securities or rental property \u2014 is not excluded by this subsection and is subject to \u00a7 3-1."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5a","heading":"\u00a7 3-5A. Cash and Deposits","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person may hold, without limit, United States currency and funds in deposit accounts at a depository institution insured by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation or the National Credit Union Administration, whether or not the account bears interest.\n\n**(b)** An interest in a money-market fund, a brokered certificate of deposit, commercial paper, or any other security is not a deposit under this Section and is subject to \u00a7 3-1.\n\n**(c)** Cash and deposits under this Section shall be disclosed under Section 1."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5b","heading":"\u00a7 3-5B. Foreign Currency and Digital Assets","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Tolerances.** A covered person may hold foreign currency, in physical form or in accounts, and digital assets, subject to the following limits measured as two separate pools:\n\n1. the aggregate value of foreign-currency holdings shall not exceed $10,000, valued at the United States dollar market rate; and\n\n2. the aggregate value of digital-asset holdings shall not exceed $10,000, valued at the United States dollar market rate.\n\n**(b) Ordinary use.** This Section does not restrict the use of credit, charge, or debit facilities, wire transfers, or the transient conversion of currency for travel or transactions.\n\n**(c) Cure or disgorge.** Where a pool exceeds $10,000 on a monthly filing under \u00a7 1-4, the covered person shall reduce that pool to $9,999.99 or less before the next monthly filing. If the pool still exceeds $10,000 on the next monthly filing, the covered person shall disgorge an amount equal to the value of the pool above $10,000 on that filing. The knowledge-and-cure rule of \u00a7 3-2(f) applies. A disgorgement under this Section is a personal civil obligation of the covered person; the Government does not take custody of any holding, and the obligation is not deductible for any tax purpose.\n\n**(d) Severability within this Section.** If subsection (c) is held invalid, foreign currency and digital assets above the tolerances are governed by \u00a7 3-1 as non-eligible assets."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5c","heading":"\u00a7 3-5C. Gifts","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person is subject to the prohibition on soliciting or accepting a gift from a prohibited source, and a gift given because of official position, under 5 C.F.R. Part 2635, Subpart B, which is hereby applied to all covered persons including Members of Congress. The exceptions of that Subpart, including the exception for gifts motivated by a personal relationship, apply.\n\n**(b)** Notwithstanding the disclosure exclusions of 5 C.F.R. Part 2634 and Part 2635 for gifts from relatives and for bequests, a covered person shall disclose under Section 1 all gifts from a family member exceeding $10,000 in the aggregate in a calendar year, and each involuntary acquisition.\n\n**(c)** A gift from a prohibited-source family member above the de minimis amount under 5 C.F.R. \u00a7 2635.204(a) that is not within the personal-relationship exception is a prohibited receipt under \u00a7 3-1. If the prohibition in this subsection is held invalid as applied to any class of transfers, such transfers are instead subject to enhanced disclosure under subsection (b) and to conversion under \u00a7 3-5D, and remain subject to \u00a7 4-7 for concealment."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5d","heading":"\u00a7 3-5D. Involuntary Acquisition","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person who receives an involuntary acquisition during covered service shall disclose it under Section 1 within 30 days of receipt and shall divest and convert it to eligible assets within the periods under \u00a7 6-3, measured from the date of acquisition, holding it during that period under a structure providing no participation in management and no receipt of non-public operational information, and recusing from any matter affecting it.\n\n**(b)** The value of an involuntary acquisition belongs to the covered person; only its form is converted. A conversion required by this Section is eligible for deferral under \u00a7 6-3. Where the asset is inherited, the \u00a7 1014 basis step-up at the decedent's death applies in the ordinary course, so that on the required conversion only gain accruing after the acquisition is recognized.\n\n**(c)** An involuntary acquisition, including an inheritance or bequest, is not prohibited under \u00a7 3-1 by reason of the source being a prohibited source. Where the source is a prohibited source, the covered person shall so state in the disclosure under Section 2, and the acquisition is subject to enhanced reconciliation review under \u00a7 1-1."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5e","heading":"\u00a7 3-5E. Solicited Donations","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a) Definition.** A **solicited donation** is a payment, transfer of property, or provision of services or other value, made to the United States, to any agency, or to any project, fund, trust, foundation, or other entity, where a covered person solicited, requested, designated, or directed the donation, or where the donation is made to a project or entity the covered person publicly identified as a recipient. A donation is a solicited donation whether or not it is made through an intermediary and whether or not the recipient is an agency of the United States.\n\n**(a-1) The solicitor states the fact.** A covered person who solicits, requests, designates, or directs a donation shall inform the recipient, at the time of the solicitation, designation, or direction, that a resulting donation is a solicited donation under this Section. The duty runs to the party who knows the fact the screen under subsection (b) requires, and a failure to inform is a violation by the covered person and not a defense for any person.\n\n**(b) Screen before acceptance.** Before a solicited donation of **$10,000** or more is accepted, the receiving agency or entity shall refer it to **the ethics committee of the covered person's chamber**, which shall determine and record **in writing, and before acceptance**, whether the donor \u2014 or any person the donor controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with \u2014 has a claim, procurement action, application, litigation, enforcement matter, license, permit, or other particular matter **pending before the United States**, and shall identify each such matter.\n\n**(c) Publication.** Within **30 days** of acceptance, the receiving agency or entity shall publish \u2014\n1. the identity of the donor and, where the donor is not a natural person, its beneficial owners;\n2. the amount or value, and the form, of the donation;\n3. the project, fund, or entity receiving it, and the covered person who solicited, designated, or directed it; and\n4. **the determination recorded under subsection (b), including each pending matter identified.**\n\n**(d) No prohibition.** This Section **prohibits no donation and no acceptance.** It requires a screen before acceptance and publication after it, and confers on no officer any authority to approve or disapprove a donation.\n\n**(e) Acceptance is not authority to obligate.** Acceptance of a donation is **not authority to obligate or expend** the amount received, and no amount received may be obligated or expended except as an appropriation provides.\n\n**(f) Restrictions travel with the money.** Where an amount received under this Section is transferred to another account, fund, or entity, **it remains subject to every limitation that applied to it before the transfer**, and becomes subject to every limitation applicable to the account into which it is transferred.\n\n**(g) Anti-substitution.** Where a covered person causes a donation to be solicited, designated, or directed **by another person**, or directs it **to an entity the covered person or a member of the covered person's family controls, advises, or serves**, the requirements of this Section apply according to their terms."},{"anchor":"sec-3-6","heading":"\u00a7 3-6. Allocation for Covered Persons","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person and any spouse holding an attributed asset shall hold their eligible assets in the funds under the People\u2019s Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4, allocated between the People's Prosperity Fund and the age-adjusted diversified lifecycle fund as provided in \u00a7 2-1. Within the diversified lifecycle fund, holdings follow the age-based glidepath and may not be self-directed.\n\n**(b)** A covered person may not self-direct or reallocate holdings, and may not change the \u00a7 2-1 allocation, except at a congressional term boundary as provided in \u00a7 2-1.\n\n**(c)** Contributions and withdrawals shall be scheduled at least 30 days in advance. This requirement may be waived by the Office for hardship, including medical, educational, housing, family, or other substantial financial need, and upon divorce, disability, or death of a spouse. The 30-day scheduling requirement of this subsection is severable; if it is held invalid, subsections (a) and (b) remain in effect.\n\n**(d) De minimis holdings.** Non-eligible holdings whose aggregate value does not exceed $5,000, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment, are not subject to the holding rules of this Section or to \u00a7 3-1, and may be reported in aggregate. This threshold is cumulative across all such holdings, not per item. This subsection does not exclude any holding from the reconciliation of net worth under \u00a7 1-1.\n\n**(e) Retirement plans not controlled by the holder.** Where an attributed person holds a retirement account whose investment menu the person does not control, including an employer-sponsored plan, compliance is satisfied by selecting the available option closest to the funds under the People\u2019s Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4, such as a broad-market index or target-date fund. Strict compliance with the People\u2019s Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4 is not required where the plan does not offer a conforming option."},{"anchor":"sec-3-7","heading":"\u00a7 3-7. Post-Service Restrictions","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"For five years after covered service, a former covered person shall:\n\n**(a)** remain subject to \u00a7 3-1;\n\n**(b)** receive no compensation from any entity with material business before the committee, agency, or program over which the person held jurisdiction;\n\n**(c)** hold no position as a director, officer, trustee, or advisory-board member of, and receive no fee for a speech, appearance, or article from, any entity with material business before the committee, agency, or program over which the person held jurisdiction.\n\n**Exceptions.** The restrictions of paragraphs (b) and (c) do not apply to compensation for services rendered to an accredited educational institution, or to a position held without compensation in a religious, charitable, or civic organization that has no material business before the person's former jurisdiction."},{"anchor":"sec-3-7a","heading":"\u00a7 3-7A. Positions and Fees During Service","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person shall not, during covered service, hold any position as a director, officer, trustee, partner, or advisory-board member of any for-profit entity, or receive any fee, honorarium, or other compensation for a speech, appearance, or article.\n\n**(b)** Subsection (a) does not reach an uncompensated position in a religious, charitable, or civic organization, service to an accredited educational institution permitted by existing law, or the receipt of royalties on a work of authorship on terms available to the public and disclosed under Section 1.\n\n**(c)** This Section supplements existing restrictions on outside earned income and honoraria and does not reduce them. As applied to officers and employees below the level of covered person, the honoraria restriction of existing law continues to govern, consistent with United States v. National Treasury Employees Union."},{"anchor":"sec-3-8","heading":"\u00a7 3-8. Advisory Rulings and Safe Harbor","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE OFFICEHOLDER RULE","body":"**(a)** A covered person may request a written opinion from the Office under \u00a7 4-1(h) as to whether an arrangement complies with this Act. The Office may find compliance only where the arrangement results in the covered person holding solely eligible assets, with no attributed exposure to a non-eligible asset under \u00a7 3-2 or \u00a7 3-3.\n\n**(b)** A covered person who fully and accurately discloses an arrangement and receives a ruling of compliance is immune from criminal liability under this Act with respect to the holding of that arrangement as disclosed.\n\n**(c)** No ruling immunizes, and no safe harbor bars prosecution for:\n\n1. a material omission or misrepresentation in the disclosure on which the ruling was based, which voids the ruling from the outset; or\n\n2. concealment, misrepresentation, or any other felony under \u00a7 4-7.\n\n**(d)** Immunity extends only to conduct fully and accurately disclosed.\n\n**(e)** The Office shall publish each ruling and the arrangement on which it is based, redacted only as necessary, and each ruling is precedential."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. The Office of Congressional Integrity","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Establishment.** There is established in the legislative branch an Office of Congressional Integrity, headed by a Director appointed for a single fixed term of 8 years by the Speaker of the House and the President pro tempore of the Senate acting jointly, on the recommendation of a bipartisan panel, and removable before the expiration of the term only by concurrent resolution of both Houses stating the grounds, which shall be published. While the office of Director is vacant, or the Director is unable to act, each function this Act assigns to the Director is performed by the senior officer of the Office then serving, and no duty of the Office is suspended, tolled, or excused by the vacancy.\n\n**(b) No executive power.** The Office receives filings, publishes them, maintains the database required by \u00a7 1-5, provides the filing tools required by subsection (f), makes referrals, and performs the ministerial functions this Act assigns. The Office shall not impose any fine, penalty, or disgorgement, shall not bring any civil action, shall not prosecute, and shall not compel any person outside the legislative branch. The Office exercises no part of the executive power of the United States.\n\n**(d) Ministerial duty to publish.** The publication required by \u00a7 1-5 is a ministerial duty, enforceable by mandamus at the suit of any person, and no discretion attaches to it.\n\n**(e) Preservation.** The Office shall preserve every filing, certification, valuation, and custodial report in immutable form with the date of receipt, and shall not alter or delete any record except as \u00a7 1-8 requires. The Comptroller General shall audit the Office's custody and publication practices annually, including the charging of amounts and positions to \u00a7 6-5(b-1) and (b-2) and the order and timeliness in which complaints were taken up, determined, and disposed of as the record under subsection (f-1) shows them, and publish the audit, stating what it tested and identifying no covered person to whom a complaint not determined to state probable cause relates. **The audit is effective in every fiscal year without regard to \u00a7 6-5(c). It is not excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated**, and a defense that it was not effective for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce it. The Comptroller General performs the audit from amounts otherwise available to the Comptroller General; the first sentence of \u00a7 6-5(e) does not reach those amounts, and the second sentence of \u00a7 6-5(e) does not bar the Comptroller General from applying them to the audit. Section 716 of title 31, United States Code, applies to the Office in the conduct of the audit as it applies to an agency, except that subsection (d)(1)(C) of that section does not apply, and data held under \u00a7 1-8 remains subject to \u00a7 1-8(b). The audit is an activity authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations: the employment of personal services for it is authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code, and an obligation incurred for it before an appropriation is made is authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of that title. The audit and its publication are ministerial duties, enforceable by mandamus as subsection (d) provides.\n\n**(f) Filing tools and auto-population.** The Office shall provide, without charge, standardized machine-readable software and forms for every filing required by this Act, and shall generate draft filings from custodial data as \u00a7 1-3 provides.\n\n**(f-1) Complaints: who may file, and the order in which they are taken.** Any person may file with the Office a complaint that a covered person has violated this Act, without showing any interest in the matter. A complaint shall be in writing and signed, and shall identify the covered person, the provision of this Act said to be violated, and the facts on which it rests. A claim presented to the Office under \u00a7 4-5(f) is a complaint under this subsection. The Office shall acknowledge each complaint in writing to the person who filed it within 14 days, stating the date of filing, from which the period in subsection (j) runs. **The Office shall take up complaints for screening under subsection (g) and for disposition under subsection (j) in the order in which they were filed**, shall consolidate complaints resting on the same facts, a consolidated complaint taking the place in that order, and the periods in subsections (g) and (j), of the earliest complaint consolidated, and shall not advance or defer a complaint by reason of the identity, party, chamber, or position of the covered person or of the person who filed it. The Office takes up a complaint for screening when it begins to determine whether the complaint states probable cause, and for disposition when, after that determination, it begins to determine how the complaint is to be disposed of, and a complaint awaiting a response under this subsection delays the taking up of no other complaint. The Office shall assign each complaint a sequence number in the order of filing, state it in the acknowledgment, and enter in the record it preserves under subsection (e) the dates on which the complaint was filed, acknowledged, taken up for screening and for disposition, determined under subsection (g), furnished to the covered person, answered, and disposed of. An entry relating to a complaint not determined to state probable cause is not published. A complaint that does not identify the covered person, the provision, or the facts is dismissed under subsection (g) by a writing stating what it lacks, given with the acknowledgment, and may be filed again, and a complaint filed again within 30 days after that writing that supplies what it identified takes the place in order, and the period in subsection (j), of the complaint dismissed. A complaint filed again after a dismissal under subsection (g) for want of probable cause, stating no fact the dismissed complaint did not state, is dismissed under subsection (g) by a writing so stating. For purposes of \u00a7 6-5(b-2) and (b-2A), complaints consolidated under this subsection are one complaint, and a complaint dismissed as stating no new fact is counted with the complaint it repeats. Before making a written finding on a complaint, the Office shall furnish the covered person the complaint and the facts on which the proposed finding rests, and 30 days to respond, extended once by a further 30 days on the covered person's written request made within the first period, and shall publish any response with the finding. The Office shall inform the person who filed a complaint of its disposition by a writing stating its ground. A complaint is dismissed only by a writing stating its ground, and the only grounds are, under subsection (g), what the complaint lacks, the element or fact for which the Office finds no probable cause, or that the complaint repeats one so dismissed and states no new fact, and, after a determination that the complaint states probable cause, the element or fact the record does not establish. Neither its withdrawal by the person who filed it nor the covered person's leaving covered service disposes of a complaint. Where a complaint states probable cause and the Office cannot determine it on the record without evidence held by a person outside the legislative branch, whom the Office may not compel, the Office shall transmit the complaint and its record to the Attorney General without finding, and the transmission disposes of the complaint to that extent for purposes of subsection (j). **A complaint is disposed of only by dismissal, by a written finding transmitted under \u00a7 4-2(a), by a referral under \u00a7 4-9(e), by a transmission to the Attorney General under this subsection, or by a transmission under subsection (j-1), and, except that a claim presented under \u00a7 4-5(f) has the effect that subsection gives it, no other consequence follows from it.**\n\n**(g) Screening before publication.** The Office shall not publish a complaint, or identify its subject, until it has determined that the complaint states probable cause. A complaint failing that threshold is dismissed. The Office shall determine whether a complaint states probable cause, or make the transmission subsection (j-1) requires, within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the complaint is filed. Upon determining that a complaint states probable cause, the Office shall furnish the covered person the determination, stating the provision and the facts on which it rests, and, not earlier than it so furnishes the determination and within 7 days after making it, shall publish the determination in the database under \u00a7 1-5, stating the covered person, the provision, and the facts, and not the name of, or any fact identifying, the person who filed the complaint. The Office shall publish any response the covered person files to the determination alongside it within 48 hours of the filing. Where the Office disposes of a complaint whose determination it has published, otherwise than by a written finding published under \u00a7 4-2(a), it shall publish the disposition, its ground, and any response of the covered person, in the same place and with the same prominence as the determination. Probable cause under this subsection governs publication and screening, and is not a finding under \u00a7 4-2(a).\n\n**(h) Advisory opinions.** The Office shall, on written request, issue a published written opinion whether a described arrangement complies with this Act. An opinion is precedential. Reliance in good faith on an opinion is a complete defense to civil liability under \u00a7 4-3 and to criminal liability under \u00a7 4-7 as to the arrangement described, and this defense is available in any court. An opinion does not survive a material omission in the request. The Office's opinion function is interpretive and advisory; it binds no person other than as a defense conferred by this subsection.\n\n**(h-1) If the defense cannot run against the United States.** If the defense conferred by subsection (h), by \u00a7 3-8(b), or by \u00a7 4-1A(d) or (e) is held invalid as applied to an action or prosecution by the United States, or to an action under \u00a7 4-5(k), on the ground that an effect of that kind may be conferred only by an officer of the United States appointed under Article II, Section 2, then as to that action or prosecution: (1) reliance in good faith on an opinion, obtained on a complete and accurate description of the arrangement, remains a complete defense to any consequence under \u00a7 4-2; (2) such reliance is admissible on whether the covered person acted willfully or concealed; and (3) in an action under \u00a7 4-3 or \u00a7 4-5(k), the court shall impose no disgorgement or civil penalty for any period during which the court finds that the covered person so relied. The court determines for itself the good faith of the reliance and the completeness and accuracy of the description. This subsection operates only upon such a holding and is severable from subsection (h).\n\n**(i) The Office is bound.** The Director and each senior officer of the Office is a covered person under this Act.\n\n**(j) Deadline.** The Office shall dispose of a complaint, in a manner subsection (f-1) provides, within 12 months of its filing. Where it has not, it shall publish the reason for the delay and renew the statement every 6 months until it disposes of the complaint, and the statement is not a disposition and does not discharge the duty in the first sentence of this subsection. A statement concerning a complaint not determined under subsection (g) to state probable cause identifies the complaint only by its sequence number under subsection (f-1) and is furnished to the person who filed it. A complaint is not resolved by inaction.\n\n**(j-1) No inquiry into a legislative act.** \u00a7 4-6 and \u00a7 3-1A(f) apply to the receipt, screening, and disposition of a complaint. The Office shall not inquire into, and shall dispose of no complaint on, a legislative act, a vote, or the obligation to recuse under \u00a7 3-1A(a). To the extent a complaint rests on any of them, the Office shall transmit it without finding to the Committee on Ethics of the House of Representatives or the Select Committee on Ethics of the Senate, as applicable, and the transmission disposes of the complaint to that extent for purposes of subsection (j). This subsection does not limit the Office's disposition of a complaint that a recusal required by \u00a7 3-1A(b) was not filed or was false, which \u00a7 3-1A(f) makes provable without reference to any legislative act.\n\n**(j-2) The complaint duties are enforceable.** Each duty of the Office under subsections (f-1), (g), and (j) to acknowledge, to give a writing, to take up complaints in order, to determine, transmit, or dispose of a complaint within a period those subsections set, to furnish, to inform, or to publish is ministerial, and no discretion attaches to whether or when it is performed. The district courts of the United States have jurisdiction of an action in the nature of mandamus by the person who filed the complaint, by the covered person to whom it relates, or by the United States acting by the Attorney General, against the Director or, while that office is vacant, the officer performing its functions, to compel its performance, and the court shall order performance upon finding that the duty was not performed as this section requires. \u00a7 4-6 applies to an action under this subsection, which requires proof of no legislative act.\n\n**(k) Counter-cyclical resources.** The funding floor in the schedule to this Act is a floor and not a ceiling, applies to amounts appropriated under \u00a7 6-5(a), and does not limit the amounts appropriated by \u00a7 6-5(b-1) and (b-2); and the Office's resources shall be permitted to expand to meet increases in filing and complaint volume.\n\n**(l) Aggregate reporting.** The Office shall publish annually the number of filings, the rate of timely and complete filing, the number of complaints received and the number after consolidation under subsection (f-1), the number screened and the number of those screened within the period subsection (g) sets, the number sustained, the number of referrals under \u00a7 4-2 and \u00a7 4-9(e), the number of complaints transmitted to the Attorney General under subsection (f-1) and the number transmitted to a committee under subsection (j-1), each with the average time from filing to transmission, the number of statements subject to \u00a7 1-1(b-4), of reconciliations under it completed within the period it sets, of notices given under \u00a7 1-1(b-3), and of transmissions to the Attorney General under \u00a7 1-1(b-4), and the outcomes of actions brought under \u00a7 4-3, \u00a7 4-5, \u00a7 3-3B, and \u00a7 3-3C.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-1a","heading":"\u00a7 4-1A. Advisory Opinion System","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Deadline.** The Office shall issue an opinion under \u00a7 4-1(h) within 60 days of a complete request, and shall publish each opinion within 30 days of issuance.\n\n**(a-1) When a request is complete.** A request under \u00a7 4-1(h) or subsection (d) is complete on the date the Office receives it unless, within the period set in the schedule to this Act, the Office identifies to the requester in writing each fact or document missing from the request that is necessary to decide it. A request so identified is complete on the date the requester supplies what the writing identified. The Office may identify a further omission only where it arises from what the requester supplied, and then within the same period running from the supply. A request is not incomplete for want of anything not so identified. This subsection fixes when the periods in subsections (a) and (d) begin; it does not decide whether a description is complete and accurate for purposes of the defense under \u00a7 4-1(h), and an opinion issued on a request complete under this subsection does not survive a material omission in it.\n\n**(b) De-identification and publication.** Every opinion issued under \u00a7 4-1(h) shall be de-identified \u2014 removing the requester's name and any facts that would identify the requester \u2014 and published in a searchable, indexed library within 30 days of issuance. An opinion that cannot be de-identified without destroying its utility shall be published with the requester's consent or, absent consent, with a statement that a non-public opinion exists on the described subject.\n\n**(c) Shared advisory opinion library.** The Office shall publish each opinion issued under \u00a7 4-1(h) in a shared advisory opinion library maintained jointly with the bodies designated under the Executive Integrity Act and the Judiciary Integrity Act. The library is an index and publication function; it is not a body, exercises no authority, and employs no staff beyond those of the contributing bodies.\n\n**(d) Applicability requests.** A covered person may request a determination that a published opinion applies to the person's arrangement. If the Office determines that it does, the person is entitled to the same reliance defense under \u00a7 4-1(h) as the original requester. If it does not, the Office shall issue a new opinion, which is de-identified and published under subsection (b). An applicability request shall be decided within 30 days.\n\n**(e) Mutual recognition.** A covered person may cite, in a request under \u00a7 4-1(h), an opinion issued by the enforcement body of another branch addressing the same or a substantially similar arrangement. Upon such a citation, the Office shall, within 30 days: (1) **adopt** the cited opinion as applicable to this branch, in which case the requesting person receives the same reliance defense as if the opinion had been issued under this Act, and the adoption is published; (2) **decline to adopt** the cited opinion without issuing a differing view, in which case the requesting person may seek an opinion under \u00a7 4-1(h) and no inference arises from the declination; or (3) **issue a differing opinion**, which is published with a statement of the respects in which it differs and the reasons, creating a published split. An opinion adopted by all three branches is marked \"uniform\" in the shared library. A covered person in any branch may rely on a uniform opinion without further request.\n\n**(f) Pending matters register.** The shared library shall include a register of pending matters. When the Office receives a request addressing a novel question \u2014 one for which no opinion exists in the library \u2014 the Office shall, within 7 days, post notice to the register identifying the subject matter in general terms. The notice shall not identify the requester, disclose confidential information, or describe the specific facts of the request.\n\n**(g) Independent action.** Each body acts independently. A body receiving a request on a subject posted to the register may wait for the pending opinion to issue and then adopt, diverge, or decline under subsection (e), or may proceed to issue its own opinion without waiting. No body is required to wait, to consult, or to conform its opinion to another's. No body shall share with another body the identity of a requester, the facts of a pending request, or any draft opinion. The register is a notice of subject matter only; it creates no joint proceeding, no shared deliberation, and no authority of one body over another.\n\n**(h) Tolling while pending.** Where a covered person has submitted a request for an opinion and the Office elects to hold that request pending the issuance of an opinion by another branch under subsection (g), any deadline under this Act that runs against the requester \u2014 including a deadline to divest, to elect, or to file \u2014 is tolled from the date the hold begins until the earlier of: (1) the date the opinion issues, or (2) the date the Office elects to proceed independently. The requester shall be notified promptly when a hold begins and when it ends. No penalty, consequence, or adverse inference attaches to a requester by reason of a hold.\n\n**(i) Maximum hold.** A hold under subsection (h) shall not exceed 60 days. If the pending opinion has not issued within that period, the Office shall proceed to issue an opinion or adopt, diverge, or decline based on any opinion then in the library.\n\n**(j) Adequate staffing.** The amounts appropriated by \u00a7 6-5(b-2) include staffing sufficient to issue opinions within the deadlines of this Section without routine resort to holds. The Office shall report annually the number of holds invoked, their average duration, the number of requests received and not decided within the periods in subsections (a) and (d) and, for those, the average time from receipt to decision or, where none has issued, to the end of the year, and whether staffing is adequate to meet demand. A pattern of holds approaching the maximum under subsection (i) is evidence of inadequate staffing and shall be stated as such in the report.\n\n**(k) No super-agency.** Nothing in this Section creates a body with authority over more than one branch, compels any branch to adopt another's interpretation, or permits any branch to bind another. The Office retains complete interpretive authority over covered persons within the legislative branch."},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Internal Consequences","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Upon a written finding that a covered person has violated this Act, the Office shall transmit the finding, with the record, to the Committee on Ethics of the House of Representatives or the Select Committee on Ethics of the Senate, as applicable, and shall publish the finding.\n\n**(b)** The consequences available on such a finding are those available to each House with respect to its Members and employees, including reprimand, censure, loss of a committee assignment or chairmanship, loss of a leadership position, restriction of privileges, and, as to an employee, discipline or removal from employment. This Act imposes none of them; it makes the finding and refers it.\n\n**(c)** No consequence under this Section excludes a Member from a seat, adds a qualification for office, or affects the outcome of any election. Expulsion remains available to each House under Article I, Section 5, and this Act neither authorizes nor limits it.\n\n**(d)** Nothing in this Section requires either House to act on a finding, and the failure of a House to act does not affect liability under \u00a7 4-3, \u00a7 4-5, or \u00a7 4-7."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Civil Liability for Concealment and Failure to Elect","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) What is remedied.** A civil remedy under this Section lies for: concealing an asset required to be disclosed under Section 1; taking none of the courses required by \u00a7 3-1(b) with respect to a non-eligible asset; retaining an asset for which no election is available under \u00a7 3-1(c); failing to file a recusal required by \u00a7 3-1A(b), or filing a recusal that is false; and a violation of \u00a7 3-1(a), \u00a7 3-2, \u00a7 3-2A, or \u00a7 3-3. **No remedy lies under this Section by reason of holding an asset lawfully retained under an election that complies with \u00a7 3-1(b)(3).**\n\n**(a-1) Disgorgement.** A violation described in subsection (a) is subject to disgorgement of three times the gain attributable to the asset, together with a civil penalty and a published finding.\n\n**(a-2) Gain includes appreciation.** Gain means the sum of income received and the appreciation in the fair market value of the asset over the period of the violation, whether or not the asset was sold, exchanged, or otherwise disposed of, measured from the later of acquisition or the expiration of the applicable period under \u00a7 6-2, to the earlier of disposition or the date the violation is found.\n\n**(a-3) Intent.** Liability for failure to elect does not require proof of intent. Liability for concealment requires concealment within the meaning of the definition of that term.\n\n**(b) Penalty scaled to what was concealed.** The civil penalty for concealment shall be a percentage, set in the schedule to this Act, of the fair market value of the asset concealed, determined as of the date the concealment began, escalating with each year or part of a year of concealment, and not less than the floor set in the schedule. A court may impose more than the amount so determined but shall not impose less.\n\n**(b-2) Measure severable; alternative measure.** If the measure in subsection (b) is held invalid in whole or in any application, the civil penalty is instead three times the gain, together with an additional amount for each day of concealment set in the schedule, and the floor continues to apply.\n\n**(b-3) Assets reported by a custodian.** The schedule shall set a lower percentage under subsection (b) for an asset held with a custodian required to report it under \u00a7 1-3, and a higher percentage for an asset not so held, determined as of the date the concealment began.\n\n**(d) Crime-fraud framing of facilitator liability.** Liability under subsection (e) reaches a lawyer, accountant, or other professional only as to services falling within the crime-fraud exception to any applicable privilege, and no privileged communication is required to be disclosed except as that exception permits. This Act creates no exception to any privilege.\n\n**(e) Facilitator liability.** A person or entity that knowingly facilitates a violation described in subsection (a) \u2014 including by forming, administering, or serving as trustee or nominee of a structure whose purpose or effect is to conceal a beneficial interest, or by preparing a valuation or certification known to be false \u2014 is subject to disgorgement of any fee or gain received and to a civil penalty. This subsection does not reach the ordinary provision of legal, accounting, or custodial services to a person complying with this Act.\n\n**(f) Universal personal liability.** A disgorgement or penalty under this Act is payable personally by the violator from the violator's own funds, and shall not be paid, advanced, indemnified, reimbursed, or insured by any employer, campaign, political committee, party, or other person. Any agreement to do so is void, and a person who pays another's liability in violation of this subsection is subject to a penalty in an equal amount.\n\n**(g) Who brings the action.** A civil action under this Section is brought by the Attorney General in a district court of the United States, or by a person under \u00a7 4-5 to the extent that Section permits.\n\n**(h) Installments.** Where a remedy under this Section exceeds the covered person's liquid resources and the asset giving rise to it cannot be sold without substantial loss, the court shall permit payment in installments on the terms provided for closely held business interests under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 6166. This subsection applies whether or not the violation involved concealment."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3a","heading":"\u00a7 4-3A. Tax on a Retained Asset","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Imposition.** There is imposed on a covered person who elects under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) to disclose and retain an asset a tax measured by a percentage of the fair market value of the asset, imposed for each period during which the asset is retained under that election.\n\n**(a-0) Computation; prorated by days retained.** The tax for a taxable year is the applicable rate under subsection (a-1), multiplied by the fair market value of the asset determined under subsection (f), multiplied by a fraction whose numerator is the number of days in the year during which the asset was retained under the election and whose denominator is the number of days in that year. An asset retained for 129 days of a 365-day year, at an applicable rate of 2.5 percent, bears a tax of 2.5 percent multiplied by 129 divided by 365.\n\n**(a-1) Rate escalating with duration of retention.** The percentage is that set in the schedule to this Act, which shall provide a rate rising with the number of consecutive years the asset has been retained under an election: a lower rate in the first year, rising in each of the second and third years, and reaching its maximum in the fourth year and each year thereafter.\n\n**(a-3) Measure of the rate.** The maximum rate is set by reference to the expected return on a diversified portfolio, so that a person retaining a conflicted asset indefinitely does not obtain a net advantage from the concentrated position. It is not set by reference to the administrative burden of retention.\n\n**(b) The tax discharges the obligation.** Payment of the tax, together with the disclosure required by Section 1 and the recusal required by \u00a7 3-1A, satisfies \u00a7 3-1 with respect to the asset. A covered person who elects under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3), discloses, recuses, and pays holds the asset lawfully and is not in violation of this Act by reason of holding it.\n\n**(c) Character.** This Section prices one of three lawful courses available under \u00a7 3-1(b). It is not imposed for unlawful conduct, is not conditioned on any finding of violation, and its payment discharges rather than accompanies the obligation.\n\n**(d) No intent required.** Liability under this Section does not depend on knowledge, purpose, or motive.\n\n**(e) Self-assessment and collection.** The tax shall be reported and paid by the covered person on the person's annual Federal income-tax return, on a form the Secretary prescribes, and is assessed and collected in the manner of chapter 42 of the Internal Revenue Code. A failure to report the tax, or a false statement in reporting it, is subject to sections 7201, 7206, and 7207 of the Internal Revenue Code to the same extent as any other tax.\n\n**(f) Valuation.** The value on which the tax is computed is the fair market value of the asset on the last day of the taxable year, or on the last day of retention if the asset ceases to be retained during the year, and shall be established by independent appraisal where the asset has no readily ascertainable market price, without regard to the reporting threshold in \u00a7 1-1(a-2). A substantial understatement of that value is subject to an accuracy-related penalty in the manner of section 6662 of the Internal Revenue Code.\n\n**(f-1) Counting years of retention.** For purposes of subsection (a-1), the years an asset has been retained are counted cumulatively and are not reset by leaving and re-entering covered service, by a change of position, or by transferring the asset into another form in which the covered person retains the same economic interest. Where an asset is divested and an equivalent interest is reacquired within two years, the count continues from where it stopped. Periods of retention of less than a full year are aggregated in determining the year in which an asset stands. **Years of retention under the Congressional Integrity Act or the Executive Integrity Act, whichever is not this Act, count as years of retention under this subsection, and a change of branch does not reset the count.**\n\n**(f-2) No deferral for a retained asset.** An asset retained under an election made pursuant to \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) is not eligible for deferral of gain under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 1043 or under the Certificate of Divestiture procedure, whether the asset is disposed of during covered service or afterward.\n\n**(f-3) Deemed disposition at the conclusion of covered service.** Upon the conclusion of a covered person's covered service, each asset then retained under an election made pursuant to \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) is treated as sold for its fair market value on that date and reacquired at that value, and the gain or loss is recognized in the taxable year in which covered service concludes.\n\n**(f-4) Relief on a deemed disposition.** Where the tax arising under subsection (f-3) exceeds the covered person's liquid resources, the Secretary shall permit payment in installments on the terms provided for closely held business interests under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 6166, and the person may elect to defer payment until the asset is actually disposed of, with interest at the underpayment rate, upon furnishing security the Secretary finds adequate. Basis is adjusted in every case to the value at which the asset was treated as sold, so that no amount recognized under subsection (f-3) is taxed again on a later disposition.\n\n**(f-5) Each of subsections (f-2) and (f-3) is independent.** Neither depends on the other, on the tax imposed by subsection (a), or on any other provision of this Section, and each operates if the others do not.\n\n**(f-6) Assets below the reporting threshold.** Notwithstanding subsection (f), where the fair market value of a retained asset is below the threshold in \u00a7 1-1(a-2), the tax is computed on a good-faith estimate, and independent appraisal is required upon entry into covered service, upon the asset ceasing to be retained, and upon any material change in the asset or its value, as \u00a7 1-1(a-2) provides. Nothing in this subsection changes which assets are subject to the tax or the rate at which it is imposed.\n\n**(f-7) A market price is waited for.** Where an asset on which the tax has been computed under subsection (f-6) is subsequently sold in an arm's-length transaction, the tax for the two taxable years preceding the sale is recomputed using the sale price, and any additional amount is reported and paid on the return for the year of the sale. A recomputation producing a lower amount is credited on that return. This subsection substitutes an actual price for an estimate and neither creates a penalty nor alters the rate schedule.\n\n**(g) Attributed assets.** Where the asset is attributed to the covered person under \u00a7 3-2 or \u00a7 3-2A, the tax is imposed on the covered person, and any person who holds the asset and who knowingly participates in the election is jointly and severally liable for it.\n\n**(h) The rate remains a price and not a prohibition.** At every level in the schedule, and in every year, the covered person retains a lawful alternative that may be taken at no cost: divestiture or conversion under \u00a7 3-1(b)(1), for which \u00a7 6-3 provides deferral of gain, installment payment, and relief from realization on reallocation within a retirement account; or an instrument under \u00a7 3-1(b)(2).\n\n**(i) Unavailable elections.** No tax under this Section is imposed with respect to an asset described in \u00a7 3-1(c), for which no election to retain is available. Retention of such an asset is a violation of \u00a7 3-1 and is remedied under \u00a7 4-3.\n\n**(j) Installments.** Where the tax exceeds the covered person's liquid resources, the Secretary shall permit payment in installments on the terms provided for closely held business interests under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 6166. Installment payment does not suspend the escalation under subsection (a-1).\n\n**(k) Not deductible.** The tax is not deductible for any tax purpose."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Self-Executing Obligations","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** The following obligations operate without any determination, election, or action by any officer of the United States, and no officer may suspend, waive, or decline to give them effect: the duty to file under Section 1; the duty of a custodian to report under \u00a7 1-3; the generation and publication of filings under \u00a7 1-3 and \u00a7 1-5; the holding requirement of \u00a7 3-1; the prohibitions of \u00a7 3-3B and \u00a7 3-3C; the divestiture and conversion periods of \u00a7 6-2; and the nondeductibility of a disgorgement or penalty under \u00a7 6-3.\n\n**(b)** The failure of any officer or of either House to act does not excuse, toll, or diminish an obligation under subsection (a)."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Action to Compel Disclosure","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Who may sue.** Any person who has been denied information to which this Act entitles the public may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel the filing, correction, or publication of a disclosure required by Section 1, and to obtain a declaration that a filing is incomplete or that a holding required to be disclosed was not disclosed.\n\n**(b) The injury.** This Act entitles the public to the information required to be filed under Section 1 and published under \u00a7 1-5. A person denied that information is injured by the denial, and the injury is concrete: without the information the person cannot determine whether an official who acts on a matter holds a financial interest in its outcome, which is the assessment the disclosure exists to permit.\n\n**(c) Remedies.** The court may order the filing, correction, or publication of a disclosure; may enter a declaration that a holding required to be disclosed was not disclosed and the date on which the obligation arose; and may award reasonable attorney's fees and costs to a prevailing plaintiff. **The court may not award damages, disgorgement, or a civil penalty in an action under this Section, and no plaintiff receives any share of any amount recovered by the United States.**\n\n**(d) Effect of a declaration.** A declaration under subsection (c) that a holding was not disclosed before it was identified by another establishes the fact of non-disclosure for purposes of \u00a7 4-8A, and the consequences of that Section attach as it provides.\n\n**(e) Fee-shifting, both ways.** A prevailing plaintiff is entitled to reasonable attorney's fees and costs. Where the court finds the action frivolous, unreasonable, or brought in bad faith, the plaintiff is liable for the defendant's reasonable fees and costs.\n\n**(f) Notice and opportunity for the United States.** Before filing, the person shall present the claim to the Office and to the Attorney General and allow 60 days. The person may proceed only if, within that period, no action has been commenced and no published finding has resolved the claim. No dismissal, determination, finding, or other act of the Office, of either House, or of any committee or officer of either House is a published finding for purposes of this subsection, and none bars, delays, or conditions an action under this Section. A claim is presented under this subsection on the date the Office and the Attorney General have each received it, whatever disposition the Office makes of it under \u00a7 4-1(f-1). The Attorney General may intervene in an action under this Section at any time, and may move to stay it on a showing that it would prejudice a pending investigation; the court shall grant a stay on such a showing and shall set its duration.\n\n**(g) Public-information bar.** An action may not be based solely on information already published under \u00a7 1-5.\n\n**(h) No proof of legislative acts.** An action under this Section is subject to \u00a7 4-6 and may not require proof of a legislative act of a Member of Congress, and this Section abrogates no privilege.\n\n**(i) Declination published.** Where a plaintiff obtains a declaration under subsection (c) that a holding was not disclosed, and the Attorney General does not commence an action under \u00a7 4-3 with respect to that holding within the period allowed by \u00a7 4-9(d), the Attorney General shall publish that fact. The reasons need not be published, and nothing in this subsection compels any charging or enforcement decision.\n\n**(j) Relation to enforcement by the United States.** This Section supplements enforcement by the Attorney General and does not limit it. Disgorgement and civil penalties are recovered only in an action under \u00a7 4-3.\n\n**(j-1) Concurrent jurisdiction; removal barred.** **(1)** An action under this Section may be brought in a district court of the United States **or in any State court of competent jurisdiction.** **(2)** An action under this Section brought in a State court **may not be removed to a district court of the United States.** **(3)** A State court shall not decline to hear an action under this Section on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States. **(4)** A person is not required to proceed under the subsection providing for partial assignment of the claim of the United States unless an action under this subsection has first been dismissed or is unavailable.\n\n**(k) Alternative structure preserved on adverse holding.** If an action under this Section is held not to present a case or controversy as to any plaintiff or claim, then as to that plaintiff or claim a person may bring an action to enforce \u00a7 3-1, \u00a7 3-2, \u00a7 3-2A, \u00a7 3-3, or Section 1 as a partial assignee of the claim of the United States, receiving a share of not less than 15 and not more than 25 percent of any amount actually recovered, which is paid otherwise to the Treasury, subject to subsections (e) through (h)."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6","heading":"\u00a7 4-6. No Proof of Legislative Acts","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"No civil or criminal liability under this Act requires proof of a legislative act of a Member of Congress, of a vote, of a committee deliberation, or of any communication integral to the legislative process. Each obligation of this Act is established by the holding of an asset, the filing or non-filing of a document, or the truth or falsity of a statement, each provable without reference to any legislative act. Nothing in this Act abrogates the privilege of Article I, Section 6, Clause 1."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6a","heading":"\u00a7 4-6A. Constitutional Claims and Performance","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Scope.** This Section applies to a claim, however phrased and in whatever form advanced, that a provision of the Constitution excuses, displaces, suspends, or renders unenforceable a duty imposed by this Act.\n\n**(b) A claim does not suspend performance.** A duty imposed by this Act continues in effect, and remains enforceable according to its terms, notwithstanding a claim to which this Section applies, unless and until a court of competent jurisdiction holds the duty invalid or inapplicable as to the covered person, or stays its operation pending adjudication.\n\n**(c) Conditions.** A claim to which this Section applies has no effect for any purpose unless it \u2014\n\n**(1)** is made in writing and signed by the covered person, who may not delegate this function;\n\n**(2)** identifies the provision of the Constitution relied upon and the duty said to be excused;\n\n**(3)** states the grounds upon which the covered person contends the provision applies to that duty; and\n\n**(4)** is filed with the Office and published in the database under \u00a7 1-5 not later than the period stated in the schedule to this Act after performance of the duty was due.\n\n**(d) Lapse.** Not later than the period stated in the schedule to this Act after making a claim under this Section, the covered person shall commence an action for declaratory relief as to its validity, or shall withdraw it. A claim neither so pursued nor withdrawn lapses and thereafter has no effect for any purpose.\n\n**(e) Application to a claim first made before the effective date.** Reliance, on or after the effective date of this Act, upon a claim made before that date is itself a claim under this Section, and subsection (c) applies to it as of the date of reliance. Nothing in this Section attaches any consequence to conduct occurring before the effective date.\n\n**(f) Rules of construction.**\n\n**(1)** The claim is preserved and may be raised as a defense in any proceeding. This Section governs the sequence in which such a claim is resolved and the effect of the claim before it is resolved; it does not govern its merits.\n\n**(2)** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of a court to stay the operation of a duty, or to grant any other relief, upon such terms as the court considers just.\n\n**(3)** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of either House to determine the rules of its proceedings, to discipline its Members, or to enforce any obligation of this Act under its own rules.\n\n**(4)** Nothing in this Section abrogates the privilege of Article I, Section 6, Clause 1, or affects the operation of \u00a7 4-6.\n\n**(5)** This Section creates no right of action and no remedy not otherwise available."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6b","heading":"\u00a7 4-6B. Whistleblower Award","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"Where the United States recovers a disgorgement or penalty under this Act based on information provided by an original source, the Attorney General shall pay that source an award of a percentage, set in the schedule to this Act, of the amount actually recovered, paid from the recovery."},{"anchor":"sec-4-7","heading":"\u00a7 4-7. Criminal Liability","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** It is a felony, punishable by imprisonment of not more than five years and the forfeitures \u00a7 4-8(a) provides, to:\n\n1. willfully file a false certification or disclosure under this Act;\n\n2. conceal or misrepresent an arrangement to evade this Act, including through an undisclosed nominee, a sham entity, a layered arrangement used to obscure a beneficial interest, or a backdated or sham transaction; or\n\n3. corruptly direct the investment of a fund offered under the People's Prosperity Act \u00a7 3-4, or corruptly alter the determination of the Middle-Class Growth Rate; or\n\n4. being an officer or employee of the United States within the meaning of \u00a7 3-3C(c), or an individual described in \u00a7 3-3C(a)(3), willfully communicate material non-public information obtained in the course of official duties, by reason of the position held, or in the performance of an arrangement with the United States or by reason of the access it affords, other than in an authorized disclosure under \u00a7 3-3C(c), with intent that a person acquire, hold, or dispose of a financial instrument on the basis of it, or knowing that a person will do so.\n\n**(b)** Criminal liability rests only on the discrete acts described in subsection (a), each provable without inquiry into how any Member legislated. No criminal liability arises from the attribution standards of \u00a7 3-2 or \u00a7 3-2A, from the anti-evasion standard of \u00a7 3-3, or from any assessment of fair market value.\n\n**(c)** An employee of the Bureau of Labor Statistics or a manager of a fund who performs a lawful duty is not liable under subsection (a)(3).\n\n**(d) Published declination.** Where the Attorney General declines to prosecute a matter referred under \u00a7 4-9(e), the Attorney General shall publish the fact of the referral and of the declination. The reasons need not be published."},{"anchor":"sec-4-8","heading":"\u00a7 4-8. Consequences of Conviction","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Forfeiture on conviction.** Paragraphs (1) and (2) apply upon the final conviction of an individual of a felony under this Act, and paragraph (3) applies upon the individual's conviction.\n\n**(1) Annuity credit for covered service.** Notwithstanding any other provision of law, service rendered by the individual in a position or capacity described in \u00a7 0-3(a), whether before or after the date of enactment of this Act, shall not be taken into account for purposes of subchapter III of chapter 83 or chapter 84 of title 5, United States Code, and no annuity is payable on the basis of that service for any month beginning after the date of the final conviction, including an annuity then in payment. The individual (or the person determined under section 8342(c) or section 8424(d) of that title, if applicable) is entitled to be paid so much of the individual's lump-sum credit as is attributable to that service, less any annuity previously paid on the basis of it, and without interest for any period after the date of the final conviction. The individual does not participate, while serving in such a position or capacity after the final conviction, in the retirement system under subchapter III of chapter 83 or chapter 84 of that title, and no retirement deduction is made from the individual's pay for that service. This paragraph is in addition to sections 8312, 8332(o), and 8411(l) of that title; service not taken into account under any of them is not taken into account a second time under this paragraph, and no amount is refunded twice. No certification, determination, or regulation by any officer is required for this paragraph to apply, and the Office of Personnel Management shall apply it to each annuity it computes or pays for a month after the date of the final conviction. For purposes of section 72 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, investment in the contract attributable to that service that remains unrecovered after the payment under this paragraph is allowed as a deduction as if the annuity had ceased by reason of the death of the annuitant on the date of the final conviction, and \u00a7 6-3(f) does not apply to that deduction.\n\n**(2) Survivors.** Paragraph (1) does not reduce an annuity payable to a survivor who is a spouse or child of the individual. Such an annuity, and any reduction in the individual's annuity to provide it, are computed as if paragraph (1) did not apply, the reduction being taken only to the extent of the annuity payable to the individual. This paragraph does not apply to a spouse or child who has been finally convicted of the felony, of aiding or abetting it, or of conspiring to commit it. No determination by any officer is required for this paragraph to apply. \"Child\" has the meaning given in section 8341 or section 8441 of title 5, United States Code, as the case may be.\n\n**(3) Investment gains during covered service.** In imposing sentence, the court shall order the individual to forfeit to the United States an amount equal to the investment gain on the individual's holdings during each period of covered service under this Act, counting only the part of a period on or after the date this Act takes effect for the individual under \u00a7 6-1, whatever the allocation of those holdings under \u00a7 2-1. The amount is a personal money judgment against the individual, determined by the court under Rule 32.2 of the Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure. Section 4-3(f) applies to the amount as it applies to a disgorgement, and \u00a7 4-3(h) applies to its payment, each holding being an asset giving rise to it. The tax attributable to income recognized on a disposition or distribution made to pay the amount is the liability of the individual alone, and no other person is liable for it under section 6013(d)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(4) Investment gain.** For purposes of paragraph (3):\n\n(A) \"Holdings\" means each asset of the individual reported, or required to be reported, under Section 1, including an asset in a tax-advantaged retirement account under \u00a7 2-2 and an asset held under an instrument under \u00a7 4-10, other than a residence, use property, and cash and deposits under \u00a7 3-5A. An asset held jointly, or through an entity or trust in which the individual holds a beneficial interest, is counted in proportion to the individual's interest in it.\n\n(B) The investment gain for a period is the sum, for every holding, of the income received on it and the change in its fair market value, measured from the latest of the beginning of the period, the date this Act takes effect for the individual under \u00a7 6-1, and the acquisition of the holding to the earlier of its disposition and the end of the period, the proceeds of a disposition being its value at disposition. The change in fair market value is reduced by amounts contributed to or invested in the holding during the period, including contributions to a retirement account under \u00a7 2-2, and is increased by amounts withdrawn from it; a partial disposition is a disposition of the part disposed of. Interest on a deposit is income of the deposit for this purpose. A loss on one holding is set off against a gain on another, and the investment gain for a period is not less than zero.\n\n(C) A period of covered service ends on the earlier of the end of that service and the date of the judgment of conviction.\n\n(D) Values and income are determined from the reports under \u00a7 1-3 and the statements and reports under Section 1, and, where those do not establish a value, as the court finds. For an asset described in \u00a7 1-1(a-1), the value on any date is its market price on that date. Income is determined from the reports under \u00a7 1-3 and, where those do not establish it, as the court finds.\n\n**(5) Severability.** Paragraphs (3) and (4) are severable from paragraphs (1) and (2). If paragraph (3) is held invalid, on its face or as applied to any person, paragraphs (1) and (2) continue to apply to that person and to every other, and Congress would have enacted them standing alone. An amount under paragraph (3) held excessive as applied to a person is reduced under \u00a7 4-11(e) and is not invalidated.\n\n**(6) Final conviction.** In this subsection, \"final conviction\" and \"finally convicted\" have the meaning given in section 8332(o)(7)(A) of title 5, United States Code.\n\n**(7) Notice.** Within 14 days after the entry of a judgment of conviction of a felony under this Act, and of any order disposing of an appeal or petition from it, the clerk of the court entering it shall transmit a certified copy to the Director of the Office of Personnel Management and to the Office. Within 14 days after a conviction becomes a final conviction, the Office shall transmit to the Director of the Office of Personnel Management, and to the head of each agency in which the individual then serves, a notice stating the date of the final conviction and each period of service described in \u00a7 0-3(a) of which the Office has a record. The head of each agency receiving such a notice shall cease the retirement deduction under paragraph (1) from the first pay period beginning after its receipt, and any deduction made after the date of the final conviction is refunded with the lump-sum credit. Paragraphs (1) and (2) apply from the date of the final conviction whether or not a transmission is made or received, and an annuity paid for a month after that date is an overpayment.\n\n**(8) Reversal.** If a final conviction is reversed, vacated, or set aside, paragraphs (1) and (2) cease to apply as of the date of the final conviction; each annuity payment withheld under paragraph (1) is paid, less any lump-sum credit paid under that paragraph, and credit for the service is restored without redeposit of the amount so offset. A transmission under paragraph (7) includes the order.\n\n**(b)** An individual convicted of a felony under this Act is barred from appointment to any position requiring Senate confirmation and from the civil service. This bar applies to future appointed service only. It does not bar election to, or the holding of, any office filled by election under the Constitution, the qualifications for which are fixed by the Constitution and may not be supplemented by statute. Accordingly, a convicted Member may not later be appointed to a covered appointed position, and is not barred by this Act from seeking or holding elected office."},{"anchor":"sec-4-8a","heading":"\u00a7 4-8A. Non-Monetary Consequences","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Purpose.** A monetary penalty is discounted by the person able to pay it, and the persons best able to construct a concealed holding are the persons least deterred by a fine. The consequences in this Section do not scale with wealth.\n\n**(a-1) Two tiers.** The consequences in subsections (b) through (d) follow from the objective fact that a holding required to be disclosed was not disclosed before it was identified by another, which is established by the filing record and the date recorded under \u00a7 0-3(k), and require no finding by any tribunal. The consequences in subsections (e) through (h) follow only from a finding of concealment by a court, or from conviction.\n\n**(b) Publication of the record, not of a characterization.** Where an asset appears in data reported by a custodian under \u00a7 1-3, or in any other record held by the Office, and does not appear in the covered person's filing under Section 1, the Office shall publish both records together in the database under \u00a7 1-5, identifying the asset, the source and date of each record, and the discrepancy between them. The Office shall make no statement that any person concealed anything, and shall characterize nothing. The publication is of two accurate records placed side by side.\n\n**(b-1) Publication of elections and recusals.** The Office shall publish each covered person's election under \u00a7 3-1(b) as to each non-eligible asset, and each recusal filed under \u00a7 3-1A. These are the covered person's own statements and are published as made.\n\n**(b-2) Response.** Before publishing under subsection (b), the Office shall give the covered person written notice of the discrepancy and 14 days to file a correction, a supplemental disclosure, or a statement explaining it, which the Office shall publish alongside the records. A correction filed within that period that resolves the discrepancy is published in place of it.\n\n**(c) Loss of tax deferral.** A covered person who concealed a holding is ineligible for deferral under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 1043 as to that holding and as to any asset divested at any time during the covered person's service, whether before or after the concealment is identified, and during any subsequent period of covered service. The divestiture that compliance would have made tax-deferred becomes fully taxable.\n\n**(c-2) Loss of the cure period.** Such a person is ineligible for the cure of good-faith error under \u00a7 1-1 and for the abatement of an obligation under \u00a7 6-2 for the remainder of covered service.\n\n**(d) Loss of safe harbor.** Such a person is ineligible to rely on an advisory opinion under \u00a7 4-1(h) as a defense with respect to any arrangement for the remainder of covered service, and is ineligible for managed compliance under \u00a7 1-7.\n\n**(e) Bar on future appointed service.** The bar under \u00a7 4-8(b) applies upon conviction, and, upon a sustained civil finding of knowing concealment, a covered person is barred for five years from appointment to any position requiring the advice and consent of the Senate and from the civil service. This consequence does not bar election to, or the holding of, any office filled by election under the Constitution.\n\n**(f) Forfeiture of the Fund interest.** Upon a sustained finding of knowing concealment, the covered person forfeits any interest in the People's Prosperity Fund.\n\n**(g) Facilitators.** A person or entity found liable under \u00a7 4-3(e) is, for a period set in the schedule to this Act, ineligible to receive a contract or grant from the United States, and shall be referred to any professional licensing authority having jurisdiction over the person.\n\n**(h) Trustees.** A trustee who fails to report a prohibited instruction as \u00a7 4-10 requires is liable for the value of the resulting benefit, is removed, and is permanently disqualified from the list of pre-qualified trustees.\n\n**(i) Not credited.** No consequence under this Section is credited against any monetary remedy.\n\n**(j) Reversible.** A consequence under subsections (c) through (c-2) attaches upon publication under subsection (b) and is reversed upon a judicial finding that the non-disclosure was not concealment. On reversal, any tax paid by reason of the loss of deferral is refunded with interest at the overpayment rate, eligibility for the cure period and for installment relief is restored, and the database entry is annotated with the finding, published with the same prominence as the record it corrects."},{"anchor":"sec-4-9","heading":"\u00a7 4-9. Prosecutorial and Procedural Provisions","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Bright-line tiers.** Conduct that turns on an elastic standard \u2014 including the attribution standards of \u00a7 3-2 and \u00a7 3-2A, the anti-evasion standard of \u00a7 3-3, and any assessment of fair market value \u2014 is enforced only civilly. Criminal liability attaches only to the discrete, provable acts enumerated in \u00a7 4-7(a).\n\n**(b) Provable record.** Each certification, valuation, and disclosure required under Section 1 shall be made under oath, dated, and retained in a form admissible in a civil or criminal proceeding.\n\n**(c) Venue.** An action or prosecution may be brought in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, or in the district in which the covered person resides or in which the conduct occurred.\n\n**(d) Limitations.** An action or prosecution must be commenced within six years after the later of the violation or its discovery. The period is tolled during any time the covered person cannot be prosecuted, does not expire before the end of the second year after the person leaves covered service, and is further tolled for any period during which the Attorney General has published a declination under \u00a7 4-7(d) as to that matter.\n\n**(d-1) Coordination with the assessment period.** Where the tax imposed by \u00a7 4-3A is not reported on a return, the period for its assessment does not begin, as under section 6501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code. Where it is reported, the period for its assessment is six years, conformed to subsection (d), so that the tax and the civil remedies of this Act do not expire on different dates.\n\n**(d-2) Parallel proceedings.** Where a criminal investigation or prosecution of the same conduct is pending, a court may stay a civil action under \u00a7 4-3 or \u00a7 4-5 on the motion of the United States or of the defendant. A stay under this subsection tolls the limitations period in subsection (d), does not suspend the tax under \u00a7 4-3A, and does not suspend the obligation to divest under \u00a7 6-2.\n\n**(e) Referral.** Upon a determination that a covered person knowingly filed a materially false report or knowingly failed to file a required report, the Office shall refer the matter to the Attorney General and publish the fact of the referral. Referral is mandatory upon a sustained finding.\n\n**(f) Cooperation.** The Attorney General may grant immunity or a charging or sentencing concession under existing law, and the Office may recommend a reduced internal consequence, for a person who provides material assistance against a principal. The Office does not grant immunity from prosecution.\n\n**(g) Use of filings.** The filing obligations of Section 1 are regulatory, apply uniformly, and require records of a kind customarily kept bearing public aspects. The compelled contents of a filing shall not be used as evidence against the filer in a criminal prosecution other than one described in \u00a7 4-7(a). A disclosure published under \u00a7 1-5 is a public record, and its publication does not taint an investigation. The Attorney General shall maintain a filter procedure walling investigating personnel from raw compelled filings, and a contemporaneous record of the independent source of each item of evidence. If a court holds that the filing obligation compels testimony within the privilege against self-incrimination and that the regulatory grounding of this subsection does not sustain it, the immunity is expanded as to that filer to full use and derivative-use immunity, and the obligation to file continues."},{"anchor":"sec-4-10","heading":"\u00a7 4-10. Compliance by Instrument","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Availability.** Any covered person may satisfy the holding requirement of \u00a7 3-1 by placing the person's assets, and the assets attributed to the person under \u00a7 3-2 and \u00a7 3-2A, under an irrevocable instrument meeting this Section, in lieu of divesting or converting them.\n\n**(b) Character of the obligation.** The instrument is a private-law obligation. Its duties arise from the covered person's own declaration and are enforceable according to the ordinary law of trusts, in a court of the United States, without the exercise of executive power by any person and without action by any officer of the United States.\n\n**(c) Required terms.** The instrument shall: be irrevocable as to its restrictions for the duration of covered service and for two years thereafter; vest legal title and sole investment discretion in an independent trustee drawn from a list of pre-qualified trustees published by the Office; prohibit any communication between the covered person and the trustee concerning the identity, holding, acquisition, or disposition of any asset; require the trustee to report to the Office quarterly and to publish the fact of compliance; require the covered person to certify compliance under oath at each reporting date under subsection (d-1); and provide that the trustee owes fiduciary duties enforceable as provided in subsection (e).\n\n**(c-1) No transfer of beneficial ownership; no gift.** The covered person remains the sole beneficial owner of the assets held under the instrument, and the remainder passes to the covered person or the covered person's estate. The instrument transfers no beneficial interest to any other person. Accordingly, the instrument is a grantor trust wholly owned by the covered person under subpart E of part I of subchapter J of chapter 1 of the Internal Revenue Code, its creation and termination are not a realization event, and no completed gift arises under section 2511 by reason of its execution, administration, or termination.\n\n**(d) Trustee's duty to report.** The trustee shall report immediately to the Office, and the Office shall publish, any instruction, communication, or attempted communication from the covered person or a family member that the instrument prohibits. This duty is not waivable and is not subject to any confidentiality term of the instrument. A trustee who fails to report is subject to \u00a7 4-8A(h).\n\n**(d-1) Sworn certification by the covered person.** Annually, the covered person shall certify under oath, in a writing filed with the Office and published, that the person has given no instruction and made no communication the instrument prohibits, and holds no asset outside the instrument other than as this Act permits. A certification under this subsection is a statement within the meaning of 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1001.\n\n**(e) Enforcement.** The instrument shall provide, and the covered person by executing it consents, that it is enforceable by the trustee, by any successor trustee, by a special fiduciary appointed by the court on the petition of any person, and by the Attorney General as a party to whose enforcement the covered person has consented. Consent to enforcement is a term of the instrument and is not withdrawable. On breach a court may order an accounting, impose a constructive trust on any gain, order disgorgement, remove and replace the trustee, and order specific performance. Liability is personal to the covered person and shall not be satisfied from public funds or by indemnification.\n\n**(f) Governing law.** The instrument is governed by the law of the State designated in it, and in the absence of designation by the law of the District of Columbia. A question arising under an instrument executed under this Section is a question arising under the laws of the United States for purposes of 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 1331.\n\n**(g) Publication.** The instrument, each trustee report, and the fact of any breach found are published in the database under \u00a7 1-5. The execution or non-execution of an instrument is published neutrally, and non-execution is not a finding, an allegation, or evidence of any impropriety.\n\n**(h) Effect.** Assets held under a compliant instrument are not held in violation of \u00a7 3-1, and \u00a7 4-3 and \u00a7 4-3A do not apply to them, as \u00a7 4-11(c) provides. Nothing in this Section relieves the covered person of any obligation under Section 1, and the covered person's disclosure obligations continue as to the existence of the instrument, its trustee, and its terms.\n\n**(i) Survival.** The obligations of the instrument survive the end of covered service, the death or resignation of the covered person, and the repeal or invalidation of any provision of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-4-11","heading":"\u00a7 4-11. Relation of Remedies","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) The tax and the sanctions do not reach the same person.** The tax under \u00a7 4-3A is imposed on a covered person who has elected under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3), disclosed, and recused. The remedies under \u00a7 4-3 and \u00a7 4-7 lie against a covered person who concealed an asset, took none of the courses required by \u00a7 3-1(b), retained an asset for which no election was available, or failed to file a recusal required by \u00a7 3-1A(b) or filed one that is false. No person is subject to both by reason of the same asset, and the question of cumulative punishment does not arise.\n\n**(b) Distinct wrongs.** Where a person is subject to more than one remedy, it is because more than one wrong was committed: concealment, failure to file a true recusal under \u00a7 3-1A(b), and breach of an instrument are separate acts. A remedy for one is not a remedy for another, and no credit runs between them.\n\n**(c) An instrument displaces the election.** A covered person whose assets are held under an instrument complying with \u00a7 4-10 has satisfied \u00a7 3-1 as to those assets. Neither \u00a7 4-3 nor \u00a7 4-3A applies to them, and the remedies of \u00a7 4-10 reach breach of the instrument alone.\n\n**(d) Proportionality determined as applied.** This Act states no ceiling on total monetary exposure. Where a person is subject to more than one remedy, the proportionality of the whole is determined as applied to that person under subsection (e).\n\n**(e) Constitutional maximum.** If any exaction or forfeiture under this Act, including a forfeiture under \u00a7 4-8(a), is held excessive as applied to a person under the Eighth Amendment, it is reduced to the greatest amount that may constitutionally be imposed on that person, and is not thereby invalidated as to that person or any other.\n\n**(f) Installments.** Payment in installments is available under \u00a7 4-3(h) and \u00a7 4-3A(j) in every case, without regard to whether the violation involved concealment.\n\n**(g) No integrated scheme.** This Section describes the relation of remedies. It does not create, describe, or presuppose an integrated enforcement scheme. Each remedy rests on its own authority, would have been enacted in the absence of every other, and operates whether or not any other is available or valid. No provision of this Section shall be cited as evidence that the provisions it mentions constitute a single design."},{"anchor":"sec-4-12","heading":"\u00a7 4-12. Defenses and Construction","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, exceptions, and limitations on liability available in an action or proceeding under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in this Act. The common law of the United States or of any State supplies none. **No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act**, and the absence of a provision addressing a defense recognized at common law is a determination by Congress that it is not available.\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A defense or limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) No channeling.** The receipt, screening, publication, or referral of a filing by the Office is not a remedy, is not a prerequisite to any action under this Act, and does not channel, preclude, or displace any action. **No statute establishing a remedial scheme shall be construed to displace a remedy provided by this Act unless it so provides by express reference to this Act.**\n\n**(d) Mootness.** The divestiture of an asset, the correction of a filing, the resignation of a covered person, or the cessation of a practice after the commencement of an action does not render the action moot as to any period during which the obligation was unmet. A person asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the conduct cannot reasonably be expected to recur.\n\n**(e) Pleading.** A complaint alleging that a required filing was not made, was incomplete, or was false states a claim. **No heightened pleading requirement applies.** Knowledge and intent may be alleged generally and proved by circumstantial evidence.\n\n**(f) Remedial construction.** This Act is remedial and shall be construed to give effect to its purpose of ensuring that the financial interests of covered persons are disclosed and that conflicts are resolved."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Contingent Effect","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 CONTINGENT UNIVERSAL ELECTION","body":"**(a)** If the application of \u00a7 3-1 to any class of covered person is held invalid as beyond the power of Congress to impose by mandate, the requirements of Sections 1 through 3 become available to each covered person in the affected class as a voluntary, irrevocable election, and the consequences under this Act attach to the election on identical terms.\n\n**(b)** A candidate for a covered elective office shall state on the statement of candidacy filed with the Federal Election Commission whether the candidate elects to be bound. The statement is published. This subsection requires disclosure of the candidate's intention and imposes no qualification for office.\n\n**(c)** An election under this Section is enforceable as a private-law obligation according to the ordinary law of trusts and contracts, and its obligations survive the end of the covered person's service.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 CONTINGENT UNIVERSAL ELECTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5a-0","heading":"\u00a7 5A-0. Definitions for This Section","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Protected individual.** For purposes of this Section, **\"protected individual\"** means \u2014\n\n**(1)** a Member of Congress, a Delegate, a Resident Commissioner, and a person who formerly held any of those offices;\n\n**(2)** an officer of either House of Congress, and an officer or employee of either House or of an office, agency, or instrumentality of the legislative branch of the United States, **without regard to the compensation threshold in \u00a7 0-3(a)**, and a person who formerly held such a position; and\n\n**(3)** a member of the household of a person described in paragraph (1) or (2).\n\n**(b) Household.** A **\"member of the household\"** of a protected individual is a person who resides with that individual, a spouse of that individual, a domestic partner of that individual as \u00a7 0-3(g) defines that term with that individual in the place of the covered person, a parent, sibling, child, or person standing in loco parentis to that individual, and a person to whom that individual stands in loco parentis.\n\n**(c) A threat arising from the performance of official duties.** A threat **arises from the performance of official duties** if the performance of those duties, or the holding of the office or position, **is a reason for the threat, whether or not the sole reason, the principal reason, or a reason the person making the threat states.** **No other causal standard applies to this Section**, and the omission of any other standard is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d) Concerning behavior.** **\"Concerning behavior\"** means communication or conduct indicating an unusual or inappropriate interest in a protected individual or in a place at which a protected individual works or resides \u2014 **including a threat, an approach, surveillance or the gathering of information about a person's residence or movements, a fixation on a person or on a matter, a grievance pursued beyond the means the law provides for pursuing it, and conduct indicating preparation for an attack.** A threat is one form of concerning behavior **and is not the paradigm of it**; concerning behavior may be present where no threat has been made, and the absence of a threat is not a reason to find that concerning behavior is absent. The enumeration is illustrative, and the omission of any conduct from it is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(e) Protection.** **\"Protection\"** includes a protective detail; residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance; protective intelligence and threat monitoring; and any other measure taken to protect a person from physical harm. The enumeration is illustrative and does not limit the measures an assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 may specify.\n\n**(f) The protective service, and the threat-assessment staff.** **\"The protective service\"** means the United States Capitol Police. **\"Threat-assessment staff\"** means the officers and employees of the protective service assigned to the assessment of threats and of concerning behavior, acting in that capacity. **The Capitol Police Board is not threat-assessment staff**, and no member of it, and no officer of either House, participates in an assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 or in a determination under it, **except that procurement of an assessment in the manner \u00a7 5A-2(a-1) prescribes is not participation.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-5a-1","heading":"\u00a7 5A-1. Who Determines That Protection Is Necessary","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Amendment.** **Subsections (a) and (b)** of section 1966 of title 2, United States Code, are amended to read as follows \u2014 and **no other subsection of that section is amended.** Subsection (c) (the authority, in the performance of protective duties under that section, to make arrests without warrant and to utilize equipment and property of the Capitol Police), subsection (d) (the offense of knowingly and willfully obstructing, resisting, or interfering with a member of the Capitol Police engaged in the protective functions that section authorizes), subsection (e) (the rule that the authority conferred is not in lieu of and does not supersede any authority, duty, or function imposed on any Federal department, agency, bureau, or other entity, or on the Metropolitan Police of the District of Columbia), and subsection (f) (the definition of \"United States\" as the several States, the District of Columbia, and the territories and possessions) **are carried forward unchanged, and nothing in this Act repeals, narrows, or qualifies any of them.**\n\n> **(a) Authority of the Capitol Police.** ~~Subject to the direction of the Capitol Police Board, the~~ **The** United States Capitol Police is authorized to protect, in any area of the United States, the person of any Member of Congress, officer of the Congress, as defined in section 4101(b) of this title, and any member of the immediate family of any such Member or officer, **and any protected individual within the meaning of section 5A-0(a) of the Congressional Integrity Act,** ~~if the Capitol Police Board determines such protection to be necessary~~ **where the threat-assessment staff of the United States Capitol Police determine, on an assessment performed under published criteria, that protection is warranted, and at the level that assessment specifies.**\n>\n> **(b) Detail of police.** ~~In carrying out its authority under this section, the Capitol Police Board, or its designee, is authorized, in accordance with regulations issued by the Board pursuant to this section, to detail, on a case-by-case basis, members of the United States Capitol Police to provide such protection as the Board may determine necessary under this section.~~ **Protection warranted by a determination under subsection (a) shall be furnished at the level that determination specifies, and members of the United States Capitol Police shall be detailed as it requires, in accordance with the criteria published under section 5A-1(b) of the Congressional Integrity Act. No further determination by the Capitol Police Board, by either House, by any committee, or by any officer of either House is a condition of the protection.**\n\n**(a-3) Three further amendments, because the Board's control does not sit only in section 1966.** Moving the determination out of section 1966 leaves two provisions under which the Capitol Police Board still controls whether protection is delivered away from the Capitol, and a third under which it controls whether anything this Section requires or permits the protective service to publish or furnish may be released. Each is amended, and each amendment is confined to this Section.\n\n**(1) Deployment outside the jurisdiction.** Section 1978(b) of title 2, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(4) Performing a threat assessment, a screen, or a case disposition required by section 5A-2, section 5A-4, or section 5A-6 of the Congressional Integrity Act, or providing a measure required by section 5A-3 or section 5A-5 of that Act.**\n\n**(2) Assistance from Executive agencies.** Section 1970(a)(4)(B) of title 2, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> (B) upon the written request of\u2014\n>\n> (i) the Capitol Police Board; ~~or~~\n>\n> (ii) in an emergency\u2014\n>\n> (I) the Sergeant at Arms and Doorkeeper of the Senate in any matter relating to the Senate;\n>\n> (II) the Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives in any matter relating to the House of Representatives; or\n>\n> (III) the Chief of the Capitol Police, if the Chief of the Capitol Police has determined that the provision of assistance is necessary to prevent the significant disruption of governmental function and public order within the United States Capitol Buildings and Grounds, as described in section 1961 of this title; ~~and~~ **or**\n>\n> **(iii) the Chief of the Capitol Police, for assistance in the performance of a duty under Section 5A of the Congressional Integrity Act; and**\n\n**(3) Release of security information.** Section 1979 of title 2, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **(f) Section 5A of the Congressional Integrity Act. Subsection (b) does not apply to a publication, or to the furnishing of information to any person, that Section 5A of the Congressional Integrity Act requires or permits. What may be withheld from a publication that Section requires is governed by section 5A-1(b-2) of that Act, and no determination of the Capitol Police Board is a condition of the publication or of the furnishing. Subsection (c) does not permit or preserve the furnishing of a part withheld under section 5A-1(b-2) of that Act except as paragraph (9) of that subsection permits.**\n\n**(b) The criteria are published; the determination is not.** The protective service shall adopt and publish the criteria by which an assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 is performed and by which a level of protection is specified, and each amendment to them, **withholding nothing except as subsection (b-2) provides.** **A determination applying those criteria to an identified person or place is not published**, and is effective upon being made. The protective service shall publish annually the number of determinations made and the levels of protection they specified, without identifying any person or place.\n\n**(b-1) Until criteria are adopted.** **The absence of criteria adopted under subsection (b) does not suspend this Section, \u00a7 5A-2, \u00a7 5A-3, or \u00a7 5A-4.** Until criteria are adopted and published, an assessment is performed on the threat-assessment standards the protective service applies in practice, which it shall reduce to writing and publish, withholding nothing except as subsection (b-2) provides, and an assessment so performed has every effect of one performed under adopted criteria.\n\n**(b-2) Withholding only what would jeopardize safety, and the withholding tested.**\n\n**(1) The standard.** The protective service **shall withhold** from a publication under subsection (b) or subsection (b-1) a specific part of the criteria **where, and only where, its publication could reasonably be expected to jeopardize the safety of a protected individual** \u2014 including by enabling a person to avoid detection by an assessment, a screen, or a case under \u00a7 5A-4, to keep conduct below what would bring a higher level of protection, or to defeat the protection an assessment specifies. **Each level of protection, and the kinds of measure it comprises, is always published.** That publication would show which persons or classes of persons receive protection, would expose the criteria or the protective service to criticism, or would invite litigation is never a ground for withholding, and does not defeat a withholding this paragraph otherwise requires.\n\n**(2) The withholding is published.** For each part withheld, the protective service shall publish, with the criteria or the amendment from which it is withheld, **that a part has been withheld, the part of the criteria to which it belongs, the subject it addresses, and the reason its publication could reasonably be expected to jeopardize safety, each stated as specifically as paragraph (1) permits.**\n\n**(3) Withholding permits nothing this Section forbids.** A part withheld is subject to \u00a7 5A-2(b-1A), \u00a7 5A-2(b-2), \u00a7 5A-4(c-1) and (c-2), and \u00a7 5A-6(b) as published criteria are, and its withholding is not a ground for applying any consideration those provisions forbid.\n\n**(4) Who holds it, and how it is kept.** The protective service shall furnish each part withheld to the threat-assessment staff, and to each person procured under \u00a7 5A-2(a-1) upon the procurement, and each of them applies it as the published criteria are applied. The protective service and each person procured shall implement and maintain reasonable security procedures and practices to protect a part withheld from unauthorized use, disclosure, access, destruction, or modification.\n\n**(5) Reliance on it is tested.** A finding under \u00a7 5A-2 or \u00a7 5A-6 that rests in any part on a part withheld shall so state, identifying it by the description published under paragraph (2). In an action under \u00a7 5A-2(f) or \u00a7 5A-6(c-1), **each part withheld on which a finding rests shall be furnished to the court and to counsel for the protected individual, under such protective order as the court enters.** The court determines de novo, and may examine the part in camera, whether its withholding meets paragraph (1) and whether paragraph (2) was complied with. Where paragraph (1) is not met, the court shall order the part published; where only paragraph (2) was not complied with, the court shall order the publication paragraph (2) requires, and not publication of the part.\n\n**(6) Open to challenge whether or not relied on.** **A protected individual may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel publication of a part withheld otherwise than as paragraph (1) permits, or of a statement paragraph (2) requires that has not been made**, and is entitled to costs and reasonable attorney's fees on prevailing. The court determines the question de novo, may examine the part in camera, shall order publication of a part whose withholding paragraph (1) does not permit, and shall order the publication paragraph (2) requires where it was not made. **A failure to comply with paragraph (2) alone is never a ground for ordering a part published.** **A protected individual denied publication this subsection requires is injured by the denial**: the criteria decide whether, and at what level, that individual is protected, and whether a reduction of that protection can be tested under \u00a7 5A-6(c-1). \u00a7 5A-2(f-2) applies to an action under this paragraph.\n\n**(7) A failed withholding costs the protected person nothing.** A failure to furnish a part withheld as paragraph (5) requires, or to comply with paragraph (1) or (2), operates against a finding only so far as the finding denies, lowers, delays, or ends protection, and to that extent the part is not part of the published criteria in that action and the finding did not apply the published criteria. **It never defeats a level of protection an assessment has specified, and never makes the rest of the criteria, or any assessment performed under them, other than published.**\n\n**(8) Criteria so published are published criteria.** Criteria published under subsection (b) or (b-1) with a part withheld under this subsection are criteria published, and criteria published in advance, for every purpose of this Section, including \u00a7\u00a7 5A-2 and 5A-6, and of section 1966 of title 2, United States Code, as amended by subsection (a), and an assessment performed under them is performed under published criteria.\n\n**(9) Congressional access to a part withheld.** In this paragraph, a **\"congressional recipient\"** is either House; any Member, Delegate, Resident Commissioner, officer, employee, office, or committee of either House; any joint committee of the two Houses; the Capitol Police Board; the Architect of the Capitol; and any person, other than an officer or employee of the protective service, acting on behalf of any of them; and a **\"receiving committee\"** is the Committee on House Administration of the House of Representatives, the Committee on Rules and Administration of the Senate, the successor of either, and a subcommittee of any of them that the committee designates for the purpose. Except for the furnishing required by paragraphs (4) and (5), the protective service **shall not furnish a part withheld under this subsection to a congressional recipient other than a receiving committee or a person receiving it on that committee's behalf.** A part withheld is furnished for purposes of this paragraph whether in whole, in part, or in substance, in writing or orally, and whether on request or in response to a subpoena or other process of either House or of any committee or joint committee, and no such subpoena or process reaches a part withheld except as this paragraph permits. **A part furnished to a receiving committee is available only to the members of that committee and to the persons who receive it on its behalf, none of whom shall furnish it to any other person; and no other person, except an officer or employee of the protective service, shall have access to it or attend any portion of a meeting or hearing at which its content is disclosed, notwithstanding any rule of either House affording access to the records of a committee or attendance at its meetings or hearings.** Each receiving committee, each person receiving a part withheld on its behalf, and any other congressional recipient that obtains a part withheld by any means shall comply with the security duty in paragraph (4), and a use, disclosure, or access this paragraph does not permit is unauthorized for purposes of that paragraph. The protective service shall keep a record of each furnishing to a receiving committee, stating its date, the committee, the part furnished, and each person who received it on the committee's behalf. **Section 1979(c) of title 2, United States Code, shall not be construed to permit or preserve the furnishing of a part withheld except as this paragraph permits**, and this paragraph permits no furnishing of a part withheld other than to a receiving committee, for purposes of section 1979(f) of that title or otherwise. Nothing in this paragraph limits the furnishing required by paragraph (5), including where the protected individual is a congressional recipient; and where a congressional recipient is the plaintiff in an action under \u00a7 5A-2(f), a part withheld contained in the record that subsection requires to be furnished is furnished as paragraph (5) provides, and this paragraph does not otherwise limit the furnishing of that record. This paragraph is enacted as an exercise of the rulemaking power of the Senate and the House of Representatives, respectively, and is deemed a part of the rules of each House, respectively, superseding other rules only to the extent inconsistent with it, with full recognition of the constitutional right of either House to change it, so far as relating to that House, at any time, in the same manner, and to the same extent as any other rule of that House. If this paragraph, or any part of it, is held not to operate as a rule of either House, it remains in force as a provision of law, and if it is held not to operate as a provision of law, it remains in force as a rule of each House.\n\n**(d) The Board keeps everything else.** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of the Capitol Police Board over the administration of the United States Capitol Police, over the security of the Capitol buildings and grounds, or over any function committed to it by law other than the determination the amendment made by subsection (a) moves and the control the amendments made by subsection (a-3) remove. Paragraph (9) of subsection (b-2) and \u00a7 5A-4(d) govern the furnishing of information to the Board and to each of its members notwithstanding this subsection."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-2","heading":"\u00a7 5A-2. Threat Assessment, and What Follows From It","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Assessment.** The protective service **shall perform a threat assessment as to a protected individual** \u2014 upon the request of that individual, **upon concerning behavior** relating to that individual reported to the protective service or through the channel under subsection (a-4), upon referral, or upon its own initiative \u2014 within the period set in the schedule to this Act; and shall perform, as to each protected individual described in \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1), at the interval set in the schedule to this Act, **a screen of the records the protective service already holds**, including threat reports, prior assessments, and reports received through that channel, **which becomes a full assessment as to any individual the screen identifies.** **As to a person receiving protection under subsection (b), the periodic duty is a full assessment and not a screen**, so that protection in effect is revisited rather than merely carried forward. Each assessment and each screen is performed under criteria published in advance.\n\n**(a-0) Assessment on request.** The periodic screen under subsection (a) does not narrow who is entitled to an assessment **on request** under that subsection, which every protected individual is.\n\n**(a-1) Where no assessment is performed.** Where an assessment has not been performed within the period required by subsection (a), the Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives or of the Senate, as applicable to the protected individual, **shall, on the request of the protected individual, procure its performance** under the published criteria by a person qualified to perform it, **and may procure it otherwise.**\n\n**(1) From a roster, with no choice among those on it.** A person is procured under this subsection only from a roster the protective service maintains and publishes. **Every person who meets the qualifications the protective service publishes for performing an assessment is enrolled on applying**, and remains enrolled while meeting them, and **no member of the Capitol Police Board and no officer of either House adds a name to the roster or removes one.** The person procured for each assessment is selected by a method the protective service publishes in advance, **which leaves no choice among the persons on the roster.** No member of the Capitol Police Board, and no officer or employee of either House, shall communicate with the person procured about the protected individual, the substance of the assessment, or its result, except to identify the protected individual and to receive the level and the measures the assessment specifies.\n\n**(2) Procurement is not participation, and the assessor is independent.** **Procurement in the manner paragraph (1) prescribes is not participation in an assessment for purposes of \u00a7 5A-0(f).** The Sergeant at Arms may not vary the published criteria, direct the content or the result of the assessment, or receive the record of the assessment, and receives only the level and the measures the assessment specifies. The person procured performs the assessment independently and, in performing it, **is bound by every provision of this Section that binds threat-assessment staff.**\n\n**(3) A procured assessment may raise a level and may not lower one.** An assessment performed under this subsection may establish a level of protection or raise one, and for that purpose has the same effect under this Section as one performed by the protective service. **It may not lower a level of protection, and it is not a subsequent assessment for purposes of \u00a7 5A-6(a).**\n\n**(a-2) Interim protection starts small, and reaches the whole class when the clock is missed.**\n\n**(1) From the request, for those now serving.** A person who then holds an office described in \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1), or who is then an officer of either House of Congress or an officer of an office, agency, or instrumentality of the legislative branch, and who requests an assessment under subsection (a), **shall receive, from the request until the assessment is performed, protection at level (2) of subsection (b-1)** \u2014 residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance \u2014 at each residence of that person. **Protection under this paragraph covers each member of the person's household at a residence they share with the person**, and no member of the household receives protection under this paragraph at any other residence.\n\n**(2) Where the clock is missed, every protected individual.** Where an assessment is required by subsection (a), on any ground, and none has been performed within the period that subsection requires, **the protected individual to whom the assessment relates shall receive protection at level (2) of subsection (b-1)** at each residence of that individual, from the expiration of that period until the assessment is performed, **whether or not paragraph (1) reaches that individual.** Where the individual did not request the assessment, the protective service shall notify the individual that protection under this paragraph is available, stating the level and the measures and not the content of any report.\n\n**(3) The higher levels wait for the assessment.** **No level above level (2) is available under this subsection**, and concerning behavior reported before an assessment is performed does not raise protection under it. Such a report is a ground for the assessment subsection (a) requires within its period, and for an assessment at any time under subsection (b-3).\n\n**(4) Common to both paragraphs.** Protection under this subsection may be declined, and a declination withdrawn, as subsection (c) provides. A request under subsection (a) shall state the office or position from the performance of whose duties the threat is said to arise. **That statement is not a showing and is not a condition of protection under this subsection**: a request that omits it is a request, its content is not tested before protection under this subsection is furnished, and, for purposes of this subsection, no inference is drawn from the statement, from its content, or from its omission. Whether a requester holds an office or position paragraph (1) describes is established from the records of the House, office, agency, or instrumentality concerned, and not from that statement. Protection under this subsection continues until an assessment performed under the published criteria determines the level required, and **the failure to perform an assessment is never a ground for withholding protection under this subsection.** **A request under subsection (a) gives rise to one entitlement under this subsection as to the assessment requested**: where paragraphs (1) and (2) both reach a person as to that assessment, the person receives protection at level (2) once, and not under each paragraph.\n\n**(a-3) Capacity is published, a shortfall is priced, and the floor moves with the caseload.** The protective service shall publish annually the number of assessments requested, the number performed within the period required by subsection (a), the number that produced protection under subsection (a-2), the number of reports received through the channel under subsection (a-4), and **the number of positions applied to the assessment function**, allocating the time of any officer or employee who performs more than one function or who performs other duties. **The number of positions so applied shall not be fewer than the floor set in the schedule to this Act, which is stated as a ratio of positions to reports received in the preceding year**. **Where the proportion of assessments performed within the period required by subsection (a) falls below the standard set in the schedule to this Act, the publication shall state the number of additional positions required to meet that standard and the amount necessary to fund them**, and that amount is appropriated by \u00a7 5A-8(b).\n\n**(a-4) The channel.** The protective service shall maintain, and publish, a channel by which a protected individual, an office of either House, a State or local law enforcement agency, or any other person may report **concerning behavior**. **The channel shall accept a report of conduct the reporting person cannot characterize**, and shall not require the reporting person to state that the conduct is a threat or to assess it in any way. **Concerning behavior reported to a State or local law enforcement agency and not transmitted to the protective service or reported through the channel does not trigger subsection (a)**. The protective service shall invite each State and local law enforcement agency to use the channel.\n\n**(b) Protection follows the assessment.** Where an assessment finds that a protected individual faces a threat arising from the performance of official duties, **that individual shall receive protection at the level the assessment specifies.** No further determination by any officer, by the Capitol Police Board, by either House, or by any committee is a condition of the protection.\n\n**(b-1) The levels, from least to greatest.** The levels of protection are \u2014\n\n**(1)** the measures \u00a7 5A-3 makes available without an assessment, together with protective-intelligence monitoring;\n\n**(2)** residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance;\n\n**(3)** presence at a residence, an office, or a place the person is to be, periodically or on a stated occasion;\n\n**(4)** a protective detail for a stated period or occasion; and\n\n**(5)** a continuous protective detail.\n\nAn assessment may specify a level in part, may combine measures from more than one level, and may specify a measure not enumerated where the risk found requires it.\n\n**(b-1A) A finding is not a prediction, and nothing here authorizes a score.** A finding under this Section is a judgment about **what protective measures the situation warrants**, and is **not a prediction that any person will act**. **Nothing in this Section authorizes the creation of a score, a rating, a profile, a predictive instrument, or any list, status, or designation that attaches to a person who is the subject of an assessment**, and no such score, profile, or status may be created, maintained, or relied upon in performing an assessment. An assessment rests on the conduct and circumstances found, stated in the record of the assessment.\n\n**(b-2) The lowest level that meets the risk, and nothing by reason of office.** An assessment shall specify **the lowest level that meets the risk it finds**, and shall state the facts on which that level rests. **Except as subsection (a-2) and \u00a7 5A-3 provide, no level of protection attaches to any person by reason of the office or position held, the chamber in which the person serves, the party of which the person is a member, the committee on which the person sits, any leadership position the person holds, the seniority of the person, or membership in any class.** A level is specified only on facts concerning the risk to the particular person, found by the threat-assessment staff.\n\n**(b-3) Escalation and de-escalation on facts, at any time.** An assessment may be performed at any time on information indicating that the risk to a person has changed, on the request of the person, on referral, or on the initiative of the protective service; and the level shall be raised or lowered to the lowest level that meets the risk then found. **A change in level requires a change in the facts and not merely the passage of time**, and \u00a7 5A-6 governs the reasons for which a level may not be lowered.\n\n**(b-4) Protection under this Section is protection under section 1966.** Protection furnished under subsection (b) or subsection (a-2), and a measure furnished under \u00a7 5A-3 or \u00a7 5A-5, are furnished under section 1966 of title 2, United States Code, for purposes of subsections (c), (d), and (e) of that section and of section 1970 of that title, **whether or not the person protected is within the class section 1966(a) describes, and whether or not the amendment made by \u00a7 5A-1(a) is in effect or is held valid.** A member of the United States Capitol Police furnishing protection under this Section accordingly holds the authority subsection (c) of that section confers, and the offense stated in subsection (d) of that section reaches obstruction of that member.\n\n**(c) It may be declined.** A person entitled to protection under subsection (b) may decline it, in writing, in whole or in part, at any time. A declination is not a finding, does not affect any later assessment, and may be withdrawn.\n\n**(d) Continuation.** Protection provided under subsection (b) continues while the finding stands, and may be discontinued or reduced **only** as \u00a7 5A-6 provides.\n\n**(e) Persons who have left office or employment.** A person who has ceased to hold an office or position described in \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1) or (a)(2) is eligible for assessment and protection under this Section where the threat assessed arises from duties performed in that office or position, on the same terms as a person then serving, except that such a person receives protection under subsection (a-2) only as paragraph (2) of that subsection provides. **Neither the passage of time since the person left, nor any fixed period, limits eligibility under this subsection**; the question is the threat found, and \u00a7 5A-6 governs when protection ends.\n\n**(f) Enforcement by the person entitled.** The duties under subsections (a), (a-1), (a-2), (b), and (d), under \u00a7 5A-3(a) and (a-1), under \u00a7 5A-5 and section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, as amended by it, under \u00a7 5A-6, under \u00a7 5A-7(c), (c-1), and (c-2), and under \u00a7 5A-1(b-2)(4) are **ministerial**, and a person entitled to an assessment, to protection, or to a measure or service under this Section **may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel it**, and is entitled to costs and reasonable attorney's fees on prevailing. The action is on the plaintiff's own entitlement, is not brought on behalf of the United States, assigns no claim of the United States, and **does not require the participation, consent, or non-opposition of the Attorney General or of any other officer.** The record of the assessment, and of any determination under \u00a7 5A-6, shall be furnished to the plaintiff, subject to such protective order as the court enters. **\u00a7 4-6 applies to an action under this subsection**, which requires proof of no legislative act.\n\n**(f-1) The injury.** This Section entitles a protected individual to an assessment within the period subsection (a) requires, to the protection an assessment specifies or subsection (a-2) requires, and to each measure and service a duty listed in subsection (f) requires. **A person denied any of them is injured by the denial, and the injury is concrete**: without the assessment the person cannot learn what risk the person faces or obtain the protection that risk warrants, and without the protection, measure, or service the person bears a risk of physical harm that this Section exists to reduce. **Protection under subsection (a-2) does not discharge the entitlement to an assessment**, and furnishing it is not a defense to an action to compel one.\n\n**(f-2) Defendants, consent to suit, and jurisdiction.** An action under subsection (f), \u00a7 5A-1(b-2)(6), \u00a7 5A-6(c-1), or \u00a7 5A-7(b) lies against the United States, and alternatively against the officer to whom the duty runs, in that officer's official capacity \u2014 the Chief of the Capitol Police for a duty of the protective service, the Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives or of the Senate, as applicable to the protected individual, for a duty of that officer, and the head of the Government agency concerned for a duty under \u00a7 5A-7(b) \u2014 or, while that office is vacant, the officer performing its functions. **The United States waives its sovereign immunity from each such action as to the relief that subsection provides, including costs and reasonable attorney's fees, and as to no other relief**, and no such action shall be dismissed, nor relief in it denied, on the ground that it is against the United States or against a member, entity, or officer of the legislative branch. The district courts of the United States have jurisdiction of each such action under section 1331 of title 28, United States Code, in addition to any jurisdiction under section 1361 of that title.\n\n**(f-3) The remedy follows the duty.** Where the court finds that a duty listed in subsection (f) was not performed as this Section requires, **it shall order its performance and shall set the time for it.** The loss of protection an assessment specifies or subsection (a-2) requires, for any period during the action, **is irreparable injury, and no further showing of it is required** for relief pending the action. If that rule is held to displace the equitable discretion of the court, it operates instead as a presumption of irreparable injury, rebuttable only by a showing that the protection is being furnished.\n\n**(g) Aggregate publication.** The protective service shall publish annually the number of assessments performed, the number performed under subsection (a-1), the number finding a threat, the number in which protection was provided, declined, discontinued, or reduced, and the number of discontinuations following a subsequent assessment under \u00a7 5A-6, without identifying any person or place.\n\n**(h) The Anti-Deficiency Act qualification is not carried forward.** 2 U.S.C. \u00a7 1966a conditions its duties on section 1341 of title 31, United States Code, and supplies no money with which to perform them. **The duties of this Section are not so conditioned**, and \u00a7 5A-8 supplies the appropriation. Section 1966a is amended by striking *\"(except section 1341 of title 31)\"*.\n\n**(h-1) Section 1966a is a floor and is not a limit.** Section 1966a requires a threat assessment and, if warranted, a protective detail for a former Speaker of the House of Representatives **for a period of not more than one year**, extendable after a further assessment and **only with that former Speaker's concurrence**. A former Speaker is a protected individual under \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1), and \u00a7 5A-2(e) reaches that person. **Neither the one-year period, nor the concurrence requirement, nor any other term of section 1966a is a limitation on this Section, on the duty under subsection (a), or on the entitlement under subsection (b)**, and the omission from this Section of any fixed period of protection is a determination of Congress and not a gap. Where section 1966a affords a former Speaker anything this Section does not require, it continues to apply."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-3","heading":"\u00a7 5A-3. Measures Available Without an Assessment","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Available on request, to anyone covered.** The following are available **on request, without a threat assessment, without a reported threat, and without any showing**, to every protected individual:\n\n**(1)** a **residential security survey** \u2014 an inspection of the person's residence and the publication to that person of what it finds and what it recommends;\n\n**(2)** **personal security training**, for the person and for members of the household; and\n\n**(3)** **suppression of covered information** under \u00a7 5A-7 and under the Personal Privacy Act, and enrollment in any mechanism either provides.\n\n**(a-1) Who provides them, in what form, and by when.** The **Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives or of the Senate, as applicable to the protected individual**, shall provide the measures in subsection (a), directly or by procurement, within the period set in the schedule to this Act after a request, and shall give each person making a request a written receipt stating the date it was received, from which that period runs. **A survey under subsection (a)(1) is satisfied in the first instance by a self-administered instrument the protective service publishes, together with a review of the completed instrument by a person qualified to conduct a security survey**; an **on-site inspection** shall be provided on the request of the person, or where that review identifies a condition warranting one. Training under subsection (a)(2) may be provided in group, recorded, or written form. **It shall be offered at the interval set in the schedule to this Act and not only on entry on duty**; each offer and each completion shall be recorded; and the proportion of persons offered the training who completed it shall be published annually, by chamber.\n\n**(a-3) Capacity is published, and a shortfall is priced.** The Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives and the Sergeant at Arms of the Senate shall each publish annually, in aggregate and without identifying any person or place, the number of requests received for each measure in subsection (a), stating separately for a survey under subsection (a)(1) the number of reviews of a completed instrument and the number of on-site inspections, whether requested or identified by a review as warranted; the number of each provided within the period subsection (a-1) requires; and the number of positions, and the number of surveys procured, applied to providing them, allocating the time of any officer or employee who performs more than one function or who performs other duties. **Where the proportion of requests for a measure provided within that period falls below the standard set in the schedule to this Act, the publication shall state the number of additional positions or the additional procurement required to meet that standard and the amount necessary to fund them**, and that amount is appropriated by \u00a7 5A-8(b). A publication stating that positions or procurement are required without stating their number and that amount does not satisfy this subsection. For purposes of this subsection, a survey under subsection (a)(1) is provided when the review of the completed instrument, and any on-site inspection subsection (a-1) requires, is complete. Each Sergeant at Arms shall furnish the Comptroller General annually a record of each request, identified by number and not by person or place, stating the date of the receipt under subsection (a-1), the measure requested, and the date it was provided, and the Comptroller General shall verify the counts this subsection requires against that record and publish the verification, identifying no person or place. Furnishing the record is not a report of a request for purposes of subsection (b). **The standard triggers only the publication and the statement this subsection requires. It does not qualify the period subsection (a-1) sets for any request, the duty to provide a measure within that period, or any remedy for a failure to do so.**\n\n**(b) A request is not a finding, and carries no consequence.** A request under subsection (a) is not a threat report, is not an assessment, and **is not evidence that any risk exists**. It shall not be recorded in, or reported to, any body other than the office performing the measure; and **no adverse inference, and no consequence of any kind, attaches to making one or to declining to make one.** In particular, a request is not reported to the Capitol Police Board, to either House, to any committee, or to any officer of either House.\n\n**(c) Not a substitute for an assessment.** A measure under this Section neither satisfies nor discharges any duty under \u00a7 5A-2, and providing one is not a ground for declining or deferring an assessment.\n\n**(e) The program reports what it learns.** There shall be published annually, in aggregate and without identifying any person or place: the number of surveys, trainings, and suppression enrollments provided under subsection (a); the proportion of recommendations from surveys under subsection (a)(1) that were subsequently implemented, and the cost of implementing them; and, for each of the measures in subsection (a), **the number of persons who received it who were thereafter the subject of an assessment finding a threat, and the number who were not.**\n\n**(f) Because the record does not exist.** No published evaluation establishes whether the measures in subsection (a) reduce threats or attacks against public officials, in either direction. Subsection (e) is drafted so that **this program generates that record rather than assuming it**, and the data shall be published in a form permitting a person outside the Government to analyze it."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-4","heading":"\u00a7 5A-4. Cases, and What Must Become of Them","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) The duty to open a case and to reach a disposition.** Upon a report of concerning behavior received through the channel under \u00a7 5A-2(a-4), or upon concerning behavior otherwise coming to its attention, the protective service **shall open a case where the behavior is conduct subsection (c-2) describes, assess it, and reach a disposition under subsection (b) within the period set in the schedule to this Act.** **A case is not disposed of by inaction**, and the expiration of that period without a recorded disposition is itself reportable under subsection (f). **Behavior that, judged on the report and on information the protective service lawfully holds, is not conduct subsection (c-2) describes opens no case.** For each report or other behavior that opens no case, the protective service shall record, within the period this subsection sets, the determination that it opens no case, with its date and the facts on which it rests, stating what was assessed and why it is not such conduct. A report that opens no case remains a report for purposes of \u00a7 5A-2, and subsection (d) governs the information in it, and any information obtained in making the determination, as it governs information in a case closed under subsection (b)(1), the retention period running from the earlier of the determination and the end of the period this subsection sets.\n\n**(b) The dispositions.** A case is disposed of by one of the following, which shall be recorded with its date and the facts on which it rests \u2014\n\n**(1)** **closure**, upon a finding that the conduct does not warrant further action;\n\n**(2)** **monitoring** for a stated period, subject to subsection (e);\n\n**(3)** **contact** with the person, by the protective service or by a State or local agency acting with it;\n\n**(4)** **referral** to a mental-health, social-service, or veterans' authority, on the terms that authority's law provides;\n\n**(5)** **referral** to a United States Attorney or to a State or local prosecutor; or\n\n**(6)** **referral** to a court for a proceeding a statute provides.\n\n**(b-1) What does not satisfy subsection (b).** A record stating only the category of disposition, or stating a conclusion without the facts on which it rests, **does not satisfy subsection (b)**, and the case is treated as not disposed of. A disposition under subsection (b)(1) shall state what was assessed and why it does not warrant further action.\n\n**(c) A case, and no apparatus.** This Section authorizes the opening of a case **upon a report or upon conduct that has come to the protective service's attention, and only as subsections (a) and (c-2) provide, and authorizes no program of monitoring, scanning, collection, or analysis directed at persons who are not the subject of a case.** **The omission from this Act of any such authority is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(c-1) What may not open a case, select a disposition, or make a referral.** No case may be opened, no disposition under subsection (b) selected, and no referral made, on the basis of a person's speech on a matter of public concern, association, political affiliation, religion, national origin, or petition to a government, **except upon conduct subsection (c-2) describes**; and the fact that a person's grievance concerns the Congress, a Member, a committee, or a legislative act is not itself concerning behavior. **Nor may a case be opened, a disposition selected, or a referral made, by reason of the party, the political position, or the constituency of the protected individual to whom the conduct relates.**\n\n**(c-2) A case acts on conduct, and not on what a grievance says.** A case may be opened, a disposition other than closure under subsection (b)(1) selected, and monitoring renewed under subsection (e), **only on communication or conduct that, beyond the content of any grievance, opinion, or criticism it expresses, indicates an interest in, a capability for, an intent toward, or preparation for physical harm to a protected individual or damage to a place at which a protected individual works or resides.** **The content of a grievance, opinion, or criticism, however persistent, repeated, or intemperate its expression, is not such conduct**, and neither is the number of times a person communicates about a matter. **A threat of such harm or damage is such conduct, whatever grievance, opinion, or criticism accompanies it.** Where the assessment of a case finds no such conduct, the case is closed under subsection (b)(1). **This subsection governs every case this Section authorizes, and does not narrow \u00a7 5A-0(d) as it applies to \u00a7 5A-2 or to a report through the channel under \u00a7 5A-2(a-4).**\n\n**(d) Closed use-list, and deletion.** Information in a case may be used **only** to assess and dispose of that case, to protect a person under \u00a7 5A-2, and to make a referral under subsection (b). It **may not** be disseminated for any other purpose, matched against any other database except as a disposition under subsection (b) requires, or retained beyond the period set in the schedule to this Act following disposition \u2014 **after which it is deleted and the deletion certified.** Where a case is closed under subsection (b)(1), the retention period is the shorter period the schedule sets. **Information in a case may not be furnished to the Capitol Police Board, to either House, to any committee, or to any officer or employee of either House**, except that the protected individual to whom the conduct relates shall be informed of any disposition affecting that individual's protection.\n\n**(e) Monitoring is bounded, is defined, and is not a status.** **\"Monitoring\" means the periodic review of information the protective service already lawfully holds and of any further report concerning the person. It does not authorize physical surveillance, the interception of any communication, the acquisition of any record that a statute makes available only on process, the acquisition of any record by purchase, or the use of any covert technique**, and none of those is authorized by this Section. A disposition of monitoring runs for a stated period not exceeding the period set in the schedule to this Act, rests on a written statement of the conduct subsection (c-2) describes that warrants it, and may be renewed **only on a new written statement of such conduct occurring during the period.** Monitoring creates no list, no designation, and no status, and \u00a7 5A-2(b-1A) applies to it.\n\n**(f) What the program publishes.** The protective service shall publish annually, in aggregate and without identifying any person: cases opened and the source of the report; reports and other behavior that opened no case under subsection (a), by source; dispositions by category under subsection (b); time to disposition, including cases exceeding the period under subsection (a); and **the number of cases in which a person who had been the subject of a case thereafter engaged in violence against a protected individual.**\n\n**(g) Being a subject is not a finding.** That a person is or was the subject of a case **is not a finding of wrongdoing**, and may not be used to deny or condition any right, benefit, license, employment, or security clearance.\n\n**(h) Used against a person only if disclosed to them.** Information in a case **may not be used against the person in any proceeding, or as a basis for any adverse action against the person, unless it is disclosed to that person** and the person is afforded an opportunity to respond. This subsection does not require notice to a person that a case exists or has existed.\n\n**(i) One design, stated in more than one Act.** The duties of this Section are the duties of Judiciary Integrity Act \u00a7 10A-4B and of the corresponding Section of the Executive Integrity Act. **Each Act states them separately because no Act of this suite depends on the enactment of another, and a difference in wording among them is not a difference in meaning.** Where the text of this Section and the text of a corresponding Section differ, each is construed to the same effect, and neither is evidence that the other means something else."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-5","heading":"\u00a7 5A-5. Residential Security Equipment","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\u00a7 5607. Member security: ~~funding for improvements~~ residential security improvements**\n>\n> **(a) In general.** The Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives ~~may use funds made available~~ **shall use the amounts appropriated by section 5A-8(b) of the Congressional Integrity Act, and any other funds made available,** for providing security for the residences of Members of the House ~~to make essential security improvements if the improvements are included in a category established and updated as necessary by the Sergeant at Arms and approved and regulated by the Committee on House Administration~~ **to provide residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance to each Member as to whom an assessment under section 5A-2 of that Act specifies protection at level (2) or above, and to each Member entitled to protection at that level under section 5A-2(a-2) of that Act.**\n>\n> **(b) Applicability.** ~~This section shall apply with respect to funds made available for fiscal year 2026 and each succeeding fiscal year.~~ **This section applies with respect to the amounts appropriated by section 5A-8(b) of that Act, which are appropriated permanently and indefinitely and are available without fiscal-year limitation, and with respect to funds made available for fiscal year 2026 and each succeeding fiscal year.**\n>\n> **(c) Who sets the standard.** **The measures to be provided under subsection (a), and the standard to which they are to be provided, are those the threat-assessment staff of the United States Capitol Police specify in the assessment. Neither the category of covered measures nor the standard is established, approved, varied, or regulated by the Sergeant at Arms, by the Committee on House Administration, by any other committee, or by any Member.**\n>\n> **(d) Not an amount.** **A Member's entitlement under this section is to the measures the assessment specifies and not to any amount of money, and no Member receives, directs, or is credited with any amount under this section.**\n\n**(b) The Senate.** The Sergeant at Arms of the Senate shall provide, to each Senator as to whom an assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 specifies protection at level (2) or above, and to each Senator entitled to protection at that level under \u00a7 5A-2(a-2), residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance, at the standard the assessment specifies, from the amounts appropriated by \u00a7 5A-8(b). **Subsections (c) and (d) of section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, as amended by subsection (a), apply to the Sergeant at Arms of the Senate under this subsection.**\n\n**(b-1) The Senate provision is free-standing.** **Nothing in subsection (b) limits the authority of the Senate to provide security for Senators by its own rules or resolutions**, and where a resolution of the Senate affords a Senator greater protection than subsection (b) requires, it continues to apply.\n\n**(b-2) The allowance is not displaced, and is not the entitlement.** Nothing in this Section reduces, conditions, or reallocates any amount made available to a Senator under section 4 of Senate Resolution 294 (96th Congress) or under any appropriation referencing it, and a Senator's use of such an amount is neither a finding under \u00a7 5A-2 nor a substitute for the entitlement subsection (b) creates. **The entitlement under subsection (b) is to the measures the assessment specifies, furnished at the expense of the United States under \u00a7 5A-8(b), and it is not satisfied by making an amount of money available to the Senator.**\n\n**(b-3) No deputization is required or implied.** **Nothing in this Section designates or deputizes any person, confers any law enforcement authority, or requires that a measure under \u00a7 5A-3 or a level of protection under \u00a7 5A-2(b-1)(1) through (3) be furnished by a person holding such authority.** Where an assessment specifies protection at level (4) or (5), it is furnished by the protective service or under an agreement made under \u00a7 5A-8(b-1), and this subsection is not a limitation on the personnel of the protective service acting in their own capacity.\n\n**(b-4) Every other protected individual has an obligor.** Residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance shall be provided, at the standard the assessment specifies and from the amounts appropriated by \u00a7 5A-8(b), to each protected individual not provided for by section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, as amended by subsection (a), or by subsection (b), as to whom an assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 specifies protection at level (2) or above, or who is entitled to protection at that level under \u00a7 5A-2(a-2) \u2014 in which case the standard is the one the criteria published under \u00a7 5A-1(b) state for level (2) \u2014 by \u2014\n\n**(1)** the Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives, for a person whose current office or position described in \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1) or (a)(2), or if none the most recent, is in or of the House of Representatives;\n\n**(2)** the Sergeant at Arms of the Senate, for a person whose current office or position described in \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1) or (a)(2), or if none the most recent, is in or of the Senate;\n\n**(3)** for a member of the household, the officer this subsection, section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, as amended by subsection (a), or subsection (b) names for any person described in \u00a7 5A-0(a)(1) or (a)(2) through whom the member is a protected individual, as the member elects, and until the member elects, the protective service, whether or not that person receives protection at any level; a change of officer transfers monitoring and maintenance to the officer elected and owes no second installation; and\n\n**(4)** the protective service, for every protected individual paragraphs (1) through (3) do not reach, including a person whose current or most recent position is only in an office, agency, or instrumentality of the legislative branch other than either House.\n\nFor purposes of paragraphs (1), (2), and (4), a position whose pay is disbursed by the Chief Administrative Officer of the House of Representatives is in or of the House of Representatives and not the Senate, a position whose pay is disbursed by the Secretary of the Senate is in or of the Senate and not the House of Representatives, and any other position in or of both Houses is reached by paragraph (4).\n\nSubsections (c) and (d) of section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, as amended by subsection (a), apply to each officer, and to the protective service, under this subsection, reading subsection (a) of that section as this subsection, a Member as the protected individual, the Sergeant at Arms as the officer or the protective service furnishing the measure, other than its threat-assessment staff acting under \u00a7 5A-2, and the measures specified in the assessment as including, for protection under \u00a7 5A-2(a-2), those the criteria published under \u00a7 5A-1(b) state for level (2). **No protected individual's entitlement to a measure at level (2) fails for want of an officer named to furnish it**; where none is named, the protective service furnishes it.\n\n**(b-5) The shared residence limits co-residents only.** Equipment furnished under this Section at a residence a protected individual shares with a member of the household serves each member residing there, and **no member of the household who resides there is owed a second installation at that residence**, except as an assessment as to that member specifies a measure or standard the installed equipment does not meet. Where more than one officer owes equipment at a residence, each owes it until it is installed, and \u00a7 5A-8(b-1) settles the cost. Equipment that serves a member under this subsection continues, or is furnished anew, for each member still entitled, notwithstanding its removal under \u00a7 5A-9(c-1) or the discontinuation or reduction of protection as to any other resident. That limit reaches only a residence the member shares. **A member of the household who resides elsewhere receives equipment at each residence of the member, not shared with that individual, at which an assessment as to that member specifies protection at level (2) or above, or at each such residence where \u00a7 5A-2(a-2)(2) entitles that member to it**, from the officer subsection (b-4)(3) names, and the shared-residence rule of \u00a7 5A-2(a-2)(1) governs only protection under that paragraph.\n\n**(d) Title, removal, and the person's own home.** Equipment installed under this Section **remains the property of the United States unless the protected individual requests in writing that title pass to that individual, as \u00a7 5A-9(c-1) provides**, and \u00a7 5A-9(c-1) governs its removal. **Installation requires the consent of the protected individual and of any other person whose consent the law of the place requires**, and neither the refusal of consent nor the removal of equipment is a finding of any kind or a ground for reducing any other level of protection. \u00a7 5A-9 governs the tax treatment in every case."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-6","heading":"\u00a7 5A-6. Withdrawal Only on a Finding That the Threat Has Abated","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) The rule.** Protection provided under \u00a7 5A-2(b) may be discontinued, reduced, varied, or delayed **only** upon a subsequent assessment, performed by threat-assessment staff under the published criteria, **finding on a stated record that the threat has abated or diminished**, and then only to the lowest level that meets the risk then found.\n\n**(b) Not for a reason relating to the person's conduct in office.** Protection shall not be discontinued, reduced, varied, or delayed by reason of a protected individual's vote, speech, debate, sponsorship of legislation, committee action, public statement, party affiliation, candidacy, or endorsement, by reason of any matter before or acted upon by that individual, **or by reason of any other consideration bearing on how that individual has exercised or may exercise the powers of the office or position.** The enumeration in this subsection is illustrative of the general prohibition its final clause states, and **the omission of any consideration from it is a determination of Congress and not a gap.** **An action taken for such a reason is void, and the protection continues as though the action had not been taken.**\n\n**(c) The finding goes to the person.** A finding under subsection (a) shall be in writing, shall state the facts on which it rests and the published criteria applied, and **shall be furnished to the protected individual not later than the period set in the schedule to this Act before the protection is discontinued or reduced.** A discontinuation or reduction effected without furnishing the finding is void. **A finding stating only that the threat has abated or diminished, reciting the published criteria without identifying the facts to which they were applied, or stating a conclusion without the facts on which it rests, does not satisfy subsection (a) or this subsection**, and a discontinuation or reduction resting on such a finding is void, and the protection continues as though it had not been made.\n\n**(c-1) And the person may contest it.** A protected individual furnished a finding under subsection (c), or entitled to one and not furnished it, **may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel its production and to obtain review of it**, and is entitled to costs and reasonable attorney's fees on prevailing. The court shall determine whether the finding was made by threat-assessment staff, whether it applied the published criteria, whether it states the facts on which it rests, and **whether the discontinuation or reduction was for a reason subsection (b) forbids.** **Whether the finding applied the published criteria, and whether it states the facts on which it rests, the court decides de novo on the record of the finding, without deference to the protective service however denominated**; a finding whose stated facts cannot support, under the published criteria, the level to which it discontinues or reduces protection did not apply them. **Whether the finding was made by threat-assessment staff, and whether the discontinuation or reduction was for a reason subsection (b) forbids, the court decides de novo on the record and on any further evidence a party offers**, and the record is not conclusive of either. The court does not otherwise substitute its own judgment of the level of risk for that of the threat-assessment staff, and does not itself specify a level; where the finding fails any question this subsection states, the discontinuation or reduction is void, and the protection continues as though it had not been made. **The court shall order the protection restored pending the determination, and shall set the time for it**, and \u00a7 5A-2(f-3) applies to the loss of protection during the action. \u00a7 5A-2(f-2) applies to an action under this subsection.\n\n**(c-2) The aggregate publication is separate.** The aggregate publication under \u00a7 5A-2(g) is a separate duty and is not discharged by compliance with this Section.\n\n**(d) Declination is not withdrawal.** A declination under \u00a7 5A-2(c) is the protected individual's own act and is not a discontinuation under this Section. Protection declined is restored on request, subject to a current assessment; protection under \u00a7 5A-2(a-2) that is declined is restored on request without one.\n\n**(e) Both directions.** This Section applies to the withdrawal of protection of a protected individual by any officer or body, and nothing in it authorizes the furnishing of protection that no assessment supports."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-7","heading":"\u00a7 5A-7. Suppression of Covered Information","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Extension of the removal duty.** Section 5933(1) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 (Pub. L. 117\u2013263, div. E, title LIX, subtitle D, 136 Stat. 3458, set out as a note preceding section 601 of title 28, United States Code) is amended by inserting, after the persons described in that paragraph, **\"and, for purposes of section 5934(a) and section 5934(b)(1) only, a protected individual within the meaning of section 5A-0(a) of the Congressional Integrity Act\"**.\n\n**(a-1) What that extension reaches, and what it does not.** The amendment made by subsection (a) applies to a protected individual \u2014\n\n**(1)** section 5934(a) of that Act, **\"Government Agencies\"** \u2014 the right to file written notice of status and to request that covered information be marked private (paragraph (1)), the duty of a Government agency not to post covered information publicly and to remove it within 72 hours of a written request (paragraph (2)), and the exceptions in paragraph (3) as \u00a7 5A-7(a-2) provides; and\n\n**(2)** section 5934(b)(1) of that Act, **\"Delegation of Authority\"** \u2014 under which the individual may make any notice or request under that section directly or through a designated agent, and which prescribes what the notice must contain, as \u00a7 5A-7(a-1A) provides.\n\n**It does not extend section 5934(b)(2), section 5934(c), section 5934(d), section 5934(e), or section 5934(f) of that Act**, and the omission of each is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(a-1A) What the notice must contain, and the cross-reference that does not travel with it.** For purposes of section 5934(b)(1) as applied to a protected individual, **the reference to redress and penalties provided in subsection (f) of that section is a reference to the remedy \u00a7 5A-7(b) of this Act provides**, and a notice stating that remedy satisfies the requirement. **No notice or request of a protected individual is insufficient, and no duty under section 5934(a)(2) fails to attach, by reason of the omission from it of a reference to a remedy that does not apply to that individual.**\n\n**(a-2) The exceptions travel with the prohibition.** For purposes of section 5934(a)(3) of that Act, **a reference to a Federal judge includes a protected individual.**\n\n**(b) Removal is ministerial.** The duty of a government agency under section 5934(a)(2) of that Act to remove covered information within 72 hours of a written request is a ministerial duty, to which no discretion attaches, **enforceable by mandamus at the suit of the protected individual or of any person the individual authorizes**, and a court shall award costs and reasonable attorney's fees to a protected individual who prevails. \u00a7 5A-2(f-2) applies to an action under this subsection.\n\n**(c) The commercial half.** Suppression of covered information held by a data broker or other private person is provided by the Personal Privacy Act, and \u00a7 5A-3(a)(3) entitles a protected individual to enrollment in every mechanism that Act provides. **Where the Personal Privacy Act is not in effect**, the protective service and the Sergeant at Arms of each House shall, on the request of a protected individual, make and pursue on that individual's behalf every request for removal of covered information held by a private person that any law affords.\n\n**(c-1) The same service as to a State or local agency, and it is not conditioned on anything.** On the request of a protected individual, the protective service and the Sergeant at Arms of each House shall make and pursue on that individual's behalf **every request for the removal, redaction, or restriction of covered information held by an agency of a State or of a unit of local government** that the law of that jurisdiction affords. **This duty operates whether or not the Personal Privacy Act is in effect**. The protective service shall report annually the number of requests made under subsections (c), (c-1), and (c-2), by class of recipient, and the number honored.\n\n**(c-2) The same service as to a Federal agency, where the removal duty does not reach it.** Where the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 is not in effect, or the extension made by subsection (a) is not in effect or is held invalid, the protective service and the Sergeant at Arms of each House shall, on the request of a protected individual, make and pursue on that individual's behalf **every request for the removal, redaction, or restriction of covered information held by an agency, office, or instrumentality of any branch of the Government of the United States** that any law affords or that a published procedure of that agency, office, or instrumentality affords. A request under this subsection is made under the law or procedure it invokes, and **imposes no obligation that law or procedure does not.**\n\n**(d) Cumulative.** This Section is in addition to every remedy available under that Act, under the Personal Privacy Act, and under any other law, and displaces none of them.\n\n**(f) The custodians this Section creates are bound.** The protective service and the Sergeant at Arms of each House shall implement and maintain reasonable security procedures and practices to protect information they collect, receive, or transmit under this Section from unauthorized use, disclosure, access, destruction, or modification, as section 5934(e)(2) of that Act requires of the judiciary's custodians. **Information collected under this Section may be used only to make and pursue a request under it**, and \u00a7 5A-4(d) governs information in a case."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-8","heading":"\u00a7 5A-8. Resources for the Security of Protected Individuals","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Authorization.** There are authorized to be appropriated, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary to carry out this Section, including the assessments under \u00a7 5A-2, the measures made available by \u00a7 5A-3, the cases required by \u00a7 5A-4, the protection required by \u00a7 5A-2(b), the protection required by \u00a7 5A-2(a-2), the residential security equipment required by \u00a7 5A-5, and the suppression required by \u00a7 5A-7. Amounts appropriated under this subsection are available until expended.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation, and to whom.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for **the assessments required by \u00a7 5A-2(a) and (a-1), the measures made available by \u00a7 5A-3(a), the opening, assessment, and disposition of cases, and the determinations, required by \u00a7 5A-4(a), the protection required by \u00a7 5A-2(b) and its continuation under \u00a7 5A-6, the protection required by \u00a7 5A-2(a-2), the residential security equipment required by \u00a7 5A-5, and the personnel who perform each of them, including the threat-assessment positions required by \u00a7 5A-2(a-3) and the positions and procurement stated under \u00a7 5A-3(a-3)** \u2014 **to the United States Capitol Police, to the Sergeant at Arms of the House of Representatives, and to the Sergeant at Arms of the Senate, in the amounts necessary for the duties this Section assigns to each.** Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse.\n\n**(b-1) Transfer.** Each recipient of amounts under subsection (b) may transfer to another recipient, and shall accept from another, amounts necessary to perform a duty this Section assigns, and may enter into an agreement with a State or local law enforcement agency for the performance of protection an assessment has specified, reimbursing that agency from amounts under subsection (b). **This subsection is the authority for the transfer and for the agreement, and no further appropriation or authorization is required for either.** **The authority this subsection confers is in addition to the authority under the final proviso of section 1966a of title 2, United States Code, to enter into memoranda of understanding with State and local law enforcement agencies: it is not limited to the person that section reaches, it extends to the performance of protection an assessment has specified anywhere in the United States, and it expressly includes reimbursement, the omission of which from section 1966a is not a determination that reimbursement is unavailable under this Section.** It is also in addition to, and not in lieu of, the appointment of special officers under section 1974 of that title, which reaches only the policing of the Capitol buildings and grounds in an emergency and is not an authority to furnish protection under this Section.\n\n**(b-2) Procurement where the service does not supply.** Where the protective service has not supplied protection an assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 has specified within the period set in the schedule to this Act, the Sergeant at Arms of the House or of the Senate, as applicable, may procure it from amounts under subsection (b), and the protective service shall transmit a statement identifying the protection not supplied, the extent to which it was not supplied, the reason, **and the personnel, funds, or authority that would be necessary to supply it.** A statement attributing non-supply to competing priorities without identifying what would be necessary does not satisfy this subsection. The statement is transmitted to the protected individual, to the Committees on Appropriations of each House, and to the Capitol Police Board; **it is not published**, and the number of instances and the aggregate duration of non-supply are published annually without identifying any person or place. **Procurement under this subsection does not relieve the protective service of the duty under \u00a7 5A-2(b).**\n\n**(c) Continuity during a lapse.** The assessments required by \u00a7 5A-2, the protection required by \u00a7 5A-2(b) and by \u00a7 5A-2(a-2), the measures required by \u00a7 5A-3(a), and the removal of covered information under \u00a7 5A-7(b) are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations. **The employment of personal services for those activities is authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code, and an obligation incurred for them before an appropriation is made is authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of that title**. The officers and employees necessary to perform them shall be designated as excepted from furlough, and each recipient under subsection (b) shall identify those positions in a plan for its operations during a lapse in appropriations, which this subsection requires, and publish the identification. The designation does not depend on the existence, content, or publication of the plan.\n\n**(c-1) \u00a7 6-5(c) does not reach this Section.** The duties of this Section are funded by subsection (b), except that the Comptroller General performs a duty this Section assigns to the Comptroller General from amounts otherwise available to the Comptroller General, which the second sentence of \u00a7 6-5(e) does not bar applying to it, and every duty of this Section is effective in every fiscal year without regard to \u00a7 6-5(c). **No duty of this Section is excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated**, and a defense that a duty was not effective for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce it.\n\n**(d) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Section are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to the United States Capitol Police, to either House, or to any office of the legislative branch. **No reduction in any other appropriation is authorized by reason of this Section.**"},{"anchor":"sec-5a-9","heading":"\u00a7 5A-9. Tax Treatment of Protection Provided","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) Not income.** Protection provided under this Section, **a measure furnished under \u00a7 5A-3 or \u00a7 5A-7, including a residential security survey, personal security training furnished to the individual or to a member of the individual's household, and the suppression of covered information and the making and pursuit of any request for its removal, redaction, or restriction,** and security equipment and its installation, monitoring, maintenance, and removal provided under \u00a7 5A-2 or \u00a7 5A-5, are **not gross income** to the protected individual or to any member of that individual's household, are not wages for purposes of chapter 21, 22, 23, or 24 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and are not a gift for purposes of chapter 12 of that Code.\n\n**(a-1) The measure, not the appropriation, is what subsection (a) reaches.** Subsection (a) applies to a measure it describes, and to residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, maintenance, and removal, furnished to a protected individual **at the expense of the United States** \u2014 whether from amounts appropriated by \u00a7 5A-8(b), from amounts made available under section 4 of Senate Resolution 294 (96th Congress) or under any appropriation referencing it, from amounts made available under section 5607 of title 2, United States Code, or from any other amounts appropriated for the security of Members, officers, or employees of the legislative branch. **Subsection (e) does not apply to a measure this subsection describes.**\n\n**(b) Without regard to the fringe-benefit conditions, and without a determination by any officer.** Subsection (a) applies **without regard to** whether a bona fide business-oriented security concern exists within the meaning of section 1.132-5(m) of title 26, Code of Federal Regulations, whether an overall security program has been established, and whether protection is provided on a 24-hour basis; and **without any determination by the Secretary of the Treasury, the Commissioner of Internal Revenue, or any other officer.** An assessment under \u00a7 5A-2 that protection is required is conclusive for purposes of this Section.\n\n**(c) Title to equipment.** Whether title to equipment installed under this Section remains in the United States or passes to the protected individual does not affect subsection (a), and no amount is includible in gross income upon the installation, use, retention, removal, or transfer of the equipment, **upon a discontinuation or reduction of protection under \u00a7 5A-6 while the equipment remains in place, upon its continued monitoring or maintenance after such a discontinuation or reduction,** or upon the person ceasing to hold the office or position.\n\n**(c-1) Basis, and the default as to title.** **No adjustment to the basis of any property under section 1016 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 arises by reason of equipment furnished at the expense of the United States under this Section**, and no deduction, credit, or allowance for depreciation is allowed for, or by reason of, an expenditure to the extent it was so furnished. Equipment installed under \u00a7 5A-5 **remains the property of the United States unless the protected individual requests in writing that title pass to that individual.** Where title has not passed, **the United States shall remove the equipment at its own expense on the request of the protected individual**, made at any time, including upon a discontinuation or reduction under \u00a7 5A-6 and upon a sale or exchange of the residence. **Neither a request under this subsection, nor a removal, nor the passage of title, is a finding of any kind or a ground for reducing any other level of protection.**\n\n**(d) Not a gift, a contribution, or a thing of value.** Protection provided under this Section is not a gift under \u00a7 3-5C or under 5 C.F.R. Part 2635 \u2014 which by \u00a7 2635.203(b)(7) excludes from the term \"gift\" *\"anything which is paid for by the Government or secured by the Government under Government contract\"* \u2014 is not a contribution or expenditure under the Federal Election Campaign Act of 1971, and is not a thing of value for purposes of any rule of either House. **The reference to Part 2635 recites that exclusion rather than construing the regulation**, and where the exclusion does not reach a measure furnished under this Section, this subsection supplies the treatment operatively and by its own force. **It is furnished by the United States on a finding of fact by its own protective service, and no person confers it.**\n\n**(d-1) Costs and fees.** An amount awarded as costs or attorney's fees under \u00a7 5A-1(b-2)(6), \u00a7 5A-2(f), \u00a7 5A-6(c-1), or \u00a7 5A-7(b), or under section 5934(f)(3) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 as applied to a protected individual, **is excluded from the gross income of the protected individual to the extent it is paid to counsel**, whether paid directly to counsel or to the individual. **This subsection states for this Section the rule Judiciary Integrity Act \u00a7 10A-9A(d) states for that one**, and the two are construed to the same effect.\n\n**(e) No inference.** Nothing in this Section determines the treatment of protection or security equipment provided to any person under any other authority, and the enumeration in subsection (b) of conditions disregarded is not a determination that any of them would otherwise apply."},{"anchor":"sec-5a-10","heading":"\u00a7 5A-10. Construction and Severability of This Section","part":"SECTION 5A \u2014 SECURITY OF MEMBERS, OFFICERS, AND EMPLOYEES","body":"**(a) No qualification, and no legislative act.** Nothing in this Section adds to or subtracts from the qualifications for service in either House, and no provision of it conditions, burdens, or is conditioned upon any legislative act. **\u00a7 4-6 applies to every action under this Section.**\n\n**(b) The rules of each House are preserved.** Nothing in this Section limits the authority of either House to determine the rules of its proceedings, to provide additional security for its Members, officers, and employees, or to discipline its Members. **This Section states a floor.** Where a rule or resolution of either House affords greater protection than this Section requires, it continues to apply.\n\n**(c) Floor, not ceiling.** This Section reduces no protection available under existing law, and where existing law affords greater protection it continues to apply.\n\n**(d) Not conditioned on another Act.** No provision of this Section depends for its operation or validity on the enactment or continued effect of the Executive Integrity Act, the Judiciary Integrity Act, the Personal Privacy Act, or the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022. **Where \u00a7 5A-3(a)(3) or \u00a7 5A-7 refers to a provision of another Act, and that Act is not in effect, \u00a7 5A-7(c) and (c-2) supply the duty.**\n\n**(e) Severability.** Each provision of this Section is severable from every other and from every other provision of this Act. Paragraph (9) of \u00a7 5A-1(b-2) is severable from paragraphs (1) through (8) of that subsection; if paragraph (9) is held invalid, those paragraphs remain in force. Each sentence of paragraph (9) of \u00a7 5A-1(b-2) is severable from the others; if any of them is held invalid, the others remain in force. **If \u00a7 5A-1 is held invalid, \u00a7\u00a7 5A-2 through 5A-9 remain in force**, the assessment-and-protection requirement operating without regard to the amendment to 2 U.S.C. \u00a7 1966, and **\u00a7 5A-2(b-4) supplying the authorities of subsections (c), (d), and (e) of that section, and of 2 U.S.C. \u00a7 1970, for protection furnished under this Section.** Each amendment made by \u00a7 5A-1(a-3) is severable from the others and from \u00a7 5A-1(a), and the invalidity of any of them leaves the duties of \u00a7\u00a7 5A-2 through 5A-9 in force. **The actions under \u00a7 5A-2(f), as to each duty that subsection lists, under \u00a7 5A-1(b-2)(6), under \u00a7 5A-6(c-1), and under \u00a7 5A-7(b) are each severable from the duties they enforce and from one another. If any of them is held invalid, or held not to present a case or controversy as to any plaintiff, defendant, or duty, the duties of \u00a7\u00a7 5A-2 through 5A-7 remain in force and ministerial, the publication duties of this Section continue, and \u00a7 5A-8(b-2) continues to operate.** **If \u00a7 5A-2(b) is held invalid, the assessment, channel, capacity, and publication requirements of \u00a7 5A-2 remain in force.** If the extension made by \u00a7 5A-7(a) is held invalid, \u00a7 5A-7(c-2) operates, and \u00a7 5A-7(c) operates as though the Personal Privacy Act were not in effect. If \u00a7 5A-5 is held invalid in whole or as to either House, \u00a7 5A-2(b) continues to require protection at level (2) and the appropriation in \u00a7 5A-8(b) continues to fund it.\n\n**(f) This Section is not part of a scheme.** \u00a7 6-4(a) applies to this Section. It is enacted because the protection of the persons who serve in the legislative branch presently depends on the determination of a board of officers and on an annual appropriation, and Congress would have enacted it standing alone.\n\n**(g) Onset.**\n\n**(1) On enactment.** **This Section, and each amendment made by it, takes effect on the date of enactment of this Act, and \u00a7 6-1(a) does not apply to it.**\n\n**(2) Requests from enactment.** Any request, report, referral, or notice this Section provides for may be made on and after the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(3) Compensation.** No provision of this Section varies the compensation of a Senator or Representative, and \u00a7 6-1(b) does not apply to it. If the application of any provision of this Section to a Senator or Representative is held to vary that person's compensation, that application alone takes effect as \u00a7 6-1(b) provides, and every other application of that provision, and every other provision of this Section, takes effect as paragraph (1) provides.\n\n**(4) The interim standards by a date.** The protective service shall publish the written standards \u00a7 5A-1(b-1) requires within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the date of enactment. **The absence of published standards does not toll or extend any period this Section sets**, and \u00a7 5A-2(a-1) and (a-2) apply to an assessment not performed within its period for that reason as for any other.\n\n**(h) No duty on a State, a unit of local government, or an Indian Tribe.** Nothing in \u00a7 5A-2(a-4), \u00a7 5A-4(b)(3), or \u00a7 5A-7(c-1) imposes a duty on a State, a unit of local government, or an Indian Tribe, or on any officer or employee of any of them. Such a government or its agency uses the channel under \u00a7 5A-2(a-4), or acts with the protective service in a contact under \u00a7 5A-4(b)(3), only by its own agreement; and a request under \u00a7 5A-7(c-1) is a request made under the law of the jurisdiction concerned and imposes no obligation that law does not."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Effective Dates","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** This Act takes effect for the Congress following the Congress in which it is enacted, except that a provision varying the compensation of Senators or Representatives takes effect as provided in subsection (b), which controls where the two differ.\n\n**(b)** No provision of this Act varying the compensation of Senators or Representatives, including \u00a7 2-3, takes effect until an election of Representatives has intervened.\n\n**(c) The Office exists, and is ready, by the date subsection (a) provides.** Notwithstanding subsection (a), this subsection, \u00a7 4-1(a) and (b), and \u00a7 6-5(a), (a-1), and (b) take effect on the date of enactment of this Act. Before the date subsection (a) provides, the Office shall have in operation the database required by \u00a7 1-5, the filing tools required by \u00a7 4-1(f), the means of receiving custodial reports under \u00a7 1-3(a) and of furnishing information to custodians under \u00a7 1-3(a-1), and the personnel to perform from that date the functions listed in \u00a7 6-5(b-2), and shall have published the plan required by \u00a7 6-5(h). The duty in the preceding sentence is effective only to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out; where they are insufficient, the Office shall publish a statement identifying each element of that duty not in operation and the amount required to put it in operation, signed and transmitted as \u00a7 6-5(c) provides for the Office's statement under that subsection. This subsection causes no other provision of this Act to take effect, and no other duty of this Act to bind any person, earlier than subsection (a) or that provision's own terms provide, and it delays no provision that takes effect earlier under its own terms.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-1a","heading":"\u00a7 6-1A. Amendment of Core Provisions","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Entrenchment.** An amendment to the disclosure requirements of Section 1, the eligible-asset categories or compensation peg of Section 2, or the officeholder rule of Section 3, requires a two-thirds vote of each House, a recorded roll-call vote, and an effective date no earlier than three years after enactment of the amendment. For purposes of this subsection, an amendment to the definition, methodology, or certification of the Middle-Class Growth Rate is an amendment to the compensation peg of Section 2.\n\n**(b) Basis and limits.** The vote and roll-call requirements of subsection (a) are enacted as an exercise of the rulemaking power of each House of Congress, operate as rules of the proceedings of each House, and may be changed by either House in the same manner and to the same extent as any other rule of its proceedings. A court that declines to treat this subsection as binding leaves the substantive provisions of Sections 1 through 3 in force as ordinary law, unaffected by that holding, unless and until repealed by whatever vote is otherwise sufficient. This subsection does not purport to bind any future Congress beyond what Article I permits."},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Divestiture","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** A covered person shall convert non-eligible assets that are readily marketable within 12 months of the applicable effective date or of assuming office, and non-eligible assets that are not readily marketable within 48 months, holding them during that period under an arrangement providing no participation in management and recusing from any matter affecting them.\n\n**(b)** An involuntary acquisition is disclosed within 30 days and converted within the periods in subsection (a), measured from receipt.\n\n**(c) Hardship.** The Office shall provide a hardship exception under published standards.\n\n**(d) Continuity.** A person who leaves and re-enters covered service within the divestiture period continues the period already running and does not begin a new one. **Covered service under this subsection includes covered service under the Congressional Integrity Act or the Executive Integrity Act, whichever is not this Act, and a period running under that Act continues under this one \u2014 a change of branch starts no clock over.**\n\n**(e) What divestiture requires.** To divest or convert an asset under this Act, the covered person shall transfer both legal title and the economic exposure of the asset to an unrelated person in a bona fide transaction. An arrangement that disposes of economic exposure while retaining legal title, or that retains economic exposure while transferring legal title, does not satisfy this Act. Without limiting the foregoing, none of the following is a divestiture: a transaction that would be treated as a constructive sale of an appreciated financial position under section 1259 of the Internal Revenue Code, or that would be so treated if the asset were an appreciated financial position, including a short sale, an offsetting notional principal contract, a futures or forward contract, and a prepaid variable forward contract; a collar, put, call, swap, or other derivative that reduces the covered person's risk of loss and opportunity for gain, taken together, by more than 80 percent of what they were before the transaction; a sale to a family member, to an entity in which the covered person or a family member holds an interest, or to a person acting at the covered person's direction; a transfer subject to an option, right of first refusal, or written or oral understanding permitting reacquisition; and a loan secured by the asset in a principal amount exceeding 80 percent of its fair market value at the time the loan is made.\n\n**(f) No exchange into another prohibited holding.** An asset divested or converted under this Act shall be exchanged only for an eligible asset. A transaction that defers recognition of gain by exchanging a prohibited asset for another prohibited asset, including an exchange under section 1031, a rollover under section 1045, and an investment under section 1400Z-2 of the Internal Revenue Code, does not satisfy this Act, and the deferral is not available for such a transaction by a covered person during covered service."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Tax Treatment","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Deferral.** A conversion required by this Act is eligible for deferral of capital gains under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 1043, as provided in \u00a7 6-3A. The Certificate of Divestiture procedure of 5 C.F.R. Part 2634, Subpart J is a procedure of the executive branch and does not apply to a covered person under this Act; \u00a7 6-3A supplies the certificate.\n\n**(b) No step-up on avoided divestiture.** Where a covered person fails to convert an asset as required and the asset passes at death, no step-up in basis under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 1014 applies to the gain that would have been recognized had the asset been converted when required. The Office shall preserve the record necessary to trace that amount.\n\n**(c) Installment relief.** Where conversion of an illiquid asset produces a tax liability disproportionate to available liquidity, the tax may be paid in installments on the terms provided for closely held business interests under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 6166.\n\n**(d) Retirement accounts.** A reallocation within a tax-advantaged retirement account under \u00a7 2-2 is not a realization event. Where compliance requires liquidation of a single illiquid asset held in a self-directed retirement account, the liquidation is not a prohibited transaction under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 4975 by reason of this Act.\n\n**(e) Tax-neutral restructuring.** A restructuring undertaken solely to comply with this Act is not a taxable event to the extent the covered person retains the beneficial interest.\n\n**(f) Nondeductibility.** A disgorgement, civil penalty, forfeiture, or tax under this Act, including under \u00a7 1-2, \u00a7 3-3B, \u00a7 3-3C, \u00a7 3-5B, \u00a7 4-3, \u00a7 4-3A, and \u00a7 4-8(a)(3), is not deductible for any tax purpose. No amount paid under this Act is treated as restitution, remediation, or an amount paid to come into compliance with law for purposes of section 162(f)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code, and no such amount is deductible under any other provision.\n\n**(g) Services of the Office.** The preparation and filing of a report, a valuation, the issuance of a certificate, and any other service the Office performs, or procures at its own expense, in carrying out this Act for a covered person, a former covered person, or a person whose assets are attributed to a covered person, are not gross income to any of them, are not wages for purposes of chapter 21, 22, 23, or 24 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and are not a gift for purposes of chapter 12 of that Code or of \u00a7 3-5C. Nothing in this subsection determines the treatment of a service provided to any person under any other authority."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3a","heading":"\u00a7 6-3A. Certificate of Divestiture and Deferral of Gain","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Treatment.** A sale or exchange of property by a covered person, made to satisfy \u00a7 3-1(b), \u00a7 3-2(f), \u00a7 6-2, or any other provision of this Act requiring an asset to be divested or converted, and made pursuant to a certificate issued under subsection (c), shall be treated as a sale of property pursuant to a certificate of divestiture for purposes of section 1043 of the Internal Revenue Code, and that section applies to the sale or exchange according to its terms.\n\n**(b) Eligibility supplied.** Subsection (a) applies without regard to whether the covered person is an eligible person within the meaning of section 1043(b)(1) of that Code, and applies to a Member of Congress, to the President, to the Vice President, and to a spouse, domestic partner, or dependent child whose asset is attributed to a covered person under \u00a7 3-2. The requirement of this Act that an asset be divested or converted is a conflict-of-interest requirement for purposes of section 1043. Subsection (a) applies without regard to the requirement of section 1043(b)(2)(B) of that Code as to the officer or body by which a certificate of divestiture is issued, and a certificate issued under subsection (c) is a certificate of divestiture within the meaning of section 1043(b)(2) of that Code.\n\n**(c) The certificate.** The Office shall issue a certificate upon the request of a covered person, identifying the property and the provision of this Act requiring its divestiture or conversion. A request shall identify the specific property. A certificate shall be issued before the sale or exchange, and the Office shall furnish each certificate it issues to the covered person and to the Secretary of the Treasury. The Office shall grant or deny a request within the period set in the schedule to this Act, and **a request neither granted nor denied within that period is granted by operation of law.** A request is denied only by a writing furnished to the covered person within that period stating that this Act does not require the divestiture or conversion of the property identified. A request granted by operation of law is a certificate issued under this subsection on the last day of that period, identifying the property the request identifies, and a sale or exchange after that day is made pursuant to it. Within 7 days after that day the Office shall furnish the covered person and the Secretary of the Treasury a written certificate so dated, identifying the property and the provision of this Act requiring its divestiture or conversion; a failure to do so does not affect subsection (a). The only question on a request is whether this Act requires the divestiture or conversion of the property identified. The Office exercises no other discretion and may not condition, delay, or decline a certificate on any other ground.\n\n**(c-1) If the Office's certificate cannot stand.** If the issuance of a certificate by the Office is held invalid on a ground relating to the appointment, removal, tenure, or placement of the Office or its Director, including a holding that the issuance is an exercise of authority that may be vested only in an officer of the United States appointed under Article II, Section 2, a sale or exchange of property that the covered person identifies, in a filing under Section 1 made before the sale or exchange, by specific description of the property and by the provision of this Act requiring its divestiture or conversion, is treated as made pursuant to a certificate issued under subsection (c), and that filing is the certificate for purposes of subsections (a) and (b). The filing does not establish that this Act required the divestiture or conversion, and subsection (a) applies only where it did. This subsection operates only upon such a holding and is severable from subsection (c).\n\n**(d) Permitted property is unchanged.** This Section does not enlarge the property in which gain may be reinvested. Reinvestment must be in permitted property within the meaning of section 1043(b)(3), and **an asset is not permitted property by reason of being an eligible asset under this Act.** A personal residence is not permitted property.\n\n**(e) No step-up on deferred gain.** Where gain is deferred under subsection (a) and the replacement property is held at the covered person's death, **no adjustment to basis under section 1014 of that Code applies to the amount of gain so deferred**, and that amount is income in respect of a decedent within the meaning of section 691 of that Code. The Office shall preserve the record necessary to identify the deferred amount and shall furnish it to the covered person, to the covered person's estate, and to the Secretary of the Treasury on request.\n\n**(f) Relation to \u00a7 6-3(b).** \u00a7 6-3(b) denies the adjustment to basis where a covered person **failed** to convert an asset as required. Subsection (e) denies it where the covered person **did** convert and deferred the gain. Neither limits the other.\n\n**(g) One certificate suffices.** Where a covered person is subject to the divestiture requirement of more than one Act of this suite, a certificate issued under any of them satisfies this Section, and no second certificate is required.\n\n**(h) No inference as to other taxpayers.** Nothing in this Section bears on the application of section 1043 of that Code to a person who is not a covered person under this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Severability","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and its application to other persons or circumstances are not affected. Each Section is severable from every other.\n\n**(a) No scheme of supervision.** This Act establishes no scheme of supervision of any branch by another. Each obligation is a discrete requirement of disclosure, holding, or conduct, stands independently of every other, and Congress would have enacted each standing alone. No provision shall be construed as part of a larger scheme by reason of its enactment alongside any other, and a holding that any provision is invalid shall not be extended to any other on the ground that the provisions together constitute a scheme.\n\n**(b) Office structure severed from substance.** The provisions governing the establishment, appointment, tenure, removal, and functions of the Office are severable from every substantive rule. If any such provision is held invalid, including on the ground that the Office exercises authority that may not be vested in a legislative-branch officer, the substantive disclosure, holding, conflict, and compensation provisions remain in full force, the duty to file continues, the duty to publish is enforceable by mandamus under \u00a7 4-1(d), and enforcement proceeds under \u00a7 4-3 and \u00a7 4-5 without the Office.\n\n**(c) Internal consequences severable.** Section \u00a7 4-2, providing for referral to the ethics committees and internal discipline, is severable from every financial and criminal consequence. If it is held invalid, \u00a7 4-3, \u00a7 4-5, and \u00a7 4-7 are unaffected, and the reverse.\n\n**(d) Enforcement channels severable.** The civil action under \u00a7 4-3, the action to compel disclosure under \u00a7 4-5, the whistleblower award under \u00a7 4-6B, the constitutional-claim provisions of \u00a7 4-6A, the disgorgement remedy of \u00a7 3-3A(e), the remedies of \u00a7 3-3B and \u00a7 3-3C, the criminal provisions of \u00a7 4-7, the tax under \u00a7 4-3A, the non-monetary consequences of \u00a7 4-8A, the instrument under \u00a7 4-10, the relation-of-remedies rules of \u00a7 4-11, the facilitator liability of \u00a7 4-3, and the self-executing rule of \u00a7 4-4 are each independently severable. In particular:\n\n**(1)** If the action to compel disclosure under \u00a7 4-5 is held not to present a case or controversy as to any plaintiff or claim, the alternative structure of \u00a7 4-5(k) applies to that plaintiff or claim. If that structure is also held invalid on the ground that a private plaintiff may not exercise the authority of the United States, the tax under \u00a7 4-3A, enforcement by the Attorney General under \u00a7 4-3, the instrument under \u00a7 4-10, and the non-monetary consequences of \u00a7 4-8A each continue to operate, and the substantive rules of this Act remain fully enforceable without any private action.\n\n**(2)** The action under \u00a7 4-5 and the alternative structure under \u00a7 4-5(k) are severable from one another, and neither is a condition of the other. Congress would have enacted each in the absence of the other.\n\n**(3)** The publication of a declination under \u00a7 4-5(i) is severable from every other provision of that Section and from every remedy of this Act.\n\n**(d-0) Tax severable; election preserved.** If the tax under \u00a7 4-3A is held not to be a tax, or to be a direct tax requiring apportionment, or is otherwise held invalid, the election under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) remains available on the conditions of disclosure and recusal alone, without payment, and every other provision of this Act continues in force.\n\n**(d-0-0) Rate severable from structure.** If any rate in the schedule is held excessive, or is held to convert the tax under \u00a7 4-3A into a penalty, the tax continues at the highest rate that may lawfully be imposed, and the escalating structure of \u00a7 4-3A(a-1) continues to operate at that rate and below.\n\n**(d-0-1) Denial of deferral severable.** The denial of deferral under \u00a7 4-3A(f-2) is severable from every other provision. If it is held invalid, the tax under \u00a7 4-3A(a), the deemed disposition under \u00a7 4-3A(f-3), and the election under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) each continue to operate.\n\n**(d-0-2) Deemed disposition severable.** The deemed disposition under \u00a7 4-3A(f-3) is severable from every other provision. If it is held invalid, including on the ground that it imposes a direct tax requiring apportionment or reaches appreciation without a realization event, the tax under \u00a7 4-3A(a), the denial of deferral under \u00a7 4-3A(f-2), and the election under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3) each continue to operate.\n\n**(d-0-3) Excessive Fines.** If any exaction or forfeiture, or combination of them, under this Act is held excessive as applied to a person under the Eighth Amendment, \u00a7 4-11(e) governs and the exaction or forfeiture is reduced rather than invalidated.\n\n**(d-1) Penalty measure severable.** The measure of the civil penalty is severable as provided in \u00a7 4-3, and an adverse holding on the primary measure substitutes the alternative measure rather than removing the penalty.\n\n**(d-2) Coordination severable in the direction of the covered person.** If any provision of \u00a7 4-11 is held invalid, each of the following that has not itself been held invalid continues to apply, and the remaining remedies of this Act are unaffected: (1) as to a covered person who has elected under \u00a7 3-1(b)(3), disclosed, and recused, the rule of \u00a7 4-11(a) that the tax and the sanctions do not reach the same person by reason of the same asset; and (2) as to every person, the constitutional maximum of \u00a7 4-11(e).\n\n**(e) Family applications severable.** Each restriction of this Act, to the extent it applies to a family member, is individually severable from the same restriction as applied to the covered person and from every other restriction as applied to a family member. Within \u00a7 3-2A, the classes are severable from one another, and if prohibition as to any class is held invalid the disclosure obligation applies to that class.\n\n**(f) Compensation severable.** The compensation provisions of \u00a7 2-3 are severable from every other provision. If they are held invalid, or if the People's Prosperity Act is not in effect, the disclosure, holding, and conflict provisions are unaffected.\n\n**(g) Mandate severable to election.** If the application of \u00a7 3-1 to any class is held invalid as beyond the power of Congress to impose by mandate, \u00a7 5-1 supplies a voluntary election for that class and the remainder of the Act is unaffected.\n\n**(h) Post-service severable.** The post-service compensation restriction of \u00a7 3-7(b) and the board-and-fee restriction of \u00a7 3-7(c) are severable from the post-service holding rule of \u00a7 3-7(a) and from the during-service restriction of \u00a7 3-7A."},{"anchor":"sec-6-5","heading":"\u00a7 6-5. Resources and Continuity of Operations","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Authorization.** There are authorized to be appropriated, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary to carry out this Act, including: the establishment and operation of the Office of Congressional Integrity; the database required by \u00a7 1-5 and the common schema; the free filing tools and automatic population of filings required by \u00a7 1-3 and \u00a7 4-1(f); the preservation of records required by \u00a7 4-1(e); valuation assistance and managed compliance under \u00a7 1-7; the pre-qualified trustee list and instrument form under \u00a7 4-10; and the advisory opinion function under \u00a7 4-1(h) and \u00a7 4-1A, including staff to prepare and publish opinions, maintain the shared advisory opinion library, process applicability requests, and meet the deadlines and staffing-adequacy requirements of \u00a7 4-1A(a), (d), and (j).\n\n**(a-1) The Office is an agency for this section.** In this section, \"agency\" includes the Office, and each duty this section places on an agency is, as to the Office, a duty of the Director. While the office of Director is vacant, or the Director is unable to act, each duty and signature this section assigns to the Director is that of the senior officer of the Office then serving, who may not delegate the signature.\n\n**(b) No-year availability.** Amounts appropriated under subsection (a) are available until expended.\n\n**(b-1) Permanent appropriation for the self-executing core.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for the receipt and preservation of the filings required by this Act and for any publication this Act makes a ministerial duty. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse. **The obligations \u00a7 4-4 declares self-executing, and the publication \u00a7 4-1(d) makes ministerial, do not depend on an annual appropriation.** Sections 1512 and 1513 of title 31, United States Code, do not apply to amounts appropriated by this subsection or by subsection (b-2), and no part of those amounts is apportioned, reserved, or withheld from obligation under them. The system of administrative control required by section 1514(a) of that title applies to those amounts and, for that purpose, restricts obligations and expenditures to the amounts necessary under each subsection in place of the amount of an apportionment.\n\n**(b-2) The permanent core reaches the functions a person waits on.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for **the issuance of opinions under \u00a7 4-1(h) and \u00a7 4-1A, the decision of applicability requests, the disposition of complaints, the issuance of certificates under \u00a7 6-3A, the preparation and filing of reports on behalf of a covered person where this Act provides for it, the furnishing of information to custodians under \u00a7 1-3(a-1), each duty under \u00a7 1-1(b-4), and the test, publication, and referral under \u00a7 1-3A**, and for **the personnel who perform them**, including opinion counsel, valuation staff and filing-assistance staff. The amounts necessary under this subsection are the amounts necessary to perform these functions within the periods this Act sets. **The number of positions applied to the issuance of opinions under \u00a7 4-1(h) and \u00a7 4-1A, the decision of applicability requests, and the issuance of certificates under \u00a7 6-3A shall not be fewer than the floor set in the schedule to this Act, stated as a ratio of positions to the requests for those functions received in the preceding year, and the number of positions applied to the disposition of complaints shall not be fewer than the floor set in the schedule to this Act for complaints, stated as a ratio of positions to the complaints received in the preceding year**. Where the proportion of those requests decided within the periods this Act sets, or the proportion of complaints disposed of within the period in \u00a7 4-1(j), falls below the standard the schedule to this Act sets for it, the publication under subsection (b-2A) shall state the number of additional positions required to meet that standard and the amount necessary to fund them, and that amount is among the amounts necessary under this subsection. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse. **Subsection (c) therefore does not reach these functions**, and the priority order in subsection (d) has nothing to rank as to them.\n\n**(b-2A) The draw is published.** The Office shall publish annually the amounts drawn under subsections (b-1) and (b-2), by function, and the full-time-equivalent positions charged to each function, allocating the time of any officer or employee who performs more than one function or who performs other duties. For each function in subsection (b-2) for which this Act sets a period, the publication shall also state the number of requests and complaints received in the preceding year, a complaint being decided when it is disposed of under \u00a7 4-1(j); the number decided within that period; the number not decided within it and, for those, the average time from receipt to decision or, where none has issued, to the end of the year; and the number of requests the Office treated as incomplete and, for those, the average time from receipt until the Office identified to the requester what was missing. For purposes of this subsection and \u00a7 4-1A(j), a request under \u00a7 4-1(h) or \u00a7 4-1A(d) is incomplete only as \u00a7 4-1A(a-1) provides and its period is measured from the date it is complete under that subsection, and every other request is counted from the date of receipt. As to \u00a7 1-1(b-4), the publication shall state the number of statements to which that subsection applied in the preceding year, the number reconciled within the period it sets, and, for those not, the average time from the filing of the statement to the completion of the reconciliation or, where none has been completed, to the end of the year.\n\n**(c) No obligation without appropriation.** **Except for the duties funded by subsections (b-1) and (b-2), and the duties \u00a7 4-1(e) and \u00a7 5A-8(c-1) except from this subsection, which are effective in every fiscal year without regard to this subsection**, no duty imposed by this Act on any agency is effective in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out. Where amounts are insufficient, the agency shall perform the duties in the order of priority in subsection (d), and shall publish a statement identifying each duty not performed, by the section of this Act that imposes it, and the amount required to perform it. A duty performed in part, or after a period this Act sets for it, is a duty not performed for purposes of the statement, to the extent of the part not performed or the delay, and a statement that does not state the amount required for each duty it identifies does not satisfy this subsection. The Office's statement is signed by the Director, who may not delegate the signature; its publication in the database under \u00a7 1-5 is a ministerial duty, enforceable as \u00a7 4-1(d) provides; and it is transmitted to the Committee on House Administration of the House of Representatives, to the Committee on Rules and Administration of the Senate, and to the subcommittee of the Committee on Appropriations of each House having jurisdiction over appropriations for the legislative branch. The statement of any other agency is transmitted to the Congress. No penalty, finding, or consequence attaches to an agency, officer, or employee for a duty not performed for want of appropriation.\n\n**(c-1) This section does not qualify \u00a7 4-4.** No obligation described in \u00a7 4-4, no duty funded by subsection (b-1) or (b-2), and no duty this Act makes ministerial, is excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated. **A defense that the duty was not effective, or was excused, for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce such an obligation or duty.**\n\n**(d) Order of priority.** Where amounts are insufficient to perform every duty to which subsection (c) applies, they shall be performed in the following order: first, the generation of draft filings under \u00a7 1-3(b); second, the free filing tools; and third, every remaining duty. This subsection ranks no duty funded by subsection (b-1) or (b-2), no other obligation described in \u00a7 4-4, and no duty this Act makes ministerial, and subsection (c-1) governs each of them.\n\n**(e) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Act are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to any agency for other purposes. No agency shall reduce, delay, or discontinue any function it performed before enactment in order to carry out this Act.\n\n**(f) Annual certification of adequacy.** Each agency with duties under this Act shall certify annually whether amounts appropriated under subsection (a) are sufficient to perform the duties to which subsection (c) applies, and shall state, where they are not, the amount required and the consequence of the shortfall. As to the functions in subsection (b-2), the certification states whether the amounts drawn under that subsection sufficed to perform them within the periods this Act sets and, where they did not, the positions and the amount that would have been required; the publication under subsection (b-2A), including any statement of additional positions under subsection (b-2), accompanies it. The certification is signed, published, and transmitted as subsection (c) provides for the statement under that subsection.\n\n**(g) Continuity during a lapse in appropriations.** The following are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations: the receipt of filings under Section 1; the receipt of custodial reports under \u00a7 1-3; the furnishing of information to custodians under \u00a7 1-3(a-1); the preservation of records under \u00a7 4-1(e); the automatic population and transmission of draft filings; publication in the database under \u00a7 1-5; and the functions in subsection (b-2). An activity so listed that is financed by subsection (b-1) or (b-2) continues because that appropriation does not lapse. As to an activity so listed that is not so financed, **the employment of personal services for it is authorized by law for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code, and an obligation incurred for it before an appropriation is made is authorized by law for purposes of section 1341(a)(1)(B) of that title**. The personnel who perform the activities in this subsection are within subsection (h).\n\n**(h) Personnel during a lapse.** The Office, acting through the Director, shall maintain staffing sufficient to perform the activities in subsection (g) without interruption during a lapse in appropriations. An officer or employee of the Office whose compensation is financed by subsection (b-1) or (b-2) is not furloughed by reason of a lapse, because that appropriation does not lapse. Every other officer or employee of the Office performing an activity in subsection (g) is an excepted employee for purposes of section 1341(c) of title 31, United States Code, without regard to any definition of the Office of Personnel Management. The Director shall identify the positions described in this subsection in a plan for the Office's operations during a lapse in appropriations, which this subsection requires, shall publish the plan before the date \u00a7 6-1(a) provides and within 30 days of any change, and shall notify each officer and employee in writing of the status this subsection confers on that person. The status this subsection confers does not depend on the existence, content, or publication of the plan.\n\n**(i) Deadlines during a lapse.** **(1)** A deadline imposed by this Act on a covered person, a filer, or any other private party to make a filing with an agency is tolled for the duration of a lapse in appropriations affecting that agency, and for 14 days thereafter. The Office is not affected by a lapse, for purposes of this paragraph, as to a filing whose receipt continues under subsection (b-1) or (g). **(2)** This subsection tolls no obligation described in \u00a7 4-4, and \u00a7 4-4(b) and subsection (c-1) govern each of them. **(3)** This subsection does not toll, extend, or suspend any period or date prescribed by or under the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, or by or under any provision of this Act relating to a tax, whether or not executed as an amendment to that Code, for filing a return, reporting or paying a tax, including the tax imposed by \u00a7 4-3A, making an election, including an election under section 1043 of that Code, purchasing permitted property under section 1043(a) of that Code, or assessing or collecting a tax. Each such period runs for a covered person as it runs for any other taxpayer. **(4)** No other deadline imposed by this Act on a private party is tolled by reason of a lapse. **(5)** A deadline imposed on an agency is not tolled as to the activities in subsection (g)."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Senior congressional staff compensation threshold** (\u00a7 0-3(a)): [$___]\n2. **Reporting threshold, assets with a readily ascertainable market price** (\u00a7 1-1(a-1)): [$5,000, indexed]\n3. **Reporting and appraisal threshold, assets without a readily ascertainable market price** (\u00a7 1-1(a-2)): [$100,000, indexed]\n4. **Net-worth reconciliation materiality threshold** (\u00a7 1-1(c)): [greater of $___ or ___% of disclosed household net worth]\n5. **Real-time transaction reporting threshold** (\u00a7 1-4A(a)): [$___]\n6. **Mandatory minimum civil penalty per violation** (\u00a7 4-3(b)): [$___]\n7. **Office of Congressional Integrity annual funding floor** (\u00a7 4-1(k)): [$___]\n\n8. **Annual tax on a retained asset** (\u00a7 4-3A(a), (a-1)): [**Year 1: 2.5%. Year 2: 5%. Year 3: 7.5%. Year 4 and each year thereafter: 10%**, in each case of the fair market value of the asset as of the valuation date.]\n9. **Civil penalty, percentage of concealed holding \u2014 custodied assets** (\u00a7 4-3(b)): [5% of fair market value for the first year of concealment, increasing by 5 percentage points per year, to a maximum of 25%]\n10. **Civil penalty, percentage of concealed holding \u2014 assets with no reporting custodian** (\u00a7 4-3(b-3)): [10% for the first year, increasing by 10 percentage points per year, to a maximum of 50%]\n11. **Alternative measure daily accrual** (\u00a7 4-3(b-2)): [$500 per day]\n12. **Response period before publication of a discrepancy** (\u00a7 4-8A(b-2)): [14 days]\n13. **Facilitator debarment period** (\u00a7 4-8A(g)): [5 years]\n14. **Pre-qualified trustee list and instrument form** (\u00a7 4-10(c)): [to be drafted and attached]\n15. **Substantial-understatement threshold and rate for valuation of a retained asset** (\u00a7 4-3A(f)): [25% understatement; 20% penalty]\n16. **Period for the Office to grant or deny a certificate of divestiture** (\u00a7 6-3A(c)): [30 days]\n17. **Materiality threshold for the initial true-salary phase-in** (\u00a7 2-3(b-1)): [10% of the pre-Act salary]\n18. **Number of intervening elections over which the initial true-salary adjustment is phased in** (\u00a7 2-3(b-1)): [2 elections]\n19. **Whistleblower award percentage** (\u00a7 4-6B): [___% of the amount actually recovered]\n20. **Period to file and publish a constitutional claim** (\u00a7 4-6A(c)(4)): [___ days after performance was due]\n21. **Period before a constitutional claim lapses** (\u00a7 4-6A(d)): [___ days after the claim is made]\n22. **Period for resolution of a material discrepancy before referral** (\u00a7 1-3A(d)): [___ days]\n\n23. **Period to perform a threat assessment after a request, a report of concerning behavior, or a referral** (\u00a7 5A-2(a)): [___ days]\n24. **Interval of the periodic screen of records held** (\u00a7 5A-2(a)): [annually]\n25. **Positions floor for the assessment function, stated as a ratio** (\u00a7 5A-2(a-3)): [___ threat-assessment positions per ___ reports received through the channel in the preceding year]\n26. **Timeliness standard for assessments** (\u00a7 5A-2(a-3)): [___% of assessments performed within the period in item 23]\n27. **Period to provide a measure available without an assessment** (\u00a7 5A-3(a-1)): [___ days after a request]\n28. **Interval at which personal security training is offered** (\u00a7 5A-3(a-1)): [___ months]\n29. **Period to open, assess, and dispose of a case, or to determine that a report or other behavior opens none** (\u00a7 5A-4(a)): [___ days]\n30. **Retention period for information in a case following disposition** (\u00a7 5A-4(d)): [___ years following disposition; ___ following a closure under \u00a7 5A-4(b)(1) or a determination under \u00a7 5A-4(a) that no case opens]\n31. **Maximum period of a disposition of monitoring** (\u00a7 5A-4(e)): [___ days]\n32. **Period before a discontinuation or reduction takes effect, within which the finding is furnished to the protected individual** (\u00a7 5A-6(c)): [___ days]\n33. **Period within which the protective service must supply protection an assessment has specified, before the Sergeant at Arms may procure it** (\u00a7 5A-8(b-2)): [___ days]\n34. **Positions floor for the opinion, applicability, and certificate functions, stated as a ratio** (\u00a7 6-5(b-2)): [___ positions per ___ requests under \u00a7 4-1(h), \u00a7 4-1A(d), and \u00a7 6-3A received in the preceding year]\n35. **Timeliness standard for the opinion, applicability, and certificate functions** (\u00a7 6-5(b-2)): [___% of requests decided within the periods in \u00a7 4-1A(a) and (d) and \u00a7 6-3A(c)]\n36. **Period within which the Office identifies what is missing from a request** (\u00a7 4-1A(a-1)): [___ days after receipt, and after each supply of a further fact or document]\n37. **Timeliness standard for the measures available without an assessment** (\u00a7 5A-3(a-3)): [___% of requests for each measure provided within the period in item 27]\n38. **Period within which the Office completes the net-worth reconciliation and gives any notice** (\u00a7 1-1(b-4)): [___ days after the statement is filed]\n39. **Series for the index return on disclosed assets, by class of asset** (\u00a7 1-1(b-4)): [___]\n40. **Period within which the Office determines whether a complaint states probable cause, or makes the transmission \u00a7 4-1(j-1) requires** (\u00a7 4-1(g)): [___ days after the complaint is filed]\n41. **Positions floor for the disposition of complaints, stated as a ratio** (\u00a7 6-5(b-2)): [___ positions per ___ complaints received in the preceding year, counted as \u00a7 4-1(f-1) provides]\n42. **Timeliness standard for the disposition of complaints** (\u00a7 6-5(b-2)): [___% of complaints disposed of within the period in \u00a7 4-1(j)]\n43. **Period to publish the interim assessment standards** (\u00a7 5A-1(b-1), \u00a7 5A-10(g)(4)): [___ days after the date of enactment]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-15"}}}};
